1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Abdullah ibn Muhammad (عبد الله بن محمد), (January 11, 844 - October 15, 912) of the Umayyad dynasty, was the seventh Emir of Córdoba, reigning from 888 to 912 in the Al-Andalus (Moorish Iberia).
2 CONT
2 CONT [source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdallah_ibn_Muhammad]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Umawi From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The Umayyad dynasty came to power forty-five years after Muslim forces swept through Iberia, led by Abd ar-Rahman I. The Umayyad were already a very distinguished family, a branch of which once lead to the Islamic Caliphate. Also,
2 CONC  the family could proudly claim descent from the Quraysh, the tribe of the Islamic prophet Muhammad himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rise to power
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporary historians were quick to accuse ibn Muhammad of orchestrating the death of his elder brother, al-Mundhir, whereby he ascended to power. This is unlikely, as ibn Muhammad showed very little interest in governing, becom
2 CONC ing a neurotic recluse who was only interested in hunting and his faith.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rule
2 CONT
2 CONT Once in power, however, he showed no reluctance to dispose of those he viewed as a threat, even if they were family. Two of his own brothers were executed on his orders, and he commanded one of his sons (al-Mutarrif) to kill his o
2 CONC wn brother. Even this extreme display of loyalty was not enough to save al-Mutarrif, as he too was executed for treason a few years later. The apathy ibn Muhammad showed towards ruling had disastrous consequences for his emirate. 
2 CONC By the time he died, he was ruler in name only, as local warlords had seized control throughout his kingdom. For instance, the second largest city under his rule, Seville, fell under the control of Ibrahim ben al-Hajjaj. He was a 
2 CONC strong patron of literature
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Muhammad I ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, amir al-Qurtubi1,2 b. circa 826, d. 886 Mother	Ishar (?)1,2 b. circa 836
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 2 Oneca Fortunez de Navarra b. circa 847 Child	 Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allâh al-Qurtubi+ b. c 8761
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdullah_of_Córdoba
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdullah was the son of Muhammad I and the younger brother of al-Mundhir.
2 CONT Around 863, Abdullah married Onneca Fortúnez, daughter of Fortún Garcés,[4] King of Pamplona and his wife Aurea (Orea). She was repudiated sometime before 880 and returned to the Kingdom of Pamplona, most probably with her father 
2 CONC who returned that year,[2] and took her cousin Aznar Sánchez of Larraun as her second husband with whom she had at least three children, including Queen Toda of Navarre who was, therefore, the aunt of Abd al-Rahman III.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdullah had several children:
2 CONT * Muhammed ibn Abd Allah (864 – 28 Jan 891). Recorded to be a son of Onneca.[5][6][7] He was murdered by his brother al-Mutarrif (with the approval of their father).[8] He married a Basque or Frankish woman named Muzna. They were 
2 CONC the parents of Abd al-Rahman III[5] who was born three weeks after his father's death.[8]
2 CONT * al-Mutarrif, murdered in 895 after being accused of conspiracy.[8]
2 CONT * Aban
2 CONT * al-Asi, executed in 921 after being accused of conspiracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdallah_ibn_Muhammad
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Umawi
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Abdallah ibn Muhammad)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT 	This article does not cite any references or sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (October 2009)
2 CONT
2 CONT Acap.svg
2 CONT
2 CONT 	This article may require copy editing for grammar, style, cohesion, tone or spelling. You can assist by editing it. (October 2009)
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdullah ibn Muhammad (عبد الله بن محمد; January 11, 844 - October 15, 912) of the Umayyad dynasty, was the seventh Emir of Córdoba, reigning from 888 to 912 in the Al-Andalus (Moorish Iberia).
2 CONT
2 CONT The most formidable threat for the emir was Umar Ibn Hafsun, who had conquered the provinces of Rayyo (including Bobastro), Elvira (including Granada) and Jaén, and had allied with the populations of Archidona, Baeza, Úbeda and Pr
2 CONC iego. In 891 Ibn Hafsun was defeated near the castle of Polei and lost several cities; Abdullah after the victory massacred all the Christians, while the Muslims of the conquered cities were pardoned. However, by the following yea
2 CONC r Ibn Hafsun had already recovered, and conquered back all the lost territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT The emir signed a peace with Ibn Hafsun (who had allied with the Banu Qasi family, controlling the Ebro valley, and the Kingdom of Asturias) in 901; the war however broke out again the following year, only to be halted by the deat
2 CONC h at Córdoba of Abdullah, who was improving his positions. The son he has designed as successor was killed by one of Abdullah's brothers. The latter was in turn executed by Abdullah's father, who named as successor Abd ar-Rahman I
2 CONC II, son of the killed son of Abdullah.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Altamira, Rafael (1999). "Il califfato occidentale". Storia del mondo medievale. II. pp. 477–515. 
