1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Luis de la Cerda "Louis d'Espagne", 1er. Príncipe de la Fortuna.
2 CONT * Born	1291, France 
2 CONT * Died	July 5, 1348  Lamotte-du-Rhône
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father	Alfonso de la Cerda
2 CONT * Mother	Matilde of Brienne-Eu
2 CONT
2 CONT * Spouses: 1) Leonor de Guzmán 2)  Guiote D'Uzès 3) unknown mistress 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luis_de_la_Cerda
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Luis de La Cerda''', also called Louis of Spain (France, 1291 - Lamotte-du-Rhône, July 5, 1348) was an expatriate royal prince of the Crown of Castile, who lived and served in the Kingdom of France. Among his titles, Luis de la
2 CONC  Cerda was the count of Talmont, count of Clermont and an admiral of France. He was also made the first 'Prince of Fortuna' (sovereign ruler of the Canary Islands) by Pope Clement VI in 1344, although he never actually set foot on
2 CONC  the islands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Luis de la Cerda was the third son of Alfonso de la Cerda, the disinherited and Matilde of Brienne-Eu (daughter of John II of Brienne).[1] Alfonso had been chosen to inherit the Kingdom of León from his grandfather King Alfonso X 
2 CONC of Castile-León, but was deposed and driven into exile in 1284 by his uncle, Sancho IV. As a result, most of Alfonso's children, including Luis de la Cerda, were born and raised abroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis de La Cerda was the great-grandson of Louis IX of France through his paternal grandmother Blanche of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Luis de la Cerda spent almost all of his life in the Kingdom of France, in the service of the French crown, and fought in the Hundred Years' War on behalf of his adopted country. King Philip VI of France invested Luis de la Cerda 
2 CONC as Count of Clermont and the first Count of Talmont in 1338/39. He was appointed Admiral of France in 1340.
2 CONT
2 CONT Luis de la Cerda was buried in the Abbey of Saint-Gilles in Languedoc, France. His titles of Talmont and Prince of Fortuna were inherited by his eldest living son Luis de la Cerda y Guzmán. But after the male lines died without is
2 CONC sue, the titles passed through Luis de la Cerda's daughter Isabel de la Cerda Pérez de Guzmán into the house of the Counts (and later Dukes) of Medinacelli. Although it is reported that the papal-conferred title of Prince of Fortu
2 CONC na automatically expired after five years with no expedition, the De la Cerda-Medinacelli family continued to press their claim for the lordship of the islands.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1306, Luis de la Cerda married Leonor Pérez de Guzmán y Coronel, lady of Huelva and Puerto de Santa María, daughter of Alonso Pérez de Guzmán and María Alfonso Coronel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Offspring from this marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Alfonso, Maria, Blanca, Fernando and a second Maria de la Cerda, all of which died in childhood.
2 CONT * Luis de la Cerda y Guzmán (c. 1325 - 1383), second titular Prince of Fortuna and second Count of Talmont.
2 CONT * Juan de la Cerda y Guzmán (1327–1357), Lord of Puerto de Santa María and Gibraleón, briefly alguacil mayor of Seville (f. 1355).
2 CONT * Isabel de la Cerda y Guzmán (c. 1329 - 1382), Lady of Puerto de Santa María, married 1. Rodrigo Pérez Ponce de León and 2. Bernal de Foix, 1st Count of Medinaceli.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his first wife's death, Luis de la Cerda married Guiote D'Uzès, daughter of Robert I, Viscount of Uzès. There was no issue from this marriage.  She married secondly (5 Apr 1361) Aymar de Poitiers Seigneur de Chalançon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Outside of marriage, Luis de la Cerda also had a bastard son Juan de España, born in France in 1347, and recognized in his father's will. [not listed at Medlands]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Titles
2 CONT *1340 - 1341 Almirante de Francia  
2 CONT *1344 - ?  de Francia ante la Santa Sede 
2 CONT *1331 - 1348  Señor de Garganta la Olla, Señor de Pasarón   
2 CONT *  1338 - 1348 Señor de Torremenga
2 CONT *1344 - 1351 Conde de Talmont     
2 CONT *Príncipe de la Fortuna 
