1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bernardo del Carpio
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernardo del Carpio is a legendary hero of medieval Iberian legend (namely Spanish), comparable to El Cid, though with less historical evidence of his actual existence.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story
2 CONT
2 CONT Supposedly the nephew of Alfonso II of Asturias, stories feature him striving against Alfonso to release his father from prison. Other stories have him as the rival and slayer of Roland at Roncesvaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernardo was said to be the son of Sancho, the Count of Saldana and Dona Jimena (Alfonso II's sister, therefore grandson of King Fruela I of Asturias). Alfonso was not happy with the marriage, so he had Sancho blinded and thrown i
2 CONC nto prison and took Bernardo. He was raised in Alfonso II's court. Everybody was ordered not to tell the young Bernardo who his father was.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso invited Charlemagne into Iberia to defeat the Moors, promising to name him as heir. Bernardo's victory at Roncesvaux ended that plan. But Bernardo then joined up with the Moors, hoping to force Alfonso into action; but Alf
2 CONC onso secretly had Sancho killed while in prison.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of the legend of Bernardo del Carpio is sung out by jongleurs from the Kingdom of León.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, the Castilian poet Pero Ferrús (fl. 1380) mentions Bernardo del Carpio in one of his cantigas, which combines the theme of the good life in Castile with a series of loores, or lyric paeans, to a series of Greek, Roman, Bibl
2 CONC ical, chivalric, and Arab heroes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1624 Bernardo de Balbuena published El Bernardo, an account of Bernardo's exploits. It was once considered one of the masterpieces of Spanish literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the opening of Don Quixote, Cervantes has the Don especially admiring Bernardo because he crushed Roland with his arms alone, although the context is clear that Don Quixote is placing too much credence in the fantastic stories 
2 CONC of romance.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]At present
2 CONT
2 CONT Nowadays, Bernardo del Carpio is seen by many as a national hero of the Kingdoms of León and Asturias.
