2 CONT Arco y Garay, Ricardo del. "Dos Infantes de Navarra, Senores en Monzon". Príncipe de Viana. 10:249-74 (1949). Pamplona, German de. "Filiation y Derechos al Trono de Navarra de Garcia Ramirez el Restaurador". Príncipe de Viana. 10:
2 CONC 275-83 (1949). Salazar y Acha, Jaime de. "Reflexiones Sobre la Posible Historicidad de un Episodio de la Crónica Najerense". Príncipe de Viana. 55:149-156 (1994).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ramiro Sánchez of Monzón (1070 - 1116) was a noble kinsman of the kings of Navarre, and lord of the town of Monzón, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was Sancho Garcés, an illegitimate son of king García Sánchez III of Navarre. His mother was Constance, whose parentage has been subject to recent speculation - associated with the lords of Marañon in traditional source
2 CONC s, she has lately been suggested to have been daughter of queen Estefanía, King García's wife, and hence stepsister of her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the fall of his uncle, king Sancho IV of Navarre, the kingdom was divided between Castile and Aragon, and the royal family parceled out between the two. Ramiro was thus raised at the Aragonese court, and was lord of Monzón, i
2 CONC n which we was succeeded by his eldest son, the future king García Ramírez of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT María and Cristina, daughters of Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar, beaten and left for dead by their husbands, the lords of Carrión. Cristina was eventually remarried to Ramiro.Ramiro was married to Cristina Rodríguez, daughter of Rodrigo Dí
2 CONC az de Vivar. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT García Ramírez (the Restorer), King of Navarre Alfonso, lord of Castroviejo Estefania, wife of count Fruela Díaz Elvira, wife of count Rodrigo Gómez and mother of Gonzalo Ruiz of Bureba [edit] Sources Primary The Chronicle of Alfo
2 CONC nso the Emperor Secondary Arco y Garay, Ricardo del. "Dos Infantes de Navarra, Senores en Monzon". Príncipe de Viana. 10:249-74 (1949). Pamplona, Germán de. "Filiation y Derechos al Trono de Navarra de Garcia Ramirez el Restaurado
2 CONC r". Príncipe de Viana. 10:275-83 (1949). Salazar y Acha, Jaime de. "Reflexiones Sobre la Posible Historicidad de un Episodio de la Crónica Najerense". Príncipe de Viana. 55:149-156 (1994). Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/w
2 CONC iki/Ramiro_S%C3%A1nchez,_Lord_of_Monz%C3%B3n" Categories: 1070 births | 1116 deaths | House of Jiménez | Navarrese monarchs | 12th-century Spanish people
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ramiro Sánchez of Monzón (1070 - 1116) was a noble kinsman of the kings of Navarre, and lord of the town of Monzón, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was Sancho Garcés, an illegitimate son of king García Sánchez III of Navarre. His mother was Constance, whose parentage has been subject to recent speculation - associated with the lords of Marañon in traditional source
2 CONC s, she has lately been suggested to have been daughter of queen Estefanía, King García's wife, and hence stepsister of her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the fall of his uncle, king Sancho IV of Navarre, the kingdom was divided between Castile and Aragon, and the royal family parceled out between the two. Ramiro was thus raised at the Aragonese court, and was lord of Monzón, i
2 CONC n which we was succeeded by his eldest son, the future king García Ramírez of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramiro was married to Cristina Rodríguez, daughter of Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar, El Cid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also see: Kings of Navarre family tree
2 CONT
