2 CONT <td colspan=3>
2 CONT William I,  called <font class="name">"William the Conqueror"</font>, was an illegitimate son of Robert I, dukeof Normandy. His mother was a tanner's daughter. William succeeded his father when he was only 7 years old. At 24 hehad
2 CONC  made himself the mightiest feudal lord in all France by various conquests, but his ambition was not satisfied. Helaid plans to become king of England also.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->William's wife Matilda was descended from the old Anglo-Saxon line of kings. Among their children were foursons: Robert, future duke of Normandy; Richard, who died as a youth; William Rufus
2 CONC , who succeeded his father as kingof England; and Henry, who succeeded William Rufus. One daughter, Adela, became the mother of England's King Stephen.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 --><font class="name">Edward the Confessor</font>, king of England, was William's cousin. William used hisconnection with Flanders to put pressure on Edward to extort a promise that he would b
2 CONC ecome heir to the Englishthrone. It is probable that Edward made some kind of pledge to William as early as 1051. Edward died childless on Jan.5, 1066. William then claimed the throne on the basis of this promise. The English, how
2 CONC ever, chose Harold, earl ofWessex, as their king.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->William prepared a large expedition and set sail for England. On Oct. 14, 1066, he defeated and killed Harold atHastings in one of the decisive battles of the world (see Hastings, Battle of
2 CONC ). Then he marched on London, and onChristmas day he was crowned king.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->After subduing England's powerful earls, William seized their lands for his Norman nobles and ordered the noblesto build fortified stone castles to protect their lands. As payment for their
2 CONC  fiefs, the nobles supplied the king witharmed knights. French became the language of the king's court and gradually blended with the Anglo-Saxon tongue.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->William won the loyalty of the mass of the people by wisely retaining the old Anglo-Saxon laws, courts, andcustoms with only a few changes. Thus the principle of self-government, which lies
2 CONC  at the root of the political systemof English-speaking peoples, was preserved and strengthened. At the same time, William taught the English theadvantages of a central government strong enough to control feudal lords.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->Toward the end of his reign, William ordered a great census to be taken of all the lands and people of England.This survey was called Domesday Book. Two of the original books may still be s
2 CONC een at the Public Records Office inLondon. "So very narrowly did he cause the survey to be made," complained the old Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, "that therewas not a single rood of land, nor an ox, or a cow, or a pig passed by, and tha
2 CONC t was not set down in the accounts."
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->William was often on the continent dealing with his widespread holdings. He died there in 1087 from injuriesreceived while warring with Philip I of France. William was a man of great statur
2 CONC e and had a tremendous voice. Suchwas the good order he established that, according to a quaint historian of his time, "any man, who was himself aught,might travel over the kingdom with a bosom of gold unmolested, and no man durst
2 CONC  kill another, however great the injuryhe might have received from him." He was succeeded in Normandy by his eldest son, Robert, and in England by his secondson, William II, called William Rufus.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->He invaded England defeating the English forces in the Battle of Hastings in Oct. 1066. He became King ofEngland on Christmas Day 1066 and ruled until his death in 1087.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->He appointed the Norman nobles to high positions and divided the land among Norman's, forcing most Anglo-Saxonsto become servants. William had England surveyed to determine how much propert
2 CONC y there was in England and who owned it.This survey became known as Doomsday Book.  It is claimed that the ancestral lineage of William also is directlytraceable to <font class="name">Charlemagne</font> and then on back to Julius 
2 CONC Caesar.
2 CONT <br class="half">&nbsp;</br><!-- 3725 -->William and Matilda founded two monastic communities in Caen, France. The <fontclass="name">Abbaye-auz-Hommes</font>, dedicated to St. Stephan for men, and the <fontclass="name">Abbaye-aux-
2 CONC Dames</font> for women.
2 CONT </td>
2 CONT <tr><td>For more information see the <a href="http://www.renderplus.com/hartgen/index.htm">Our Folk - Hart family</a> Web Site</td></tr></table>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT from &quot;Our Folk&quot; by Albert D Hart, Jr.
1 NOTE Guillaume le Bâtard, Guillaume Ier roi d'Angleterre
1 NOTE Reigned 1066-1087. Duke of Normandy 1035-1087. Invaded England,
2 CONT defeated and killed his rival Harold at the Battle of Hastings and
2 CONT became King. The Norman conquest of England was completed by 1072
2 CONT aided by the establishment of feaudalism under which his followers
2 CONT were granted land in return for pledges of service and loyalty. As
2 CONT King William was noted for his efficient if harsh rule. His
2 CONT administration relied upon Norman and other foreign personnell
2 CONT especially Lanfranc, Archbishop of Canterbury. In 1085 started
2 CONT Domesday Book.
