2 CONT William I
2 CONT The victory of William I, 'the Conqueror' (1066-1087), Duke of Normandy,at Hastings and his subsequent coronation in Westminster Abbey onChristmas Day 1066 did not give him complete control of England.Remaining resistance was, how
2 CONC ever, severely crushed and castles built tocontrol the country (including a fortress at Windsor, and the White Towerat the Tower of London). The lands of defeated Saxon nobles were given toWilliam's followers in return for militar
2 CONC y service by a certain number ofknights, so that the tenants' foremost obligation was allegiance to theKing. This firmly established the feudal system. In 1086, Williamcommissioned the Domesday Book, to record land holdings for th
2 CONC eassessment of taxes and other dues. William spent long periods inNormandy to maintain his authority there, dealing with rebellions andFrench invasions. William died in 1087 in Normandy, leaving his duchy tohis eldest son, Robert,
2 CONC  and England to his next surviving son, WilliamRufus.
2 CONT
