2 CONC hich was being held for him before the hall, struck in his spurs, and went his way. At this deed was Count Baldwin much enraged; and when matters had thus remained a while, Duke William sent once more to Count Baldwin to parley ag
2 CONC ain of the marriage. The count sounded his daughter on the subject, and she answered that it pleased her well. So the nuptials took place with very great joy. And after the aforesaid matters, Count Baldwin, laughing withal, asked 
2 CONC his daughter, wherefore she had so lightly accepted the marriage she had aforetime so cruelly refused. And she answered that she did not know the duke so well then as she did now; for, said she, if he had not great heart and high 
