2 CONT
2 CONT 7 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Her birthdate is generally believed to have been between 1060 and 1064; however, there is some evidence she was born after her father's accession to the English throne in 1066. She was the favourite sister of King Henry I of Engla
2 CONC nd; they were probably the youngest of the Conqueror's children. Adela was a high-spirited and educated woman, with a knowledge of Latin.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Stephen Henry, son and heir to the count of Blois, sometime between 1080 and 1084, probably in 1083. Stephen inherited Blois, Chartres and Meaux in 1089, and owned over 300 properties, making him one of the wealthiest 
2 CONC men of his day. He was a pious and revered leader who managed huge areas of France which inherited from his father and added to by his sharp administrations. He was, essentially a king in his own right. Stephen-Henry joined the Fi
2 CONC rst Crusade, along with his brother-in-law Robert Curthose. Stephen's letters to Adela form a uniquely intimate insight into the experiences of the Crusade's leaders. The Count of Blois returned to France in 1100 bringing with him
2 CONC  several cartloads of maps, jewels and other treasures, which he deposited at Chartres. He was, however, under an obligation to the pope for agreements made years earlier and returned to Antioch to participate in the crusade of 11
2 CONC 01. He was ultimately killed in an ill advised charge at the Battle of Ramla. Rumors of his cowardice and defection under fire are untrue and unfounded and have been proven to be propaganda generated by later biased historians. St
2 CONC ephen-Henry was often referred to as "le Sage," and was a great patron of Troubadours and writers.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela and Stephen's children are listed here as follows. Their birth order is uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.William, Count of Sully married Agnes of Sully (d. aft 1104) and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Theobald II, aka Thibaud IV Count of Champagne
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Odo of Blois, aka Humbert. died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Stephen of Blois {King of England}.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Lucia-Mahaut, married Richard d'Avranches, 2nd Earl of Chester. Both drowned on 25 November 1120.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.Agnes of Blois, married Hugh de Puiset and were parents to Hugh de Puiset.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.Eléonore of Blois (d. 1147) married Raoul I of Vermandois (d.1152) & had issue they were divorced in 1142.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.Alix of Blois (d. 1145) married Renaud (d.1134)III of Joigni & had Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.Lithuise of Blois (d. 1118) married Milo I of Montlhéry(Divorced 1115)
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.Philip (d. 1100) Bishop of Châlons-sur-Marne
2 CONT
2 CONT 11.Henry of Blois b.1101- d. 1171 (oblate child raised at Cherite sur Loire (Cluny Abbey) 1103.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Regent
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela was regent for her husband during his extended absence as a leader of the First Crusade (1095-1098), and when he returned in disgrace it was at least in part at her urging that he returned to the east to fulfil his vow of se
2 CONC eing Jerusalem.[citation needed] She was again regent in 1101, continuing after her husband's death on this second crusading expedition in 1102, for their children were still minors. Orderic Vitalis praises her as a "wise and spir
2 CONC ited woman" who ably governed her husband's estates in his absences and after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT She employed tutors to educate her elder sons, and had her youngest son Henry pledged to the Church at Cluny. Adela quarrelled with her eldest son Guillaume, "deficient in intelligence as well as degenerate", and had his younger b
2 CONC rother Theobald replace him as heir. Her son Stephen left Blois in 1111 to join his uncle's court in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela retired to Marcigny in 1120, secure in the status of her children. Later that same year, her daughter Lucia-Mahaut, was drowned in the wreck of the White Ship alongside her husband. She lived long enough to see her son Steph
2 CONC en seize the English throne, and took pride in the ascension of her youngest child Henry Blois to the bishophric of Winchester, but died soon after on 8 March 1135 in Marsilly, Charente, Poitou-Charentes, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] In fiction
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela was portrayed by Nike Arrighi in the two-part BBC TV play Conquest (1966), about her father's conquest of England, part of the series Theatre 625.
2 CONT
