2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Fille de Guillaume le Conquérant
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT      Adela 1062?-1137, mother of Stephen, king of England, and the fourth, and probably the youngest, daughter of
2 CONT
2 CONT  William the Conqueror and Matilda of Flanders, was born about 1062. Her beauty and valour in her early years are 
2 CONT
2 CONT described by many contemporary Norman chroniclers. While she was still a child she was affianced to Simon 
2 CONT
2 CONT Crispin, earl of Amiens, the son and heir of Ralph, earl of Valois and Mantes, who received his military training at 
2 CONT
2 CONT the court of William the Conqueror. But soon after his father's death in 1074 Simon fell into a settled melancholy; 
2 CONT
2 CONT and on being summoned in 1077 to marry Adela, he refused, and withdrew to a monastery. But already in 1075 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela had been demanded in marriage by Stephen, earl of Meaux and Brie, son and heir of Theobald, earl of Blois 
2 CONT
2 CONT and Chartres, a powerful neighbour of William the Conqueror in Normandy; and although Stephen's suit had at first been unfavourably received, it was repeated in 1080, and readily accepted by William and his nobles. Adela was marri
2 CONC ed in the same year at Breteuil, and the ceremony was repeated with much splendour at Chartres, the chief town in her father-in-law's dominion. Baldric of Anjou, abbot of Bourgeuil, and other courtly poets, speak of her at the tim
2 CONC e as being her father's equal in bravery, a Latin and Greek scholar, and a generous patron of poetry, at which she was herself an adept (Histoire Littéraire de la France, vii. 152, ix. 131).
2 CONT      In 1090, on the death of Theobald, her husband's father, Stephen succeeded to his rule, and Adela played an active part in public life. In most of the charters issued by Stephen her name was mentioned, and an inscription, unt
2 CONC il recently legible, on a gate at Blois testifies to a grant of privileges to the town from Stephen the Earl and Adela the Countess conjointly. Disputes between monasteries, and ecclesiastical affairs generally, she seems to have 
2 CONC controlled by her own authority, with the aid of her intimate friend Ivo, bishop of Chartres
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with a previous suggestion of Anselm, she spent the last years of her life in a convent. She took the veil at the Cluniac priory of Marcigny on the Loire, in the diocese of Autun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT      Ordericus Vitalis, Historia Ecclesiastica, is the chief contemporary authority. The best account of Adela's life will be found in Mrs. Green's Lives of the Princesses of England, i. 34-72, where very full references to all th
2 CONC e original authorities are given
2 CONT      see also Freeman's Norman Conquest, iii. and iv., and his William Rufus.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/48107633/adele_of_normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT Royalty. Born the daughter of William the Conqueror and Mathilda of Flanders. She married Stephen of Blois sometime between 1080 and 1084. She acted as regent while he was in the Holy Land during the first crusade. He returned aft
2 CONC er the siege of Antioch (1100) and she pursued him to complete his promise to see Jerusalem. So he returned in 1101 and was killed in the Battle of Ramla. She continued to act as regent for her minor children and later retired to 
2 CONC the Abbey of Marcigny where she died.
2 CONT
