1 NOTE Adela, French ADÉLE (b. 1062?--d. 1137), daughter of William I the Conqueror of England and his wife, Matilda of Flanders, and mother of Stephen, king of England, whose right to the throne derived through her. She was married to 
2 CONC Stephen, earl of Meaux and Brie, in 1080 at Breteuil. Upon the death of his father in 1090, her husband succeeded to the earldom of Blois and Chartres. She appears to have played an active role in the administration of her husband
2 CONC 's lands, regularly witnessing his charters, took an active interest in civil and ecclesiastical affairs, and was instrumental in rebuilding the catherdral of Chartres in stone. Having inherited her father's appetite and ability t
2 CONC o rule, she became regent in 1095 when she persuaded her popular but weak-willed husband to join the First Crusade to the Holy Land. Although in charge of the central funds of the Crusade, Stephen deserted at Antioch in 1098, unde
2 CONC rstandably enough in face of overwhelming odds. Unfortunately for his reputation, the crusaders survived and succeeded in capturing Jerusalem in 1099. After Stephen's return home in 1099, Adela waged a sustained campaign of bullyi
2 CONC ng and moral blackmail that extended into their bedroom where, between intercourse, she would urge Stephen to think of his reputation and return to the Holy Land. In the end, her nagging worked and Stephen departed east once more 
2 CONC in 1101, to meet a satisfactorily noble death at Ramlah in 1102. No longer a coward's wife but more congenially a hero's widow, Adela continued to rule Blois-Chartres during the minority of her sons. Anselm, her guest and teacher 
2 CONC in 1097, was often entertained by her during 1103 and 1105 and she affected a temporary reconciliation between him and her brother, Henry I, who lavished patronage on her second son, Stephen, and appointed a third, Henry, bishop o
2 CONC f Winchester, the richest see in England. In 1107 Adela entertained Pope Pascal during Easter and in the following year was hostess to Bohemund of Antioch. She made her son Theobald her successor in 1109, and persuaded him to join
2 CONC  her brother Henry I against France in 1117. In 1120 she retired to the abbey of Marcigny-sur-Loire where she died in 1137. By all accounts a forceful personality, Adela's qualities were not uncommon among women artistocrats, alth
2 CONC ough more often they found an outlet in the running of nunneries. Adela's secular career, as de facto ruler for more than a decade of one of the most powerful principalities of northern France, is exceptional testimony to the powe
2 CONC r of breeding as well as to her own determination. She was a benevolent patroness of churches and monasteries. Although married to a French count and living to see a son crowned king of England, she chose to be buried beside her m
2 CONC other at Caen under an inscription 'Adela, filia regis'. She was always the Conqueror's daughter. [Sources: Who's Who in Early Medieval England, Christopher Tyerman, Shepheard-Walwyn, Ltd., London, 1996; Encyclopædia Britannica CD
2 CONC , 1997]
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela, French ADÉLE (b. 1062?--d. 1137), daughter of William I the Conqueror of England and his wife, Matilda of Flanders, and mother of Stephen, king of England, whose right to the throne derived through her. She was married to 
2 CONC Stephen, earl of Meaux and Brie, in 1080 at Breteuil. Upon the death of his father in 1090, her husband succeeded to the earldom of Blois and Chartres. She appears to have played an active role in the administration of her husband
2 CONC 's lands, regularly witnessing his charters, took an active interest in civil and ecclesiastical affairs, and was instrumental in rebuilding the catherdral of Chartres in stone. Having inherited her father's appetite and ability t
2 CONC o rule, she became regent in 1095 when she persuaded her popular but weak-willed husband to join the First Crusade to the Holy Land. Although in charge of the central funds of the Crusade, Stephen deserted at Antioch in 1098, unde
2 CONC rstandably enough in face of overwhelming odds. Unfortunately for his reputation, the crusaders survived and succeeded in capturing Jerusalem in 1099. After Stephen's return home in 1099, Adela waged a sustained campaign of bullyi
2 CONC ng and moral blackmail that extended into their bedroom where, between intercourse, she would urge Stephen to think of his reputation and return to the Holy Land. In the end, her nagging worked and Stephen departed east once more 
2 CONC in 1101, to meet a satisfactorily noble death at Ramlah in 1102. No longer a coward's wife but more congenially a hero's widow, Adela continued to rule Blois-Chartres during the minority of her sons. Anselm, her guest and teacher 
2 CONC in 1097, was often entertained by her during 1103 and 1105 and she affected a temporary reconciliation between him and her brother, Henry I, who lavished patronage on her second son, Stephen, and appointed a third, Henry, bishop o
2 CONC f Winchester, the richest see in England. In 1107 Adela entertained Pope Pascal during Easter and in the following year was hostess to Bohemund of Antioch. She made her son Theobald her successor in 1109, and persuaded him to join
2 CONC  her brother Henry I against France in 1117. In 1120 she retired to the abbey of Marcigny-sur-Loire where she died in 1137. By all accounts a forceful personality, Adela's qualities were not uncommon among women artistocrats, alth
2 CONC ough more often they found an outlet in the running of nunneries. Adela's secular career, as de facto ruler for more than a decade of one of the most powerful principalities of northern France, is exceptional testimony to the powe
2 CONC r of breeding as well as to her own determination. She was a benevolent patroness of churches and monasteries. Although married to a French count and living to see a son crowned king of England, she chose to be buried beside her m
2 CONC other at Caen under an inscription 'Adela, filia regis'. She was always the Conqueror's daughter. [Sources: Who's Who in Early Medieval England, Christopher Tyerman, Shepheard-Walwyn, Ltd., London, 1996; Encyclopædia Britannica CD
2 CONC , 1997]
1 NOTE Source:
2 CONT Stuart Roderick, W.
2 CONT Royalty for Commoners, 3rd Edit. Published, Genealogical Publishing Co, Inc. Baltomore, MD. 1998,
2 CONT ISBN-0-8063-1561-X Text 324-40
2 CONT Source II
2 CONT Alison Weir, Britains Royal Family A Complete Genealogy 1999, ppg 41-44
1 NOTE Adela av Normandie, også kjent som Adela av Blois og Adela av England (ca 1062 eller 1067 - 8. mars 1137?) var gjennom ekteskap grevinne av Blois, Chartres og Meaux. Hun var datter av William Erobreren og Matilda av Flandern. Hun
2 CONC  var også mor til både Stefan, konge av England, og Henrik av Blois, biskop av Winchester.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hennes fødselsdag regnes vanligvis å ha vært mellom 1060 og 1064, men der er bevis som antyder at hun var født etter at hennes far ble konge i England i 1066. Hun var favorittsøsteren til Henrik I av England. De var antagelig de 
2 CONC yngste av Williams barn. Hun var en utdannet kvinne med kunnskap om latin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hun giftet seg med Stefan, greve av Blois, sønn og arving av greven av Blois, en gang mellom 1080 og 1084, antagelig i 1083. Stefan arvet Blois, Chartres og Meaux i 1089 og eide over 300 eiendommer, noe som gjorde ham til en av de
2 CONC  rikeste mennene på hans tid. Han var en from og aktet leder. Stefan sluttet seg til det første korstoget sammen med sin svoger, Robert Curthose. Stefans brev til Adela gir et unikt intimt innblikk i opplevelsene til korstogets l
2 CONC edere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greven av Blois kom hjem til Frankrike i 1100 og hadde med seg flere vognlass med kart, juveler og andre skatter som satte fra seg i Chartres. Men han var under forpliktelse til paven og dro tilbake til Antiokia for å delta i kor
2 CONC stoget i 1101. Han ble til slutt drept i et lite gjennomtenkt angrep i slaget ved Ramlah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stefan og Adelas barn:
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume (William) (død 1150), greve av Chartres som giftet seg med Agnes of Sulli (død etter 1104). 
2 CONT Teobald II, greve av Champagne 
2 CONT Odo, døde ung. 
2 CONT Stefan, konge av England 
2 CONT Lucia-Mahaut, giftet seg med Richard d'Avranches, 2. jarl av Chester. Begge druknet den 25. november 1120. 
2 CONT Agnes, giftet seg med Hugh III av Le Puiset 
2 CONT Eléonore (død 1147) giftet seg med Raoul I av Vermandois (død 1152). De ble skilt i 1142. 
2 CONT Alix (død 1145) giftet seg med Renaud III av Joigni (død 1134) 
2 CONT Lithuise (død 1118) giftet seg med Milo de Brai, markgreve av Troyes (skilt 1115) 
2 CONT Henrik, biskop av Winchester 
2 CONT Humbert, døde ung. 
2 CONT Adela var regent for hennes ektemann under hans lange fravær som leder i korstoget (1096-1098), og da han kom tilbake i vanære, var det i det minste delvis på grunn av hennes oppfordringer at han dro tilbake til østen for å full
2 CONC føre sine løfter og se Jerusalem. Hun var igjen regent i 1101 og fortsatte etter hennes ektemanns død på sin andre korsfarerreise i 1102, for deres barn var fremdeles mindreårige. Orderic Vitalis lovpriste henne som en ?vis og a
2 CONC ̊ndelig kvinne? som styrte trygt hennes ektemanns eiendommer i hans fravær og etter hans død.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hun ansatte veiledere for å utdanne hennes eldste sønner, og fikk hennes yngste sønn Henrik til å avlegge løfter til kirken ved Cluny. Adela kranglet med sin eldste sønn Guillaume og fikk hans yngre bror Teobald til å erstatte 
2 CONC ham som arving. Hennes sønn Stefan forlot Blois i 1111 for å slutte seg til sin onkels hoff i England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela trakk seg tilbake til Marcigny i 1120. Senere samme år, druknet hennes datter Lucia-Mahaut i vraket til Det hvite skip sammen med hennes ektemann. Hun levde lenge nok til å se sin sønn Stefan ta den engelske tronen, og fik
2 CONC k oppleve at hennes yngste barn, Henrik, ble biskop i Winchester, men hun døde kort tid etterpå.
2 CONT
2 CONT Litteratur
2 CONT LoPrete,Kimberly, ?The Anglo-Norman Card of Adela of Blois?, Albion 22 (1990) 
2 CONT LoPrete, Kimberly, ?Adela of Blois and Ivo of Chartres: Piety, Politics, and the Peace in the Diocese of Chartres?, Anglo-Norman Studies 19 
2 CONT Parsons, John Carmi, Medieval Mothering (New Middle Ages), 1996 
2 CONT LoPrete, Kimberly. ?Adela of Blois? Countess and Lord, C.1067-1137, Four Courts, Dublin, 2007 
1 NOTE OR "ADELIZA"
1 NOTE BETROTHED TO HAROLD (LATER KING HAROLD II)
