2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=feca6090-21f3-4d24-ad19-d83bac94324c&tid=12140672&pid=-321237034
1 NOTE The third Norman king of England, also duke of Normandy. Because his father, who died in 1087, left him no land, Henry made several unsuccessful attempts to gain territories on the Continent. On the death of his brother William II
2 CONC  in 1100, Henry took advantage of the absence of another brother Robert, who had a prior claim to the throne to seize the royal treasury and have himself crowned king at Westminster. Henry subsequently secured his position with th
2 CONC e nobles and with the church by issuing a charter of liberties that acknowledged the feudal rights of the nobles and the rights of the church. In 1101 Robert, who was duke of Normandy, invaded England, but Henry persuaded him to w
2 CONC ithdraw by promising him a pension and military aid on the Continent. In 1102 Henry put down a revolt of nobles, who subsequently took refuge in Normandy, where they were aided by Robert. By defeating Robert at Tinchebray, France,
2 CONC  in 1106, Henry won Normandy. During the rest of his reign, however, he constantly had to put down uprisings that threatened his rule in Normandy. The conflict between Henry and Anselm, archbishop of Canterbury, over the question 
2 CONC of lay investiture (the appointment of church officials by the king), was settled in 1107 by a compromise that left the king with substantial control in the matter. Because he had no surviving male heir, Henry was forced to design
2 CONC ate his daughter Matilda as his heiress. After his death Henry's nephew, Stephen of Blois, usurped the throne, plunging the country into a protracted civil war that ended only with the accession of Matilda's son, Henry II, in 1154
2 CONC . He also had a child who died young. He was called "the Lion of Justice". 
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-3597
1 NOTE Henry I of England
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=1f5eb8cb-6aaf-4ce6-a348-278d26be93f2&tid=7179083&pid=-603563936
1 NOTE Henry I was Duke of Normandy from 1106-1135 and King of England from1100-1135. William I left Normandy to his oldest son Robert II Curthoseand England to his next oldest son, William II Rufus. Henry was leftgreat wealth and eventu
2 CONC ally outmanuvered his brothers to become King ofEngland in 1100 and ruled 35 years. Henry is remembered for expanding andstrengthening royal justice, integrating the Norman and Anglo-Saxon legalsystems, and laying the foundation f
2 CONC or more centralized royal rule. "TheEncyclopedia of the Middle Ages" Norman F. Cantor, General Editor.
