2 CONT William I (b. 1131 - d. May 7, 1166), called the Bad or the Wicked, was the second king of Sicily, ruling from his father's death in 1154 to his own. He was the fourth son of Roger II and Elvira of Castile. His maternal grandparen
2 CONC ts were Alfonso VI of Castile and a concubine (perhaps wife) baptised Isabella, but born Zaida.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's title "the Bad" seems little merited and expresses the bias of the historian Hugo Falcandus and the baronial class against the king and the official class by whom he was guided. It is obvious, however, that William was f
2 CONC ar inferior in character and energy to his father, and was attached to the semi-Muslim life of his gorgeous palaces of Palermo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT He grew up with little expectation of ruling. The deaths of his three older brothers Roger, Tancred, and Alfonso between 1138 and 1148 changed matters, though when his father died William was still not well-prepared to take his pl
2 CONC ace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kingship
2 CONT
2 CONT On assuming power, William kept the administration which had guided his father's rule for his final years. Only the Englishman Thomas Brun was removed, and the chancellor Maio of Bari was promoted. The real power in the kingdom wa
2 CONC s at first exercised by this Maio, a man of low birth, whose title ammiratus ammiratorum was the highest in the realm. Maio continued Roger's policy of excluding the nobles from the administration, and sought also to curtail the l
2 CONC iberties of the towns. The barons, always chafing against the royal power, were encouraged to revolt by Pope Adrian IV, whose recognition William had not yet sought, by the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus, and by the Holy Roma
2 CONC n Emperor Frederick I.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of 1155, Greek troops recovered Bari, Trani, Giovinazzo, Andria, Taranto and began to besiege Brindisi. Landing on the peninsula, William's army destroyed the Greek fleet (4 ships) and army at Brindisi (May 28, 1156) an
2 CONC d recovered Bari. Adrian came to terms at Benevento in a treaty of the same name (June 18), abandoning the rebels and confirming William as king. During the summer of AD 1157, he sent a fleet of 164 ships carrying 10,000 men to sa
2 CONC ck Euboea and Almira. In 1158 William made peace with the Greeks.
2 CONT
2 CONT These diplomatic successes were probably due to Maio; on the other hand, the African dominions were lost to the Almohads, and it is possible that he advised their abandonment in face of the dangers threatening the kingdom down fro
2 CONC m the north. In 1156, a revolt began in Sfax and quickly spread. Nothing was done to put it down. In 1159, the admiral Peter led a raiding expedition against the Saracen-held Balearic Islands with 160 ships. He tried to relieve be
2 CONC sieged Mahdia with the same fleet, but turned around just after engaging in battle. Peter did not fall out of favour, but no further assistance was sent to the Christians holding out in Mahdia and the city surrendered on 11 Januar
2 CONC y 1160, ending the "African Empire."
2 CONT
2 CONT The policy of Maio led to a general conspiracy, and in November 1160 Maio was murdered in Palermo by Matthew Bonello, leader of the Sicilian nobles. The barons, however, had long been plotting to overthrow the king. Desiring a wea
2 CONC k power on the throne, they had been eyeing the king's eldest son, Roger, Duke of Apulia, as a possible replacement for his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the assassination of Maio, the royal palace was stormed by two of the king's own relatives: Simon, his illegitimate half-brother, whom he had dispossessed of Taranto early in his reign, and Tancred, his bastard nephew, the c
2 CONC ount of Lecce. The king was captured along with his whole family, his life being barely spared by one Richard of Mandra. Roger was then paraded through the streets and it was announced that he would be crowned in the cathedral thr
2 CONC ee days thence.
2 CONT
2 CONT For a while the king remained in the hands of the conspirators, who purposed murdering or just deposing him, but the people and the army rallied round him; he recovered power, crushed the Sicilian rebels, had Bonello blinded, and 
2 CONC in a short campaign reduced the rest of the Regno, avenging the rebel burning of Butera. Sadly, during the initial assault on the palace, to release the captive king, the king's son Roger was killed by a wayward arrow (though Falc
2 CONC andus, seemingly ever-ready to impugn the royal character, has the king kicking his "faithless" son dead).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus freed from feudal revolts, William confided the government to men trained in Maio's school, creating a triumvirate: the grand protonotary, Matthew of Ajello; Count Sylvester of Marsico, who had inherited Maio's property; and 
2 CONC the Bishop Palmer of Syracuse, elect, but not consecrated. His latter years were peaceful; he was now the champion of the true pope against the emperor, and Alexander III was installed in the Lateran Palace in November 1165 by a g
2 CONC uard of Normans.
2 CONT
2 CONT William died on May 7, 1166 and was interred in Monreale Cathedral. By his wife, Margaret of Navarre, daughter of García Ramírez of Navarre, he had four sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger IV, Duke of Apulia (b. 1152 - d. 1161).
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, Prince of Capua (b. 1153 - d. 1158).
2 CONT
2 CONT William II of Sicily (b. 1155 - d. 1189).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Prince of Capua (b. 1158 - d. 1172).
