2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/AQUITAINE.htm#Eleonoredied1204
2 CONT ----
2 CONT =Timeline
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1071: Born to Guy-Geoffrey, William VIth Count of Poitiers, VIIIth Duke of Aquitaine (“William VIII”), and to his third wife, Hildegarde of Burgundy
2 CONT * 1086: Succeeds to his father’s titles at the age of fifteen
2 CONT * 1087: Death of Bishop Isembert and appointment of Peter II of Poitiers
2 CONT * 1094: Marries as his second wife Philippa (or Matilda), heiress of Toulouse
2 CONT * 1098-1100: William IX’s first occupation of Toulouse
2 CONT * 1099: The future Duke William X is born
2 CONT * 1100: Confrontation with the legates of Urban II at a Council at Poitiers
2 CONT * 1101-1102: Participates in a disastrous crusade to the Holy Land
2 CONT * 1113-1122: William IX’s second occupation of Toulouse
2 CONT * 1114-1115: Repudiates Philippa in favour of the Viscountess of Châtellerault and is excommunicated by Bishop Peter II of Poitiers
2 CONT * 1117: Bishop William Gilbert succeeds Peter II; the excommunication lapses
2 CONT * 1119: A former wife complains to Pope Calixtus II at a synod at Reims
2 CONT * 1120: Takes part in Alfonso I of Aragon’s victory over the Moors at Cutanda
2 CONT * 1121: William X marries the daughter of the Viscountess of Châtellerault
2 CONT * 1127: William IX dies and is succeeded by William X
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT GUILLAUME d’Aquitaine, son of GUILLAUME VIII Duke of Aquitaine [GUILLAUME VI Comte de Poitou] & his third wife Hildegarde de Bourgogne [Capet] (22 Oct 1071-10 Feb 1126).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Saint-Maxence records the birth "1071 XI Kal Nov" of "Goffredo duci…Guillelmus filius"[456].  "Willelmi filius eius" subscribed the donation by "Willelmus dux Aquitanorum" of property to St Cyprien, Poitiers by ch
2 CONC arter dated [1073/87][457].  "Goffredus…dux Aquitanorum et Guillelmus filius eius" set entry conditions for monks at Saint-Hilaire de Poitiers by charter dated 1078 or 1079[458].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1086 as GUILLAUME IX Duke of Aquitaine, GUILLAUME VII Comte de Poitou.  Albert of Aix records that "Guillaume comte et prince du Poitou de la famille d'Henri III empereur" crossed Hungary peacefully with
2 CONC  Welf Duke of Bavaria and "la noble comtesse Ida de la marche d'Autriche", entered the territory of the Bulgars in which "le duc des Bulgares nommé Guzh" refused their passage into Adrianople, but that Guillaume captured "le duc d
2 CONC es Bulgares" who was forced to allow the pilgrims to continue[459], undated but in a passage adjacent to text which records events in 1101.  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Albert of Aix, after the army was dispersed in Asia Minor by the Turks, Duke Guillaume fled to Longinach near Tursolt, from where he was rescued and brought to Antioch by Tancred's forces[460].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a troubadour and composer of lyric poetry.  The Chronicle of Saint-Maxence records the death "1126 IV Id Feb" of "Willelmus dux Aquitanorum" and his burial "Pictavis civitate apud Novum Monasterium"[461].  The necrology of 
2 CONC the Prieuré de Fontaines records the death "10 Feb" of "Guillermus dux Aquitanorum"[462]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (1089, divorced 1090) as her first husband, ERMENGARDE d'Anjou, daughter of FOULQUES IV "le Rechin" Comte d'Anjou & his first wife Hildegarde de Baugency ([1068]-Jerusalem 1 Jun 1146).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-
2 CONC Fontaines refers to the daughter of "Fulco" as "comitissam Redonensem" but does not name her[463].  "Fulco Andegavensis comes" donated property to Angers with the consent of "filiis meis Gaufrido et Fulconello et filia mea Ermenga
2 CONC rde" by charter dated 23 Jun 1096[464].  William of Tyre names her "Hermingerda", gives her father's name implying that she was born from his fifth marriage, and names her first husband "Pictaviensium comitis Willelmi", her divorc
2 CONC e and her second husband "comes Brittaniæ"[465].  She married secondly ([1093]) as his second wife, Alain IV "Fergant" Duke of Brittany.  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum records that "comitissa Brittaniæ" was the daughter of Foulqu
2 CONC es & his first wife "filiam Lancelini de Baugenciaco", adding that she became a nun at "Jerusalem in ecclesia Sanctæ Annæ" after her husband died[466].  "Fulco Andecavorum comes nepos Goffridi Martelli…consulis" donated property t
2 CONC o Angers with the consent of "Ermenjarde filia sua comitissa Brittaniæ" by charter dated 12 Apr 1109[467].  The necrology of Angers Cathedral records the death "Kal Jun" of "Ermengardis comitissa Britanniæ mater Conan ducis et sor
2 CONC or Fulconis regis Hierosolymitani"[468].  The Annals of St Salvator Redon record that "Ermengardeque Alani conjugem, vere piam ac religiosam" was buried at the abbey of Redon[469]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (1094, divorced 1115) PHILIPPA [Mathilde] de Toulouse, daughter of GUILLAUME IV Comte de Toulouse & his second wife Emma de Mortain (-28 Nov 1117).  The Chronicle of Saint-Maxence records the marriage of "Guillelmus" an
2 CONC d "Philippam…filiam Willelmi comitis Tolosani et neptem Raimundi de Sancto Egidio"[470].  Robert of Torigny refers to, but does not name, "filiam unam" of "comes Tolosanus frater Raimundi comitis Sancti Ægidii" & his wife, who mar
2 CONC ried "Guillermus comes Pictavensis et dux Aquitanorum"[471].  "Guillelmus…Aquitainie similiter et Vasconie dux et comes" confirmed donations to Sainte-Croix, Bordeaux by "genitor noster Guillelmus qui et Gaufridus vocatus est" wit
2 CONC h the consent of "Mathildis uxor…" by charter dated 23 Mar 1096[472].  It is assumed that Mathilde and Philippa refer to the same person.  "Philippia" daughter of "Wilelmi comitis Tolose" and wife of Guillaume Comte de Poitou made
2 CONC  a joint donation with her husband to Toulouse Saint-Sernin dated Jul 1098[473].  She is also named in an undated donation by Bertrand Comte de Toulouse which names her father but not her husband[474].  “Philippæ comitissæ…Emmæ fi
2 CONC lia” reached agreement with “Bernardus-Atonis filius Ermengardis” by charter dated 1114[475].  Orderic Vitalis recounts that "Hildegarde Ctss de Poitou" complained to the synod of Reims, held in Oct 1119 by Pope Calixtus II, that 
2 CONC her husband had abandoned her for "Malberge wife of the vicomte de Châtellerault"[476].  This is inconsistent with the date of death of Philippa, shown above, not to mention the difference of first name.  She became a nun.  The ne
2 CONC crology of the Prieuré de Fontaines records the death "28 Nov" of "Philippa monacha, Pictavensis comitissa"[477]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): AMAUBERGE [Dangerose], wife of AIMERY [I] Vicomte de Châtellerault, daughter of ---.  She left her husband to live with Duke Guillaume, for which he was excommunicated.  Orderic Vitalis recounts that "Hildegarde Ctss
2 CONC  de Poitou" complained to the synod of Reims, held in Oct 1119 by Pope Calixtus II, that her husband had abandoned her for "Malberge wife of the vicomte de Châtellerault"[478].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230008918&size=large}}
2 CONT Companho, farai un vers qu'er covinen,
2 CONT Et aura·i mais de foudatz no·y a de sen,
2 CONT Et er totz mesclatz d'amor e de joy e de joven.
2 CONT
2 CONT E tenguatz lo per vilan qui no·l enten,
2 CONT O dins son cor voluntiers non l'apren:
2 CONT Greu partir si fai d'amor qui la troba a talen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dos cavalhs ai a ma sselha, ben e gen,
2 CONT Bon son et adreg per armas e valen,
2 CONT E no·ls puesc amdos tener, que l'us l'autre non cossen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Si·ls pogues adomesjar a mon talen,
2 CONT Ja no volgr'alhors mudar mon garnimen,
2 CONT Que meils for'encavalguatz de nuill ome viven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Launs fon dels montaniers lo plus corren,
2 CONT Mas aitan fer' estranhez'a longuamen
2 CONT Et es tan fers e salvatges, que del bailar si defen.
2 CONT
2 CONT L'autre fon noyritz sa jus part Cofolen
2 CONT Ez anc no·n vis bellazor, mon escien:
2 CONT Aquest non er ja camjatz ni per aur ni per argen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qu'ie·l donei a son senhor polin payssen,
2 CONT Pero si·m retinc ieu tan de covenen
2 CONT Que, s'ilh lo tenia un an, qu'ieu lo tengues mais de cen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cavalier, datz mi cosselh d'un pessamen:
2 CONT -Anc mays no fuy issaratz de cauzimen- :
2 CONT Res non sai ab qual me tengua, de n'Agnes o de n'Arsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT De Gimel ai lo castel e·l mandamen,
2 CONT E per Niol fauc ergueill a tota gen:
2 CONT C'ambedui me son jurat e plevit per sagramen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comrades, I shall write a fitting poem,
2 CONT one with more folly than sense,
2 CONT all laden with love, joy and youth.
2 CONT
2 CONT And let he be called a knave, who doesn't understand it,
2 CONT or learn it, for that matter, by heart:
2 CONT people who like poetry hardly part from love.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have two horses I can saddle well and gladly
2 CONT they are good and brave and fit for fighting,
2 CONT and I can't keep them both because they can't stand each other.
2 CONT
2 CONT If I could tame them as I wish,
2 CONT I wouldn't take my gear elsewhere,
2 CONT because I would be mounted better than anyone else.
2 CONT
2 CONT The one is the fastest of the mountaineers
2 CONT but it has been acting fiercely oddly for a long time
2 CONT and it is so fierce and savage that it refuses to be bridled.
2 CONT
2 CONT The other was reared around Confolens
2 CONT and you never saw a prettier one, by my troth:
2 CONT such one can't be traded for gold nor for silver.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because I gave it to its master as a filly
2 CONT but we agreed to the condition
2 CONT that, if he had it for one year, I would have it for a century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Knights, advise me about this conundrum:
2 CONT -never was I [so] troubled by a choice-
2 CONT I don't know which one to keep to, that of dame Agnes or that of dame Arsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT I hold the castle of Gimel and its land,
2 CONT and I boast about Nieul with everybody
2 CONT since both are sworn faithful to me.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230296861&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT Compagno, non puesc mudar qu'eo no m'effrei
2 CONT De novellas qu'ai auzidas et que vei:
2 CONT Qu'una domna s'es clamada de sos gardadors a mei.
2 CONT
2 CONT E diz que non volo prendre dreit ni lei,
2 CONT Ans la teno esserrada quada trei:
2 CONT Tant l'us no·ill larga l'estaca que l'altre plus no la·ill plei.
2 CONT
2 CONT Et aquill fan entre lor aital agrei:
2 CONT L'us es compains gens a foc mandacarrei,
2 CONT E meno trop major nausa que la mainada del rei.
2 CONT
2 CONT Et eu dic vos, gardador, e vos castei:
2 CONT e sera ben grans folia qui no·m crei:
2 CONT Greu verretz neguna garda que ad oras non sonei.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qu'eu anc non vi nulla domn'ab tan gran fei,
2 CONT Qui non vol prendre son plait o sa mercei,
2 CONT S'om l'aloigna de proessa, que ab malvestatz non plaidei.
2 CONT
2 CONT E si·l tenez a cartat lo bon conrei,
2 CONT Adoba·s d'aquel que troba viron sei:
2 CONT Si non pot aver caval, ela compra palafrei.
2 CONT
2 CONT Non i a negu de vos ia·m desautrei,
2 CONT S'om li vedava vi fort per malavei,
2 CONT Non begues enanz de l'aiga que·s laissez morir de sei.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chascus beuri'ans de l'aiga que·s laises morir dessei
2 CONT
2 CONT Comrades, I don't know where to turn without being upset
2 CONT about a case I am called to judge:
2 CONT because a woman complains to me about her wardens.
2 CONT
2 CONT And she says that they don't acknowledge either custom or law;
2 CONT instead, they keep her locked, all the three of them,
2 CONT so much that when one loosens her snares, the others tighten them more.
2 CONT
2 CONT And they behave in such a way
2 CONT (one is as courtly as a hangman's noose)
2 CONT and they make more noise than the King's gang.
2 CONT
2 CONT And I tell you, wards, and I admonish you,
2 CONT and it shall be a proper fool he who doesn't believe me:
2 CONT there hardly is a warden who doesn't sometimes doze.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since I never saw a woman so steadfast
2 CONT that she wouldn't want to take what she likes or deserves,
2 CONT and who, if kept from worth, wouldn't turn to depravity.
2 CONT
2 CONT And if you keep her from proper harness
2 CONT she'll do with what she finds around herself
2 CONT and if she can't have a steed, she'll buy a palfrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT None of you would contradict me if I said
2 CONT that, if one were forbidden to drink strong wine because of an illness,
2 CONT he would rather drink water than let himself die of thirst.
2 CONT
2 CONT Everyone would rather drink water than die of thirst!
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230386855&size=large}}
2 CONT Companho, tant ai agutz d'avols conres
2 CONT Qu'ieu non puesc mudar no·n chan e que no·m pes;
2 CONT Enpero no vueill c'om sapcha mon afar de maintas res.
2 CONT
2 CONT E dirai vos m'entendensa, de que es:
2 CONT No m'azauta cons gardatz ni gorcs ses peis,
2 CONT Ni gabars de malvatz homes com de lor faitz non agues.
2 CONT
2 CONT Senher Dieus, quez es del mon capdels e reis,
2 CONT Qui anc premier gardet con, com non esteis
2 CONT C'anc no fo mestiers ni garda c'a sidons estes sordeis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pero dirai vos de con, cals es sa leis,
2 CONT Com sel hom que mal n'a fait e peitz n'a pres:
2 CONT Si queg'autra res en merma, qui·n pana, e cons en creis.
2 CONT
2 CONT E sel qui no volra·n creire mos casteis,
2 CONT An ho vezer pres lo bosc, en un deveis:
2 CONT Per un albre c'om hi tailla n'i naison ho dos ho treis.
2 CONT
2 CONT E quan lo bocx es taillatz, nais plus espes
2 CONT E·l senher no·n pert son comte ni sos ses:
2 CONT A revers planh hom la tala, si·l dampnatges no·i es ges.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tortz es c'om planha la tala si negun dan no·i a ges.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comrades, I have had so many bad receptions
2 CONT that I cannot do without singing and grieving about it;
2 CONT however, I don't want people to know my business in many things.
2 CONT
2 CONT And I shall tell you my opinion about this:
2 CONT I don't like a warded cunt nor a pond without fish,
2 CONT nor boasting of base men, as if there weren't records of their actions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord God, who are refuge and king of the world,
2 CONT why didn't the first cunt-warden drop dead?
2 CONT Because there never was service, or watch, worse than that.
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore shall I tell you the Law of the Cunt,
2 CONT as a man who's done badly and has been repaid worse:
2 CONT if other things dwindle when you take of them, the cunt grows.
2 CONT
2 CONT And he who will not believe my teachings,
2 CONT let him go see by the wood, in a reservation:
2 CONT for each tree people fell, two or three grow.
2 CONT
2 CONT And when the wood is cut, it grows back thicker
2 CONT and the owner doesn't miss his profit, nor his income:
2 CONT one complains wrongly about the loss, if no harm is done.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230667821&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230587833&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230431869&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Farai un vers, pos mi sonelh,
2 CONT E-m vauc e m'estauc al solelh.
2 CONT Donnas i a de mal conselh,
2 CONT Et sai dir cals:
2 CONT Cellas c'amor de cavalier tornon a mals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Donna no fai pechat mortal
2 CONT Que ama cavalier leal;
2 CONT Mas s'ama monge o clergal
2 CONT Non es raizo:
2 CONT Per dreg la deuri'hom cremar ab un tezo.
2 CONT
2 CONT En Alvernhe, part Lemozi,
2 CONT M'en anei totz sols a tapi:
2 CONT Trobei la moller d'En Guari
2 CONT E d'En Bernart;
2 CONT Saluderon mi sinplamentz per san Launart.
2 CONT
2 CONT La una-m diz en son latin:
2 CONT "O, Deus vos salv, don pelerin;
2 CONT Mout mi semblatz de belh aizin,
2 CONT Mon escient;
2 CONT Mas trop vezem anar pel mond de folla gent."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ar auzires qu'ai respondutz;
2 CONT Anc no li diz ni ba ni butz,
2 CONT Ni fer ni fust no ai mentagutz,
2 CONT Mas sol aitan:
2 CONT "Babariol, babariol, babarian."
2 CONT
2 CONT So diz n'Agnes a n'Ermessen:
2 CONT "Trobat avem que anam queren:
2 CONT Sor, per amor Deu l'alberguem,
2 CONT Que ben es mutz,
2 CONT E ja per lui nostre conselh non er saubutz."
2 CONT
2 CONT La una-m pres sotz son mantel
2 CONT Et mes m'en la cambra, el fornel:
2 CONT Sapchatz qu'a mi fo bon e bel,
2 CONT E-l foc fo bos,
2 CONT Et eu calfei me volentiers als gros carbos.
2 CONT
2 CONT A manjar mi deron capos,
2 CONT E sapchatz agui mais de dos,
2 CONT Et no-i ac cog ni cogastros,
2 CONT Mas sol nos tres;
2 CONT E-l pans fo blancs e-l vins fo bos e-l pebr'espes.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sor, si aquest hom es ginhos
2 CONT Ni laicha a parlar per nos,
2 CONT Nos aportem nostre gat ros
2 CONT De mantement,
2 CONT Qe-l fara parlar az estros, si de re-nz ment."
2 CONT
2 CONT N'Agnes anet per l'enoios:
2 CONT Et fo granz, et ag loncz guinhos:
2 CONT Et eu, can lo vi entre nos,
2 CONT Aig n'espavent,
2 CONT Qu'a pauc no-n perdei la valor e l'ardiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quant aguem begut e manjat,
2 CONT Eu mi despoillei per lor grat;
2 CONT Detras m'aporteron lo gat
2 CONT Mal e felon:
2 CONT La una-l tira del costat tro al tallon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Per la coa de mantenen
2 CONT Tira-l gat, et el escoisen:
2 CONT Plajas mi feron mais de cen
2 CONT Aquella vetz
2 CONT Mas eu no-m mogra ges enquers qi m'ausizetz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pos diz N'Agnes a N'Ermessen:
2 CONT "Mutz es, que ben es conoissen.
2 CONT Sor, del banh nos apareillem
2 CONT E del sojorn."
2 CONT .xli. jorn estei az aquel torn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tant las fotei com auziretz:
2 CONT Cen e quatre vint et ueit vetz,
2 CONT Q'a pauc no-i rompei mos corretz
2 CONT E mos arnes;
2 CONT E no-us pues dir los malaveg tan gran m'en pres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Monet, tu m'iras al mati,
2 CONT Mo vers porteras el borsi
2 CONT Dreg a la molher d'en Guari
2 CONT E d'en Bernat,
2 CONT E diguas lor que per m'amor aucizo-l cat
2 CONT
2 CONT I'll write a verse, since I'm dozing off
2 CONT and I move, though motionless, under the sun.
2 CONT There are malicious women,
2 CONT and I'll tell you which ones:
2 CONT those who take a knight's love badly.
2 CONT
2 CONT A woman is not in mortal sin
2 CONT if she loves a loyal knight;
2 CONT but if she loves a monk or priest,
2 CONT it's evil:
2 CONT she should rightfully burn on a stake.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Auvergne, around Limoges,
2 CONT I was going alone and incognito:
2 CONT I found the wife of Sir Guari
2 CONT and of Sir Bernart;
2 CONT they greeted me simply by the name of Saint Leonard.
2 CONT
2 CONT One told me in her tongue:
2 CONT "God save you, Sir Pilgrim;
2 CONT you certainly look of high status,
2 CONT in my opinion,
2 CONT but we see so many fools going around the world."
2 CONT
2 CONT And hear what I answered;
2 CONT I didn't say "ah" nor "bah"
2 CONT (neither did I mention iron nor wood)
2 CONT but only as much as:
2 CONT "Babariol, babariol, babarian"
2 CONT
2 CONT So Dame Agnes told Dame Ermessen:
2 CONT "We have found what we looking for:
2 CONT sister, by all means, let's host him,
2 CONT since he is dumb,
2 CONT and nobody will know our purpose from him."
2 CONT
2 CONT One took me under her cape
2 CONT and lead me into the room, by the hearth:
2 CONT and know that I appreciated it,
2 CONT and the fire was good
2 CONT and I warmed myself gladly with those large cinders.
2 CONT
2 CONT And they fed me capons
2 CONT and know that I had more than two,
2 CONT and there were neither a cook nor kitchen-boys
2 CONT but only we three;
2 CONT and the bread was white, and the wine good, and the pepper abundant.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sister, if this man is playing it dumb
2 CONT and refrains from speaking for our sake
2 CONT let's bring in our red cat
2 CONT right away:
2 CONT it will make speak immediately, if he deceives us in any way."
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes went fetch the bothersome thing
2 CONT and it was big and had long whiskers
2 CONT and I, as I saw it among us,
2 CONT was so afraid of it
2 CONT that I almost lost heart, and lust.
2 CONT
2 CONT When we had drunk and eaten,
2 CONT I stripped myself naked for their sake.
2 CONT They brought in, from behind, the cat,
2 CONT [which was] mean and treacherous:
2 CONT one spread it from the ribcage down to the heels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suddenly, she pulled the cat by the tail
2 CONT and it scratched:
2 CONT they gave me more than a hundred sores
2 CONT that time
2 CONT but I wouldn't have budged, even if they had killed me.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thereafter Dame Agnes told Dame Ermessen:
2 CONT "He is dumb, it is clear:
2 CONT sister, let's get ready for merryment
2 CONT and pleasure."
2 CONT I lingered 41 days that way.
2 CONT
2 CONT You shall hear how much I fucked them:
2 CONT a hundred and eighty-eight times,
2 CONT so much that they almost broke my equipment
2 CONT and my tool;
2 CONT and I can't describe the aching, so much I was taken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Monet, you shall go in the morning,
2 CONT bringing my verse in your purse,
2 CONT straight to the wife of Ser Guari
2 CONT and of Sir Bernat,
2 CONT and tell them, for the love of me, to kill the cat.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230789827&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230809826&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230694863&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben vuelh que sapchon li pluzor
2 CONT D'un vers, si-s de bona color
2 CONT Qu'ieu ai trag de mon obrador,
2 CONT Qu'ieu port d'ayselh mestier la flor,
2 CONT Et es vertatz,
2 CONT E puesc en trair lo vers auctor,
2 CONT Quant er lassatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eu conosc ben sen e folor,
2 CONT E conosc anta et henor,
2 CONT Et ai ardiment e paor;
2 CONT E si-m partetz un juec d'amor,
2 CONT No suy tan fatz
2 CONT No sapcha triar lo melhor
2 CONT Entre-ls malvatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eu conosc ben selh qui be-m di
2 CONT E selh qui-m vol mal atressi
2 CONT E conosc be selluy qui-m ri;
2 CONT E si-ll pro s'azauton de mi,
2 CONT Conosc assatz:
2 CONT Qu'atressi dei voler lor fi
2 CONT E lor solatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ja ben aya selh qui-m noyri,
2 CONT Que tan bo mestier m'eschari
2 CONT Que anc a negu no-n falhi:
2 CONT Que de jogar sobre coyssi,
2 CONT A totz tocatz
2 CONT Mais en say que nulh mo vezi,
2 CONT Qual que-m vejatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dieus en lau e sanh Jolia:
2 CONT Tant ai apres del joc doussa
2 CONT Que sobre totz n'ai bona ma,
2 CONT E selh qui cosselh me querra
2 CONT No l'er vedatz,
2 CONT Ni us de mi non tornara
2 CONT Descosselhatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qu'ieu ai nom "maistre certa":
2 CONT Ja m'amigu'anueg no m'aura
2 CONT Que no-m vuelh'aver l'endema!
2 CONT Qu'ieu suy d'aquest mestier, so-m va,
2 CONT Tan ensenhatz
2 CONT Que be-n sai gazanhar mon pa
2 CONT En totz mercatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pero no m'auzetz tan guabier
2 CONT Qu'ieu non fos rahuzatz l'autrier,
2 CONT Que jogav'a un joc grossier
2 CONT Que-m fon trop bos el cap primier
2 CONT Tro fo taulatz;
2 CONT Quan gardiey, no m'ac plus mestier:
2 CONT Si-m fon camjatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mas elha-m dis un reprovier:
2 CONT "Don, vostre datz son menudier"
2 CONT "Et ieu revit vos a doblier,
2 CONT Fis-m ieu:qui-m dava Monpeslier
2 CONT Non er laissatz!"
2 CONT E leviey un pauc son taulier
2 CONT Ab ams mos bratz.
2 CONT
2 CONT E quan l'aic levat lo taulier
2 CONT Empeys los datz:
2 CONT E-ill duy foron cairat vallier,
2 CONT E-l terz plombatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT E fi-l ben ferir al taulier,
2 CONT E fon joguatz.
2 CONT
2 CONT I would well like most people to know
2 CONT whether this verse is well crafted.
2 CONT I produce it from my workshop,
2 CONT since I am really the champion of this art
2 CONT and it is true
2 CONT and I bring as a witness this verse itself
2 CONT when it is done.
2 CONT
2 CONT I know wisdom and foolishness well,
2 CONT and I know shame and honour
2 CONT and I have both fear and courage;
2 CONT and if you propose a love-game
2 CONT I am not so fatuous
2 CONT that I can't tell the best one
2 CONT among the mediocre.
2 CONT
2 CONT I know well those who mean well
2 CONT and those who hate me as well
2 CONT and I know who makes merry with me;
2 CONT and if gentlemen enjoy my company,
2 CONT I am quite aware
2 CONT that I have to take care of their comfort
2 CONT and of their amusement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Be blessed the one who took care of my upbringing
2 CONT and taught me such a good occupation
2 CONT which never failed me
2 CONT because when it comes to board-playing
2 CONT
2 CONT I know more than any of my neighbours
2 CONT no matter what you see me trying.
2 CONT
2 CONT I thank god and Saint Julian:
2 CONT I have learned so much about the sweet game
2 CONT that I am more skilled than anybody else,
2 CONT and he who asks for my advice
2 CONT won't be denied it,
2 CONT nor will anyone come back from me
2 CONT with bad advice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since I have for a nickname "Infallible Master":
2 CONT never will my mistress have me one night
2 CONT and not wish to have me the next day!
2 CONT Because in this trade I am (and I freely say it)
2 CONT so expert
2 CONT that I can earn my living
2 CONT in every market.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, I am not so big
2 CONT that I wasn't defeated the other day
2 CONT when I was playing for a big stake
2 CONT and the preliminaries were all in my favour
2 CONT until the game was laid out:
2 CONT when I looked at it, it wasn't of any use anymore,
2 CONT it had changed on me.
2 CONT
2 CONT But she reproached me:
2 CONT "Sir, your dice are small"
2 CONT "And I will double the stakes:"
2 CONT Said I "Even if they gave me Montpellier,
2 CONT I wouldn't resign!"
2 CONT And I lifted her board a little
2 CONT with both arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT And after having lifted the board,
2 CONT I threw the dice,
2 CONT and the first two were good points
2 CONT and the third was loaded.
2 CONT
2 CONT And I had it hit the board well,
2 CONT and it was game.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217230430913&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217231015832&size=large}}
2 CONT Pos vezem de novel florir
2 CONT Pratz, e vergiers reverdezir,
2 CONT Rius e fontanas esclarzir,
2 CONT Auras e vens,
2 CONT Ben deu chascus lo joi jauzir
2 CONT Don es jauzens.
2 CONT
2 CONT D'amor non dei dire mas be.
2 CONT Quar no-n ai ni petit ni re?
2 CONT Quar ben leu plus no m'en cove!
2 CONT Pero leumens
2 CONT Dona gran joi qui be-n mante
2 CONT Los aizimens.
2 CONT
2 CONT A totz jorns m'es pres enaisi
2 CONT C'anc d'aquo c'amei no-m jauzi;
2 CONT Ni o farai, ni anc non ho fi;
2 CONT C'az essiens
2 CONT Fauc maintas ves que-l cor me di:
2 CONT Tot es niens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Per tal n'ai meins de bon saber
2 CONT Quar vueill so que non puesc aver;
2 CONT E si-l reprovers me ditz ver,
2 CONT Sertanamens
2 CONT A bon coratge bon poder,
2 CONT Qui-s ben sufrens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ja no sera nuils hom ben fis
2 CONT Contr'amor, si non l'es aclis,
2 CONT Et als estranhs et als vezis
2 CONT Non es consens,
2 CONT Et a totz sels d'aicels aizis
2 CONT Obediens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obediensa deu portar
2 CONT A motas gens qui vol amar,
2 CONT E cove li que sapcha far
2 CONT Faitz avinens
2 CONT E que-s gart en cort de parlar
2 CONT Vilanamens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Del vers vos dic que mais ne vau
2 CONT Qui be l'enten, e n'a plus lau:
2 CONT Que-ls motz son faitz tug per egau
2 CONT Comunalmens,
2 CONT E-l sonetz, ieu meteus m'en lau,
2 CONT Bos e valens.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Narbona, mas ieu no-i vau,
2 CONT Sia-l prezens
2 CONT Mos vers, e vueill que d'aquest lau
2 CONT Sia guirens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mon Esteve, mas ieu no-i vau,
2 CONT Sia-l prezens
2 CONT Mos vers, e vueill que d'aquest lau
2 CONT Sia guirens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since we see, again, blossoming
2 CONT meadows and greening gardens
2 CONT and clearing rills and fountains,
2 CONT and breezes and winds,
2 CONT everybody should enjoy the joy
2 CONT which makes him joyous.
2 CONT
2 CONT I can't say anything bad about love:
2 CONT why don't I get any of it?
2 CONT Probably because I've had my full share!
2 CONT Still, easily
2 CONT gives great joy the one who keeps
2 CONT to the rules.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has always been like this for me:
2 CONT that I never enjoyed the one I loved;
2 CONT I never will, just as I never have;
2 CONT therefore, it is consciously that,
2 CONT many times, I do what my heart suggest:
2 CONT all is nothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT For this reason I have less pleasure,
2 CONT because I want what I cannot have;
2 CONT but if the adage tells the truth,
2 CONT certainly
2 CONT persistence is success
2 CONT to one who can cope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Never will a man be loyal
2 CONT to love, unless he is submitted to it,
2 CONT and is kind
2 CONT towards both strangers and neighbours
2 CONT and obliging
2 CONT towards everybody in these circles.
2 CONT
2 CONT One must oblige
2 CONT many people, if he wants to love
2 CONT and he ought to have
2 CONT elegant manners
2 CONT and that he refrains, in court, from speaking
2 CONT coarsely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Concerning this verse, I tell you that it acquires more value
2 CONT and praise if one understands it well,
2 CONT because the words are well arranged
2 CONT together
2 CONT and the melody, I boast about it myself,
2 CONT is nice and moving.
2 CONT
2 CONT Let Narbonne (but I won't go there)
2 CONT have my verse
2 CONT and I want it to be the voucher
2 CONT of this praise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Let my Esteve (but I won't go there)
2 CONT have my verse
2 CONT and I want him to be the voucher
2 CONT of this praise.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217232436839&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217231136828&size=large}}
2 CONT Pos de chantar m'es pres talenz,
2 CONT Farai un vers, don sui dolenz:
2 CONT Mais non serai obedienz
2 CONT En Peitau ni en Lemozi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qu'era m'en irai en eisil:
2 CONT En gran paor, en gran peril,
2 CONT En guerra, laisserai mon fil,
2 CONT Faran li mal siei vezi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lo departirs m'es aitan greus
2 CONT Del seignorage de Peitieus
2 CONT En garda lais Folcon d'Angieus
2 CONT Tota la terra e son cozi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Si Folcos d'Angieus no-l socor,
2 CONT E-l reis de cui ieu tenc m'onor,
2 CONT Guerrejar l'an tut li plusor,
2 CONT Felon Gascon et Angevi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Si ben non es savis ni pros,
2 CONT Cant ieu serai partitz de vos,
2 CONT Vias l'auran tornat en jos,
2 CONT Car lo veiran jov'e mesqui.
2 CONT
2 CONT Merce clam a mon conpaignon,
2 CONT S'anc li fi tort, qu'il m'o perdon,
2 CONT Et il prec en Jezu del tron
2 CONT En romans et en son lati.
2 CONT
2 CONT De proez'e de joven fui,
2 CONT Mais ara partem ambedui,
2 CONT Et ieu irai m'en a Cellui
2 CONT On tut peccador troban fi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mout ai estat cuendes e gais,
2 CONT Mas Nostre Seigner no-l vol mais;
2 CONT Ar non puesc plus soffrir lo fais
2 CONT Tant soi aprochatz de la fi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tot ai guerpit cant amar sueill:
2 CONT Cavalaria et orgueill
2 CONT E pos Dieu platz, tot o acueill,
2 CONT E El que-m reteigna ab Si.
2 CONT
2 CONT Totz mos amics prec a la mort,
2 CONT Qu-il vengan tuit e m'onren fort,
2 CONT Qu'eu ai agut joi e deport
2 CONT Loing e pres et e mon aizi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aissi guerpisc joi e deport,
2 CONT E vair e gris e sembeli.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since I feel like singing,
2 CONT I'll write a verse I grieve over:
2 CONT I shall never be a vassal anymore
2 CONT in Poitiers nor in Limoges
2 CONT
2 CONT For now I shall be exiled:
2 CONT in a dreadful fright, in great peril,
2 CONT in war, shall I leave my son,
2 CONT and his neighbours shall turn on him.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is hard for me to abandon
2 CONT the rule of Poitiers
2 CONT I leave Folcon of Angiers as a keeper
2 CONT of the whole country and of his cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT If Folcon of Angiers doesn't aid him
2 CONT (and the king I owe my title to doesn't do likewise)
2 CONT most of them will attack him,
2 CONT those villainous Gascons and Angevins.
2 CONT
2 CONT If he isn't very wise and valiant,
2 CONT as soon as I have left you,
2 CONT they will upturn him,
2 CONT because they will see him young and defenceless.
2 CONT
2 CONT I beg my companion for mercy:
2 CONT if I ever wronged him, let him forgive me,
2 CONT and let him praise lord Jesus in his throne
2 CONT both in the common tongue and in his.
2 CONT
2 CONT I lived with youth and valour,
2 CONT but now they are both gone,
2 CONT and I shall go to the One
2 CONT by whom all sinners find peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have been agreeable and gay,
2 CONT but Our Lord doesn't allow it anymore;
2 CONT now I can't bear the burden anymore,
2 CONT so close I am to the end.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have given up all I loved so much:
2 CONT chivalry and pride;
2 CONT and since it pleases God, I accept it all,
2 CONT that He may keep me by Him.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT I enjoin my friends, upon my death,
2 CONT all to come and do me great honor,
2 CONT since I have held joy and delight
2 CONT far and near, and in my abode.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus I give up joy and delight,
2 CONT and grey squirrel and sable furs.
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://trobar.org/troubadours/
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William IX, Duke of Aquitaine From Wikipedia http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_IX,_Duke_of_Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT William IX (French: Guillaume de Poitiers ; Occitan: Guilhèm de Peitieus) (22 October 1071 – 10 February 1126), called the Troubador, was the Duke of Aquitaine and Gascony and Count of Poitou (as William VII) between 1086 and his 
2 CONC death. He was also one of the leaders of the Crusade of 1101. Though his political and military achievements have certain historical importance, he's best known as the earliest troubadour[1] — a vernacular lyric poet in the Occita
2 CONC n language — whose work survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide] 1 Ducal career  1.1 Early career, 1088–1102 1.2 Conflict with Church and wife, 1102–1118 1.3 Later career, 1118–1126 2 Poetic career 3 See also 4 References  4.1 Notes 4.2 Bibliography 4.3 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Ducal careerWilliam was the son of William VIII of Aquitaine by his third wife, Hildegarde of Burgundy. His birth was a cause of great celebration at the Aquitanian court, but the Church at first considered him illegitimate
2 CONC  because of his father's earlier divorces and his parents' consanguinity. This obliged his father to make a pilgrimage to Rome soon after his birth to seek Papal approval of his third marriage and the young William's legitimacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early career, 1088–1102William inherited the duchy at the age of fifteen upon the death of his father. In 1088, at the age of only sixteen, William married his first wife, Ermengarde, the daughter of Fulk IV of Anjou. She w
2 CONC as reputedly beautiful and well-educated, but also suffered from severe mood-swings, vacillating between vivacity and sullenness. She was considered a nag, and had a habit of retiring in bad temper to a cloister after an argument,
2 CONC  cutting off all contact with the outside world until suddenly making a reappearance at court as if her absence had never occurred. Such behaviour, coupled with her failure to conceive a child, led William to send her back to her 
2 CONC father and have the marriage dissolved (1091).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1094 he remarried to Philippa, the daughter and heiress of William IV of Toulouse. By Philippa, William had two sons and five daughters, including his eventual successor, William X. His second son, Raymond, eventually became th
2 CONC e Prince of Antioch in the Holy Land, and his daughter Agnes married firstly Aimery V of Thouars and then Ramiro II of Aragon, reestablishing dynastic ties with that ruling house.
2 CONT
2 CONT William invited Pope Urban II to spend the Christmas of 1095 at his court. The pope urged him to "take the cross" (i.e. the First Crusade) and leave for the Holy Land, but William was more interested in exploiting the absence on C
2 CONC rusade of Raymond IV of Toulouse, his wife's uncle, to press a claim to Toulouse. He and Philippa did capture Toulouse in 1098, an act for which they were threatened with excommunication. Partly out of a desire to regain favor wit
2 CONC h the religious authorities and partly out of a wish to see the world, William joined the Crusade of 1101, an expedition inspired by the success of the First Crusade in 1099. To finance it, he had to mortgage Toulouse back to Bert
2 CONC rand, the son of Raymond IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT William arrived in the Holy Land in 1101 and stayed there until the following year. His record as a military leader is not very impressive. He fought mostly skirmishes in Anatolia and was frequently defeated. His recklessness led 
2 CONC to his being ambushed on several occasions, with great losses to his own forces. In September 1101, his entire army was destroyed by the Seljuk Turks at Heraclea; William himself barely escaped, and, according to Orderic Vitalis, 
2 CONC he reached Antioch with only six surviving companions.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Conflict with Church and wife, 1102–1118William, like his father and many magnates of the time, had a rocky relationship with the Church. He was excommunicated twice, the first time in 1114 for an alleged infringement of th
2 CONC e Church's tax privileges. His response to this was to demand absolution from Peter, Bishop of Poitiers. As the bishop was at the point of pronouncing the anathema, the duke threatened him with a sword, swearing to kill him if he 
2 CONC did not pronounce absolution. Bishop Peter, surprised, pretended to comply, but when the duke, satisfied, released him, the bishop completed reading the anathema, before calmly presenting his neck and inviting the duke to strike. 
2 CONC According to contemporaries, William hesitated a moment before sheathing his sword and replying, "I don't love you enough to send you to paradise."
2 CONT
2 CONT William was excommunicated a second time for "abducting" the Viscountess Dangereuse (Dangerosa), the wife of his vassal Aimery I de Rochefoucauld, Viscount of Châtellerault. The lady, however, appears to have been a willing party 
2 CONC in the matter. He installed her in the Maubergeonne tower of his castle in Poitiers (leading to her nickname La Maubergeonne), and, as related by William of Malmesbury, even painted a picture of her on his shield.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon returning to Poitiers from Toulouse, Philippa was enraged to discover a rival woman living in her palace. She appealed to her friends at court and to the Church; however, no noble could assist her since William was their feud
2 CONC al overlord, and whilst the Papal legate Giraud (who was bald) complained to William and told him to return Dangereuse to her husband, William's only response was, "Curls will grow on your pate before I part with the Viscountess."
2 CONC  Humiliated, Philippa chose in 1116 to retire to the Abbey of Fontevrault, where she was befriended, ironically, by Ermengarde of Anjou, William's first wife. While in residence she may have had direct conversations or corresponde
2 CONC nce with Countess Adela of Blois, who was in constant contact with Fontevrault from Marcigney abbey. Philippa did not remain there long, however: the abbey records state that she died on the 28 November 1118.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later career, 1118–1126Relations between the Duke and his elder son William also became strained—although it is unlikely that he ever embarked upon a seven-year revolt in order to avenge his mother's mistreatment, as Ralph 
2 CONC of Diceto claimed, only to be captured by his father. Other records flatly contradict such a thing. Ralph claimed that the revolt began in 1113; but at that time, the young William was only thirteen and his father's liaison with D
2 CONC angereuse had not yet begun. Father and son improved their relationship after the marriage of the younger William to Aenor of Châtellerault, Dangereuse's daughter by her husband, in 1121.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was readmitted to the Church around 1120, after making concessions to it. However, he was after 1118 faced with the return of his first wife, Ermengarde, who had, upon the death of Philippa, stormed down from Fontevrault t
2 CONC o the Poitevin court, demanding to be reinstated as the Duchess of Aquitaine—presumably in an attempt to avenge the mistreated Philippa. In October 1119, she suddenly appeared at the Council of Reims being held by Pope Calixtus II
2 CONC  and demanded that the Pope excommunicate William (again), oust Dangereuse from the ducal palace, and restore herself to her rightful place. The Pope "declined to accommodate her"; however, she continued to trouble William for sev
2 CONC eral years afterwards, thereby encouraging him to join the Reconquista efforts underway in Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1120 and 1123 William joined forces with the Kingdoms of Castile and León. Aquitanian troops fought side by side with Castilians in an effort to take Cordoba. During his sojourn in Spain, William was given a rock crystal v
2 CONC ase by a Muslim ally that he later bequeathed to his granddaughter Eleanor. The vase probably originated in Sassanid Persia in the seventh century.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1122, William lost control of Toulouse, Philippa's dower land, to Alfonso Jordan, the son and heir of Raymond IV, who had taken Toulouse after the death of William IV. He did not trouble to reclaim it. He died on 10 February 11
2 CONC 26, aged 55, after suffering a short illness.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Poetic career  William from a 13th-century chansonnier.William's greatest legacy to history was not as a warrior but as a troubadour - a lyric poet employing the Romance vernacular language called Provençal or Occitan. An a
2 CONC nonymous 13th-century vida of William remembers him thus:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Count of Poitiers was one of the most courtly men in the world and one of the greatest deceivers of women. He was a fine knight at arms, liberal in his womanizing, and a fine composer and singer of songs. He traveled much thro
2 CONC ugh the world, seducing women.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the earliest troubadour whose work survives. Eleven of his songs survive (Merwin, 2002). The song traditionally numbered as the eighth (Farai chansoneta nueva) is of dubious attribution, since its style and language are sig
2 CONC nificantly different (Pasero 1973, Bond 1982). Song 5 (Farai un vers, pos mi sonelh) has two significantly different versions in different manuscripts. The songs are attributed to him under his title as Count of Poitou (lo coms de
2 CONC  Peitieus). The topics vary, treating sex, love, women, his own sexual and literary prowess, and feudal politics. His frankness, wit and vivacity caused scandal and won admiration at the same time. He is among the first Romance ve
2 CONC rnacular poets of the Middle Ages, one of the founders of a tradition that would culminate in Dante, Petrarch, and François Villon. Ezra Pound mentions him in Canto VIII:
2 CONT
2 CONT And Poictiers, you know, Guillaume Poictiers, had brought the song up out of Spain with the singers and viels...
2 CONT
2 CONT In Spirit of Romance Pound also calls William IX "the most 'modern' of the troubadours":
2 CONT
2 CONT For any of the later Provençals, i.e., the high-brows, we have to... 'put ourselves into the Twelfth Century' etc. Guillaume, writing a century earlier, is just as much of our age as of his own. —Ezra Pound, cited in Bond 1982, p.
2 CONC  lxxvi William was a man who loved scandal and no doubt enjoyed shocking his audiences. In fact, William granted large donations to the church, perhaps to regain the pope's favour. He also added to the palace of the counts of Poit
2 CONC ou (which had stood since the Merovingian era), later added to by his granddaughter Eleanor of Aquitaine and surviving in Poitiers as the Palace of Justice to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of William's poems, possibly written at the time of his first excommunication, since it implies his son was still a minor, is partly a musing on mortality: Pos de chantar m'es pres talenz (Since I have the desire to sing,/I'll
2 CONC  write a verse for which I'll grieve). It concludes:
2 CONT
2 CONT I have given up all I loved so much: chivalry and pride; and since it pleases God, I accept it all, that He may keep me by Him. I enjoin my friends, upon my death, all to come and do me great honour, since I have held joy and deli
2 CONC ght far and near, and in my abode. Thus I give up joy and delight, and squirrel and grey and sable furs. Orderic Vitalis refers to William composing songs (c. 1102) upon his return from the Crusade of 1101. These might be the firs
2 CONC t "Crusade songs".
2 CONT
