0 @N6000000000945078355@ NOTE Elvira var en spansk grevinne som tilhørte en adelsfamilie i Galicien. Hun var født i 
1 CONT slutten av det 9. århundre.
1 CONT Hun hadde sønnene Sancho, Alonso, Ramiro og Garcia og datteren Ximene.
0 @N6000000002244429900@ NOTE Given Galicia by father, Alfonso III.
0 @N6000000000945071531@ NOTE Konge av Leon 914 - 924. 
1 CONT Ordoño ble konge av Galicien i 910 og ble dessuten valgt til konge av Leon i 914. Han
1 CONT lå stadig i strid med kaliffene av Córdova.
1 CONT Han ble 2. gang gift med Aragonta fra Galicien som ble forstøtt.
1 CONT Ordoño døde i januar 924 i Zamora og ble bisatt i Santa Maria de Leon.
0 @N6000000000945078419@ NOTE Ordoño, ?folkets far?, var konge av Leon i 850. 
1 CONT Han var tapper og måtte kjempe mot baskerne. Dette hindret ham fra å dra fordel av de
1 CONT stridigheter som lammet kalifatet i Córdova
1 CONT Han var gift med Muña.
0 @N6000000002322342085@ NOTE Given Galicia by father, Alfonso III.
0 @N6000000002176683626@ NOTE Given Galicia by father, Alfonso III.
0 @N6000000003051292955@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Ordoño II of León", retrieved 5 Oct 07]
1 CONT Ordoño II (c. 873-924, León) was king of Galicia from 910 and of León from 914 until his death. He was the second son of the King Alfonso III the Great and his wife, Amulina of Navarre (called Jinema after his marriage).
1 CONT
1 CONT His father sent him to Zaragoza to be educated in the court of the Banu Qasi. Upon Alfonso's death in 910, his sons divided the kingdom of Asturias. León went to García, Galicia to Ordoño, and Asturias to Fruela. Asturian primacy 
1 CONC was nevertheless recognised, though Ordoño was of a harder temperament than his brothers. Upon his brother García's death in Zamora in 914, he succeeded him in León.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ordoño continued thereafter the expansion of the Christian polity of his forefathers on two fronts. In the west of his territiories, he sacked Mérida and Évora and forced the Muslim governor of the region to buy his retreat.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the east of his territories, he united with Sancho I Garcés, king of Navarre, against the emir of Córdoba, Abd-ar-Rahman III. The Moors were destroyed at San Esteban de Gormaz (917). Arnedo and Calahorra were taken the next yea
1 CONC r from the Banu Qasi. The reaction of Abd-ar-rahman, however, was not light. In 920, he put to march and army to recover Osma and San Esteban de Gormaz. He crossed into Navarre and destroyed the Christians at Valdejunquera and too
1 CONC k captive the bishops of Tui and Salamanca. Though intending to crush Pamplona itself, he turned around to deal with his immense booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ordoño II?who had come at King Sancho's request?attributed the loss to the absence of the counts of Castile, who had not come at his call. He brought them together at Tejares and had them killed. The Christian counteroffensive was
1 CONC  immediate, occupying La Rioja and incorporating into Navarre Nájera and Viguera.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ordoño married Sancha, daughter of Sancho I of Pamplona. He died in 924 without descendants of age and the final surviving brother of his family, Fruela, the king of Asturias, succeeded him in his two realms, thus reuniting their 
1 CONC father's patrimony.
0 @N6000000003051292943@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Ordoño I of Asturias", retrieved 5 Oct 07]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ordoño I (831 - 27 May 866) became the King of Asturias in 850. He was born in Oviedo, where he spent his early life in the court of Alfonso II. He was probably associated with the crown from an early age.
1 CONT
1 CONT When just nine years old, he moved to Lugo, capital of the province of Galicia, of which his father, Ramiro, had been named governor. There he was educated, even in the military arts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ordoño was named governor of Galicia when his father went off to Vardulias to marry Paterna (his second wife). While away, Alfonso died and Ramiro was proclaimed king. However, Ramiro still away, the impatient nobles elected Count
1 CONC  Nepocian in opposition. Ordoño immediately began to raise an army to assist his father in putting down the usurper. He could not leave his post in Galici to help, however, and his army went unused. When his father finally prevail
1 CONC ed, he confirmed Ordoño in his heretofore provisional position.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 847, at the age of 26, Ordoño married Muniadona, probably a sister of the count of Bierzo. She was to give him six children, including his successor, Alfonso III.
1 CONT
1 CONT On January 1, 850, Ordoño succeeded his father as king. He was the first king of Asturias to ascend the throne without election, he was the heir of his father. His first confrontation was with the Basques, who rebelled with the su
1 CONC pport of the Banu Qasi of Zaragoza. While returning to Oviedo after defeating the rebels, he received news of an impending Moorish assault on Vardulias. Before the Moors could attack, he met them near the Ebro and defeated them. T
1 CONC hese victories meant little in the long run, for the governor of Zaragoza, Musa ibn Musa fortified the city of Albaida (Albelda). In 859, Ordoño defeated him at Albelda and besieged and razed the city.
1 CONT
1 CONT He supported the mozarab rebels, which cost him at Guadacelete in 854. But this fiasco only incited him to consolidate the "Desert of the Duero", the depopulated region between the Asturian cordillera and the river Duero. He direc
1 CONC ted the Repoblación, by which were repopulated the towns of León, Astorga, Tui, and Amaya.
1 CONT
1 CONT He intended to advance against the governor of Tudela and thus control all access to the Navarre and the Basque Country, but the Emirate of Córdoba responded with an invasion of Álava and a sack of the eponymous city. At Bureba, t
1 CONC he Arabs defeated Rodrigo, first count of Castile, and set back the Reconquista for years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ordoño died in Oviedo and was succeeded by his eldest son.
