0 @N6000000002188307420@ NOTE Sancho VI William of Gascony
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho VI William (Basque: Antso Gilen, French: Sanche Guillaume, Gascon: Sans Guilhem, Spanish: Sancho Guillén; died 4 October 1032) was the Duke of Gascony from 1009 to his death. His reign is most notable for the renewal of Gas
1 CONC cons ties with Spain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho was a son of William II Sánchez and Urraca of Navarre and relative of Sancho III of Navarre and he spent a portion of his life at the court of that king in Pamplona. He also partook in the Reconquista. It is possible he eve
1 CONC n submitted Gascony to the suzerainty of Navarre. In 1010, he appeared together with Sancho, Robert II of France, and William V of Aquitaine at Saint-Jean d'Angély. He certainly never paid homage to the king of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1027, he met William V at Blaye and they jointly selected Geoffrey, a Frank, as Archbishop of Bordeaux, which had become the Gascon capital during Sancho's reign. He had given his sister Brisca in marriage to William and when h
1 CONC e died without successors, the Aquitainian duke's children inherited Gascony.
1 CONT
1 CONT His daughter Sancha married Berenguer Ramon I, Count of Barcelona, in 1018.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Zurita, p 71, says 8 January 1025.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Higounet, Charles. Bordeaux pendant le haut moyen age. Bordeaux, 1963.
1 CONT     * Lewis, Archibald R. The Development of Southern French and Catalan Society, 718–1050. University of Texas Press: Austin, 1965.
1 CONT     * Zurita, Gerónimo. Anales de la Corona de Aragón I. Edited by Antonio Ubieto Arteta and Desamparados Pérez Soler. Valencia: 1967.
