0 @N6000000005598859455@ NOTE The history of Portugal up to the end of the eleventh century is indis tinguished from that of the rest of the Spanish peninsula, in spite o f the efforts of some historians to identify that country with ancien t Lusitania as a se
1 CONC parate province.  The name Portucalia, Portus Calio e, Terra portucalensis, is believed to derive from the little seaportt own of Portus Cale, situated on the left bank of the Douro at the mout h of the river, below the ancient Ch
1 CONC ateau de Cale, mentioned by Roman writers.  The name was first applied to the town, then to the surround ing territory, and finally, to the whole country, which in 1095 wassti ll an obscure border fief of the Kingdom of Leon, boun
1 CONC ded on the nort h by Minho, on the sourth of Mondego.  From these furgal beginningsPor tugal arose in four centuries to be the greatest maritime, commercial and co0lonial power in Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT The emergence of Portugal as a distinct political state came as a resu lt of the Christian reconquest of Spain and is somewhat obscured by th e accumulation of legends.  Late in the eleventh century crusadeing kn ights from all pa
1 CONC rt os Europe journeyed to Spain to help drive out th e Moors. About 1089, among them came two nobles from Burgundy, with ag reat number of knights, to the Court of Alfonso VI, King of Castile an d Leon.  They were Raymond, son of 
1 CONC William the Great, Count of Burgund y and Henry, grandson of Robert I Duke of Burgundy ( the instant Teres a's husband ). They became companions at arms of the famed El Cid and rendered such services that they were rewarded by mar
1 CONC riage to the King s daughters; Raymond married Urraca, heiress of Alfonso VI, and Henry of Burgundy married the instant Therese or Mathilde, receiving as her dowry the territory of Portugal, which had been recaptured from theMoo r
1 CONC s, and the title of Count of Portugal.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of her husband, Teresa governed Portugal as regent dur ing the minority of her son and for some time thereafter, but, althoug h she is sometimes called Queen of Portugal, the title of Queen was pr obably accorded h
1 CONC er out of respect to her parentage.  Her rule extende d the territory north of Mondego, but to the south of the river, the M oors still retained control.
1 CONT
1 CONT Therese renewed the struggle begun by her  husband with her half siste r Urraca, Queen of Castile, and after a war lasting from 1116 to 1122 , during which Therese was captured and imprisoned, a treaty of peacew as concluded.  By 
1 CONC lavishing wealth and titles upon Fernando Perez, Cou nt of Trava, however, Therese estranged her son and most of the noble s nad bishops of Portugal, and after her troops were defeated at Saint -Marrede, 24 June 1128, she was depo
1 CONC sed and exiled.
1 CONT
1 CONT Teresa married Henry of Burgundy, who thereupon assumed the title of C ount of Portugal.  At first he ruled the country between the Minho an d the Tagus as a vasall of Alfonso VI, but after that monarch died in1 109, leaving all h
1 CONC is possessions to his legitimate daughter, Urraca,He nry invaded Leon, hoping to add to his own dominions.  He did not succ eed in this, but was victorius  in his wars against the Moors and capt ured many cities which are said to 
1 CONC have belonged to the church before the invasion of the Moors.  He established his residence in Guimares o n the Ave and was recognized as an independent ruler and lord of all P ortugal.  His death occurred in the village of Astorg
1 CONC a during the expe dition against Alfonso the Warrior, King of Aragon, husband of Urraca , Queen of Castile.  It is reported that in addition to Alfonso, Henr y and Teresa had two daughters.
1 CONT
1 CONT (ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theresa%2C_Countess_of_Portugal) Th eresa is referenced as the illegitimate daughter of Alfonso VI and Xim ena Nunia de Guzman.  Teresa, Countess of Portugal, a.k.a. Princess Te resa of León (108
1 CONC 0–November 11, 1130), was an illegitimate daughter o f King Alfonso VI of Castile and León and Jimena Muñoz, daughter of th e Count of Asturias. In 1094, she married Henry of Burgundy while on c rusade in Iberia against the Moors.
1 CONC  The County of Portugal was part o f her dowry, establishing Henry as Count of Portugal.  At first, Henr y was a vassal of his father-in-law, but when Alfonso VI died in 1109 , leaving everything to his daughter Urraca of Castile,
1 CONC  Henry invadedL eón, hoping to add it to his lands. When he died in 1112, Teresa wasle ft to deal with the mess. Although their son Afonso I of Portugal (a.k .a. Afonso Henriques) succeeded to the countship, he was young and wa s 
1 CONC therefore placed under his mother's tutelage. She took on the respon sibility as regent and governed her land that had only recently beenre conquered from the Moors and only as far as the Mondego River.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1116, in an effort to expand the land that would descend to her so n (who later became the first King of Portugal), Teresa fought her hal f-sister and queen, Urraca. They fought again in 1120. In 1121, she wa s besieged and cap
1 CONC tured at Lanhoso. A negotiated peace was coordinate d with aid from the Archbishops of Santiago de Compostela and Braga. T he terms included that Teresa would go free and hold the county of Por tugal as a fief of León.  Teresa tri
1 CONC ed to retain the rule of the count y even after her son's majority. Over the course of five years, she la vished wealth and titles on her lover, Fernando Peres, Count of Trava . These actions estranged her other son (who was the A
1 CONC rchbishop of Bra ga) and the nobles, who were mostly foreign crusaders. By 1128, her so ns and the nobles were fed up. Afonso was named sole ruler. He defeate d Teresa's troops near Guimarães and took her prisoner. She was depose 
1 CONC d and exiled (some sources say to a convent, other say with Fernando P eres). Teresa died in 1130.
