0 @N6000000002187750853@ NOTE Ferdinand I of León
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand I, called the Great (in his time, El Magno) (1017–León, 1065), was the count of Castile from his uncle's death in 1029 and the king of León—through his wife—after defeating his brother-in-law in 1037 until his death in 1
1 CONC 065. He was crowned Emperor of All Hispania in 1056.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand was barely in his teens when García Sanchez, Count of Castile, was assassinated by a party of exiled Castilian noblemen as he was entering the church of St John the Baptist in León, where he had gone to marry Sancha, sis
1 CONC ter of Bermudo III. In his role as feudal overlord, Sancho III of Navarre nominated his second legitimate son Ferdinand, born to the deceased count's sister Munia, as successor, and further arranged for Ferdinand to marry García'a
1 CONC  intended bride, Sancha of León.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his father's death, Ferdinand continued as count of Castile, now recognizing the suzerainty of his brother-in-law Bermudo III, but they fell out and on 4 September 1037 Bermudo was killed in battle with Fernando at Tamarón. Fer
1 CONC dinand took possession of León by right of his wife, who was the heiress presumptive, and the next year had himself formally crowned king of León and Castile. He overran the Moorish section of Galicia, and set up his vassal as cou
1 CONC nt in what is now northern Portugal. With northern Iberia consolidated, Ferdinand, in 1039, proclaimed himself emperor of Hispania. The use of the title was resented by the Emperor Henry III and Pope Victor II in 1055 as implying 
1 CONC a claim to the headship of Christendom and as a usurpation of the Roman Empire. It did not, however, mean more than that the sovereign of León was the chief of the princes of the Iberian peninsula, and that Iberia was independent 
1 CONC of the Holy Roman Empire. Ferdinand's brothers García V of Navarre and Ramiro I of Aragón opposed his power, but were both killed in ensuing battles, leaving Ferdinand preeminent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand died on the feast of Saint John the Baptist, 24 June 1065, in León, with many manifestations of ardent piety, having laid aside his crown and royal mantle, dressed in the robe of a monk and lying on a bier covered with a
1 CONC shes, which was placed before the altar of the church of Saint Isidore. At his death, Ferdinand divided up his kingdom between his three sons: Sancho, who received Castile; Alfonso, who received León; and Garcia, who received Gali
1 CONC cia. His two daughters each received cities: Elvira received Toro and Urraca received Zamora. By giving them his dominion, he wanted them to abide by the split in the kingdom and respect his wishes. However, Sancho (born 1032), be
1 CONC ing the oldest, believed that he deserved more of the kingdom, and therefore sought to gain possession of the divided parts of the kingdom that had been given to his siblings.
1 CONT
1 CONT (note: the Dutch version of Wikipedia says that Ferdinand died on 27 December 1065 ?)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Sancho I Count of Castile
1 CONT 1029-1037 Succeeded by
1 CONT Title Extinct 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Bermudo III King of León
1 CONT 1037-1065 Succeeded by
1 CONT Alfonso VI (in Leon)
1 CONT Sancho II (in Castile)
1 CONT Garcia (in Galicia) 
1 CONT Vacant 
1 CONT Title last held by
1 CONT Bermudo III Emperor of Spain
1 CONT 1056 – 1065 Vacant 
1 CONT Title next held by
1 CONT Alfonso VI of Castile 
0 @N6000000002134168022@ NOTE Ferdinand I or Ferdinand the Great,d. 1065, Spanish king of Castile (1035?65) and León (1037?65). He inherited Castile from his father, Sancho III of Navarre, conquered León, and took parts of Navarre from his brother García. Ferd
1 CONC inand fought successfully against the Moors and reduced to vassalage the Moorish kings of Zaragoza, Badajoz, Seville, and Toledo. At the Council of Coyanza (1050) he confirmed the laws of Alfonso V and introduced church reforms. H
1 CONC e divided his kingdom among his sons: Castile went to Sancho II, León to Alfonso VI, and Galicia to García.
0 @N6000000002176683212@ NOTE After his troops killed his brother Garcia IV, King of Navarre in 1054, he
1 CONT assumed rule of Navarre. On his death, he divided his kingdom amongst his
1 CONT three sons, Sancho, Alfonso, and Garcia. By 1037, the male line of the house
1 CONT of Leon had become extinct, so Ferdinand united the kingdom with his own. The
1 CONT history of Leon is identical with that of Castile,until 1157 when Leon became
1 CONT an independent kingdom. Ferdinand III, King of Castile, inherited the throne
1 CONT of Leon in 1230, and Leon & Castile became as one: Castile.
