0 @N6000000004533159878@ NOTE Notes for Reina Dona Urraca Reina de León y Castilla:
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperatriz de España Urraca (b. 1077-81--d. March 8, 1126, Saldana,Castile [Spain]), queen of Leon and Castile from 1109 to 1126,daughter of Alfonso VI.
1 CONT
1 CONT Urraca became her father's heiress when her brother, Sancho, waskilled at Uclés (1108). She was the widow of Count Raymond ofBurgundy, by whom she had had one son, Alfonso Ramírez (born 1104),the future Alfonso VII. To counterbala
1 CONC nce--it was hoped--the dangersof a female succession during the Almoravid crisis, Urraca's marriageto her second cousin, Alfonso I of Aragon, was arranged (1109). Thismarriage, instead of producing political stability in Urraca'sk
1 CONC ingdom, led to years of anarchy. Urraca and her husband, according tothe marriage settlement, became corulers in each other's lands, andAlfonso thereupon put Aragonese garrisons into many Leonese andCastilian cities. The notion of
1 CONC  an Aragonese-Castilian political unionwas, however, premature, and although Urraca's municipalities tendedto accept the Aragonese king, the magnates were hostile. Civil warbroke out and continued for years, many supporting the cl
1 CONC aims of thechild Alfonso Ramírez to the throne. Matters were further complicatedby the temperamental incompatibility of Urraca and her husband, whosoon quarreled. Urraca was tenacious of her right as proprietary queenand had not l
1 CONC earnt chastity in the polygamous household of her father.Husband and wife quarrelled with the brutality of the age, and came toopen war. Alphonso had the support of one section of the nobles whofound their account in the confusion
1 CONC . Being a much better soldier thanany of his opponents he gained victories at Sepúlveda and Fuente de laCulebra, but his only trustworthy supporters were his Aragonese whowere not numerous enough to keep down Castile and Leon. Pop
1 CONC e PaschalII, moreover, declared their marriage canonically invalid. Theyfinally separated in 1114, though the Aragonese king continued forsome years thereafter to keep his garrisons in Castile and to use theroyal title.
1 CONT
1 CONT Struggles also continued between nobles and municipalities, betweenrival bands of magnates, between the archbishops of Santiago andToledo, and between the former, the bishop Diego Gelmírez, and Urracaherself. Alfonso Ramírez was c
1 CONC rowned by Gelmírez in 1111, and hisreign in Galicia began effectively -- despite Urraca's intermittentbut active opposition--in 1116. Urraca's death in 1126 ended adisastrous episode in the medieval political history of ChristianS
1 CONC pain. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
0 @N6000000005598859496@ NOTE Urraca (b. 1077-81 d. March 8, 1126, Saldana, Castile [Spain]), queeno f Leon and Castile from 1109 to 1126, daughter of Alfonso VI. Urracabe came her father's heiress when her brother, Sancho, was killed at Uclé s (1108). She was
1 CONC  the widow of Count Raymond of Burgundy, by whom she had had one son, Alfonso Ramirez (born 1104), the future Alfonso VII .  To counterbalance, it was hoped the dangers of a female successiond uring the Almoravid crisis, Urraca's 
1 CONC marriage to her second cousin, Al fonso I of Aragon, was arranged (1109). This marriage, instead of prod ucing political stability in Urraca's kingdom, led to years of anarchy . Urraca and her husband, according to the marriage se
1 CONC ttlement became co-rulers in each other's lands, and Alfonso thereupon put Aragonese g arrisons into many Leonese and Castilian cities. The notion of an Arag onese-Castilian political union was, however, premature, and although Ur
1 CONC raca's municipalities tended to accept the Aragonese king, the magna tes were hostile.  Civil war broke out and continued for years, many s upporting the claims of the child Alfonso Ramirez to the throne. Matte rs were further com
1 CONC plicated by the temperamental incompatibility of Ur raca and her husband, who soon quarreled. Urraca was tenacious ofher r ight as proprietary queen and had not learnt chastity in the polygamou s household of her father. Husband a
1 CONC nd wife quarrelled with the brutal ity of the age,and came to open war. Alphonso had the support ofone se ction of the nobles who found their account in the confusion. Being a much better soldier than any of his opponents he gaine
1 CONC d victories at S epúlveda and Fuente de la Culebra, but his only trustworthy supporter s were his Aragonese who were not numerous enough to keep down Castil e and Leon. Pope Paschal II, moreover, declared their marriage canonic al
1 CONC ly invalid. They finally separated in 1114, though the Aragonese kin g continued for some years thereafter to keep his garrisons in Castil e and to use the royal title.  Struggles also continued between noble s and municipalities,
1 CONC  between rival bands of magnates, between thearch bishops of Santiago and Toledo, and between the former, the bishop Die go Gelmirez, and Urraca herself.  Alfonso Ramirez was crowned byGelmir ez in 1111, and his reign in Galicia b
1 CONC egan effectively -- despite Urra ca's intermittent but active opposition--in 1116.  Urraca's death in 1 126 ended a disastrous episode in the medieval political history of Ch ristian Spain. (ref: EncyclopaediaBritannica CD '97).  
1 CONC It is reported that Urraca suceeded her father in 1109, at the time of his death.
