0 @N6000000000437316423@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> of Portugal
0 @N6000000000437351893@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Of Leon
0 @N6000000001783465072@ NOTE King of Leon from 1157 to 1188, second son of Alfonso VII.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite several internal revolts against his rule, Ferdinand's reign was notable for the repopulation of Leonese Extremadura and for the victories he secured farther south against the Almohads in the last 20 years of his reign. Th
1 CONC ese included the capture of Alcántara (1166) and Badajoz (1169). He also gave important support to the new military order of Santiago, founded with his approval in 1170. Ferdinand, who called himself rex hispanorum ("king of the S
1 CONC paniards"), established a temporary tutelage over Castile during the minority of his nephew Alfonso VIII and occupied Segovia and Toledo (1162-66), though Alfonso later reacted violently against Ferdinand. Ferdinand was also frequ
1 CONC ently engaged in hostilities with the nascent Portuguese kingdom but came successfully to the rescue of the Portuguese when the Almohads invested the key city of Santarém (1184).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand II rec'd Leon on his father's division of the kingdom (F. being 2nd son); ruled for 30 years; attempted to impose himself on Castile as regent during minority of nephew Alphonso VIII. He married Urraca of Portugal (d. 11
1 CONC 78) as her first husband in 1160.
1 CONT http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps06/ps06_497.htm
0 @N6000000002187755008@ NOTE Ferdinand II of Leon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand II, king of León (d. 1188), was the son of Alfonso VII of Castile & León and of Berenguela, of the house of the Kings of Aragon & Counts of Barcelona. On the division of the kingdoms which had obeyed his father, he recei
1 CONC ved León, 1157. His reign of thirty years was one of strife marked by no signal success or reverse. He had to contend with his unruly nobles, several of whom he put to death. During the minority of his nephew Alfonso VIII of Casti
1 CONC le he endeavoured to impose himself on the kingdom as regent. On the west he was in more or less constant strife with the Kingdom of Portugal, which had separated from León in 1139. His relations to the Portuguese House of Burgund
1 CONC y must have suffered by his repudiation of his wife Urraca, daughter of King Afonso I of Portugal. Though he took the King of Portugal prisoner in 1169, he made no political use of his success. He extended his dominions southward 
1 CONC in Extremadura at the expense of the Moors. Ferdinand, who died in 1188, left the reputation of a good Knight and hard fighter, but did not display political or organizing faculty.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his only son Alfonso IX.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alfonso VII King of Leon
1 CONT 1157–1188 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alfonso IX 
0 @N6000000002322339495@ NOTE He was the 2nd son of Alfonso II, King of Castile, who was also King of Leon
1 CONT as Alfonso VII. Ferdinand was noted both for his intermittent wars with
1 CONT Castile and Portugal, and for his reorgannization (c1170) of the military
1 CONT order of Saint James of the Sword to drive the Moors from Spain.
0 @N6000000002134159538@ NOTE Ferdinand II, d. 1188, Spanish king of León (1157?88), son and successor of Alfonso VII. He invaded Castile and set up a protectorate during the minority (1158?66) of his nephew Alfonso VIII. He also fought the Moors in Estremadur
1 CONC a. His son Alfonso IX succeeded him
0 @N6000000000437044355@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> King Of Leon
0 @N6000000002240829495@ NOTE He was the 2nd son of Alfonso II, King of Castile, who was also King of Leon
1 CONT as Alfonso VII. Ferdinand was noted both for his intermittent wars with
1 CONT Castile and Portugal, and for his reorgannization (c1170) of the military
1 CONT order of Saint James of the Sword to drive the Moors from Spain.
0 @N6000000002244429310@ NOTE He was the 2nd son of Alfonso II, King of Castile, who was also King of Leon
1 CONT as Alfonso VII. Ferdinand was noted both for his intermittent wars with
1 CONT Castile and Portugal, and for his reorgannization (c1170) of the military
1 CONT order of Saint James of the Sword to drive the Moors from Spain.
