1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT
1 CONT Lincoln Castle, Lincolnshire
1 CONT http://www.camelotintl.com/heritage/castles/linccastle.html
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT This 1st Norman King had to invade England to claim his crown in 1066 at the
1 CONT Battle of Hastings, then on into London where he was crowned King on
1 CONT Christmas Day, 1066, in Westminster Abbey. Harold, Earl of Wessex, was slain
1 CONT at the battle. By 1070, the Norman Conquest was concluded. In 1087, during a
1 CONT campaign against Philip of France, William was fatally injured when his horse
1 CONT fell on him. William I is generally considerated as one of the outstanding
1 CONT figures in western European history.
0 @N6000000001744843059@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1066-1087.
1 CONT
1 CONT "William was the illegitimate son of the Duke of Normandy and a second cousin of Edward the Confessor. When Edward died childless, the English crown was offered to Harold Godwinson, although it had previously been promised to Will
1 CONC iam. Feeling cheated, he led a Norman army across the English Channel and defeated Harold at Hastings in 1066.
1 CONT
1 CONT "William was a powerful man who had grown up fearing for his life and trusting no one. He imposed a harsh rule on the English, introducing the feudal system and listing the nation's wealth in the Domesday Book so that he could tax
1 CONC  it heavily. He was merciless to anyone who stood in his way. But he was also a fine administrator who reintroduced law and order to a land that had been in chaos for centuries. Feared rather than loved, he left the country in ver
1 CONC y good shape when he died in 1087."
1 CONT
1 CONT From Nicholas Best, "The Kings and Queens of England" (Boston: Bullfinch, 1995).
1 CONT
1 CONT The second cousin of Edward the Confessor and upon Edward's bequest, the most justified claimant to the English crown upon Edward's death. Although Harold II had fallen at Hastings, Saxon-English hopes of retaining the throne had 
1 CONC not, and these hopes were placed upon the shoulders of Edgar Aetheling. William was in no mood to tolerate any further delay from what he saw as his rightful crown and in a systematic, devastating, act of attrition he ordered his 
1 CONC troops to burn and kill in the shires of south and south eastern England. The Saxon generals had no moral option but to surrender to such an onslaught. William was duly crowned King of England on Christmas Day 1066 in Westminster 
1 CONC Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was the illegitimate son of Robert the Devil (or the Magnificent), Duke of Normandy, and the daughter of a commoner. Protocol forbade a marriage between the two and thus branded William a bastard. He was not alone in being
1 CONC  the son of his mother, Herleva, and indeed his two half-brothers, Odo and Robert, supported him in single combat at the Battle of Hastings.
1 CONT
1 CONT William's claim to the throne of England and his subsequent invasion were fully supported by noblemen of not only Normandy but also Brittany and Flanders. These allies were as equally well rewarded upon his victory by gifts of lan
1 CONC d and titles. These and all other lands within the English realm were recorded in the famous Domesday Book in 1085.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was not loved by the Saxons and it is not surprising when one considers the freedoms and basic human rights they lost under the Norman rule. There were many rebellions but they were generally poorly co-ordinated and were e
1 CONC asily stamped out by William. Invasions by the Welsh and the Danish were just as smartly defeated and for the 21 years of his reign there was only one master
1 CONT of England, William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was married to Matilda of Flanders and had no less than 10 children; Robert, Richard, Cecily, William, Adeliza, Constance, Adela, Agatha, Matilda and Henry. In a death fit for a warrior king, William died whilst in combat 
1 CONC against the French near the town of Rouen. His body lies in the Normandy town of Caen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: http://www.camelotintl.com/heritage/willconq.htmlRuled 1066-1087.
1 CONT
1 CONT "William was the illegitimate son of the Duke of Normandy and a second cousin of Edward the Confessor. When Edward died childless, the English crown was offered to Harold Godwinson, although it had previously been promised to Will
1 CONC iam. Feeling cheated, he led a Norman army across the English Channel and defeated Harold at Hastings in 1066.
1 CONT
