0 @N6000000003615761513@ NOTE William " The Conqueror " "The Bastard " Duke of Normandy, King of England[Brøderbund WFT Vol. 3, Ed. 1, Tree #0193, Date of Import: Apr 18, 1998]
1 CONT
1 CONT AS DUKE OF NORMANDY HE DESCENDS FROM ROLLO (ROLF) THE VIKING, HIMSELF A DESCENDANT OF THE EARLY KINGS OF HIS NATIVE NORWAY, AND OF SWEDEN AND DENMARK, BACK TO ODIN (WODEN), PRIEST AND KING FROM SCYTHIA (NOW pERSIA), WHO CONQUERED 
1 CONC SCANDINAVIA ABOUT 70 B.C., AND FOSTERED THE IDEA OF HIS OWN DIVINITY.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the first Norman king of England, also duke of Normandy, who has
1 CONT been called one of the first modern kings and is generally regarded as one
1 CONT of the outstanding figures in western European history.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was an illegitimate son and is therefore sometimes called William
1 CONT the Bastard. Upon the death of his father, the Norman nobles, honoring
1 CONT their promise to Robert, accepted William as his successor. Rebellion
1 CONT against the young duke broke out almost immediately, however, and his
1 CONT position did not become secure until 1047 when, with the aid of Henry I,
1 CONT king of France, he won a decisive victory over a rebel force near Caen.
1 CONT
1 CONT During a visit in 1051 to his childless cousin, Edward the Confessor, king
1 CONT of England, William is said to have obtained Edward's agreement that he
1 CONT should succeed to the English throne. In 1053, defying a papal ban,
1 CONT William married a descendant of King Alfred the Great, thereby
1 CONT strengthening his claim to the crown of England. Henry I, fearing the
1 CONT strong bond between Normandy and Flanders resulting from the marriage,
1 CONT attempted in 1054 and again in 1058 to crush the powerful duke, but on
1 CONT both occasions William defeated the French king's forces.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conquest of England
1 CONT
1 CONT About 1064, the powerful English noble, Harold, earl of Wessex, was
1 CONT shipwrecked on the Norman coast and taken prisoner by William. He secured
1 CONT his release by swearing to support William's claim to the English throne.
1 CONT When King Edward died, however, the witenagemot (royal council) elected
1 CONT Harold king. Determined to make good his claim, William secured the
1 CONT sanction of Pope Alexander II (died 1073) for a Norman invasion of
1 CONT England. The duke and his army landed at Pevensey on September 28, 1066.
1 CONT On October 14, the Normans defeated the English forces at the celebrated
1 CONT Battle of Hastings, in which Harold was slain. William then proceeded to
1 CONT London, crushing the resistance he encountered on the way. On Christmas
1 CONT Day he was crowned king of England in Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT The English did not accept foreign rule without a struggle. William met
1 CONT the opposition, which was particularly violent in the north and west, with
1 CONT strong measures; he was responsible for the devastation of great areas of
1 CONT the country, particularly in Yorkshire, where Danish forces had arrived to
1 CONT aid the Saxon rebels. By 1070 the Norman conquest of England was complete.
1 CONT
1 CONT William invaded Scotland in 1072 and forced the Scottish king Malcolm III
1 CONT MacDuncan (died 1093) to pay him homage. During the succeeding years the
1 CONT Conqueror crushed insurrections among his Norman followers, including that
1 CONT incited in 1075 by Ralph de Guader, 1st earl of Norfolk, and Roger
1 CONT Fitzwilliam, earl of Hereford, and a series of uprisings in Normandy led
1 CONT by his eldest son Robert, who later became Robert II, duke of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT His Achievements
1 CONT
1 CONT One feature of William's reign as king was his reorganization of the
1 CONT English feudal and administrative systems. He dissolved the great
1 CONT earldoms, which had enjoyed virtual independence under his Anglo-Saxon
1 CONT predecessors, and distributed the lands confiscated from the English to
1 CONT his trusted Norman followers. He introduced the Continental system of
1 CONT feudalism; by the Oath of Salisbury of 1086 all landlords swore allegiance
1 CONT to William, thus establishing the precedent that a vassal's loyalty to the
1 CONT king overrode his fealty to his immediate lord. The feudal lords were
1 CONT compelled to acknowledge the jurisdiction of the local courts, which
1 CONT William retained along with many other Anglo-Saxon institutions. The
1 CONT ecclesiastical and secular courts were separated, and the power of the
1 CONT papacy in English affairs was greatly curtailed. Another outstanding
1 CONT accomplishment was the economic survey undertaken and incorporated in the Domesday Book in 1086.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1087, during a campaign against King Philip I of France, William burned
1 CONT the town of Mantes (now Mantes-la-Jolie). William's horse fell in the
1 CONT vicinity of Mantes, fatally injuring him.  William was succeeded by his
1 CONT third-born son, William II.
0 @N6000000003635729401@ NOTE [1032] "Ros's Pedigree"
1 CONT
1 CONT "Encyclopedia Brittania 11th ed.", 1911: King of England 1066-1087; King William I; bastard son of Robert the Devil; 5 or 6 daughters
1 CONT
1 CONT WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 2181256 = 229424
1 CONT
1 CONT Survived his father as (William II) Duke of Normandy 1035, conquered Maine 1063, Obtained crown of England by conquest 14, Oct. 1066. Crowned at Westminster Abbey by Ealdred, Archbishop of York, 25 Dec. 1066. He died at the Priory
1 CONC  of St. Gervais, near Rouen and was buried at the Abbey of St. Stephen Caen, which he founded.
1 CONT
1 CONT Most ancesters of Wm I added from GEDCOM file ROYALS.GED downloaded from CompuServe
1 CONT
1 CONT William I or William the Conqueror, 1027?-1087 (r.1066-1087), was the illegitimate son of Robert I, duke of Normandy, and succeeded to the dukedom in 1035. While visiting (1051) England, he was probably named by his cousin Edward 
1 CONC the Confessor as successor to the throne, and in 1064 he extracted a promise of support from Harold, then earl of Wessex. In 1066, hearing that Harold had been crowned king of England, William raised an army and crossed the Channe
1 CONC l. He defeated and slew Harold at Hastings and was crowned king. William immediately built castles and harshly put down the rebellions that broke out; by 1072 the military part of the Norman conquest was virtually complete. He sub
1 CONC stituted foreign prelates for many English bishops, and land titles were redistributed on a feudal basis to his Norman followers. After 1075 he dealt frquently with continental quarrels. Wiliam ordered a survey (1085-1086) of Engl
1 CONC and, the results of which were compiled as the Doomsday Book. He was one of the greatest English monarchs and a pivotal figure in European history. His son Robert II succeeded him in Normandy, while another son, William II or Will
1 CONC iam Rufus succeeded him in England. - Encyclopedia, p. 921
1 CONT
1 CONT Nearly twenty years before the Conquest there was at the court of Count Baldwin of Flanders a very beautiful girl, his sister Matilda. Young Duke William of Normandy fell in love with her at first sight. He did not manfiest his de
1 CONC votion, however, in the usual ways. There was none of the mooning of adolescence, no shy dancing of attendance on the part of the determined Norman.
1 CONT He courted Matilda in a forthright way, making it clear that he intended to marry her, come what may. Matilda met his advances cooly. Her reluctance may have been due to the fact that William's mother had been Herleva, the pretty 
1 CONC daughter of a tanner, and that his father had taken no steps to legitimatize his birth, thereby condemning him for life to the appellation of Bastard ... William's courting of Matilda lasted seven years in all. At the end of that 
1 CONC time, in the year 1047, he became so enraged at her continued refusals that he waited outside a church in the city of Bruges, where she was hearing mass, and attacked her when she emerged. He knocked her down, rolled her in the di
1 CONC rt of the street, tore her fine cloak, upbraided her furiously, and then sprang into the saddle and rode away. Perhaps he shold have adopted this method of courthsip earlier. It produced, at any rate, the desired effect. Matilda g
1 CONC ave in. They were married in William's castle of Angi, and it was a grand occasion. ... William saw to it that Matilda was the first wife of an English king to have the title Queen. .. The consort of a Saxon ruler had been called 
1 CONC "lady companion of the King", but this was not good enough for Matilda. ... When she died in 1083, he mourned her for the rest of his days. He built a great tomb for her at Caen in Normandy, covered with gems and with an epitaph l
1 CONC ettered in gold. - The Conquerors,Thomas B. Costain, pp. 14-16
1 CONT
1 CONT Ordered the creation of the Doomsday book
1 CONT
1 CONT It was surely an instance of divine justice that the Conqueror, while riding through the conflagration, should sustain a fall when his horse stepped on a burning plank. He was carried out of the blazing ruins and back to a monaste
1 CONC ry under the walls of Rouen. Here he lingered for six weeks, suffering greatly and becoming wearker with each day. When it was certain he was going to die he made a verbal will. He left Normandy to Robert, in spite of the eldest s
1 CONC on's mutinous record. Then he looked at William, his favorite, who also was strong and cruel and who was called the Red because of his colring. "As for the kingdom of England", he said, struggling for breath, "I bequeath it to no 
1 CONC one, for it was not bequeathed to me. I acquired it by force and at the cost of blood. I leave it in the hands of God - only wishing that my son William, who has been submissive to me in all things, may obtain it if he please God 
1 CONC and prosper." - The Conquerors,Thomas B. Costain, p. 28
1 CONT
1 CONT ... led the Norman conquest of England and provided stability and firm government in an age of great disorder. The illegitimate son of the Norman duke, Robert I, he interited Normandy in 1035, consolidating his rule in the face of
1 CONC  baronial opposition in about 1042. He successfully faced further rebellions and after 1050 began to take an interest in acquiring land in England - perhamps given some encouragement by the English king Edward the Confessor. After
1 CONC  Edward's death (Jan 5, 1066), Vikings under King Harold III of Norway moved on England, and William soon followed with an invasion force of his own. The new English king, Harold II, defeated the Vikins and confronted the Normans 
1 CONC near Hastings in October 1066. William's forces achieved a decisive victory, and on Christmas Day 1066, William was crowned king of Engalnd, founding the Anglo-Norman monarchy and realm. A highly capable, intelligent, and determin
1 CONC ed man, William I established a strongly personal monarchy. He imposed Norman institutions and personnel both on the state and on the church, and he brought England into the mainstream of continental development. Imposing royal au
1 CONC thority directly on courts and other institutions, William ordered the inquests that resulted in the monumental DOMEDAY BOOK. He revolutionized the social, political, and military structures of England, replacing the English nobil
1 CONC ity with French nobles, thus strengthening his authority, and introducing continental feudalism, a structure in which nobles held land in return for service in the royal army. As the Anglo-Saxon state was Normanized, so too was th
1 CONC e English church: English bishops and abbots were replaced by ntoed churchmen from the Continent, including Lanfranc of Bec, who became (1070) archbishop of Canterbury. The Conqueror had been a great reformer of the church in Norm
1 CONC andy, and he continued this role in England. He established the archbishop of Canterbury as primate of the English church, held reforming councils (which he attended), and exercised not only his rights but also his responsibilitie
1 CONC s over the church. The bishops were among his closest advisors and officials. William I kept a close but cool relationship with the papacy, supporting ecclesiastical reform while carefully maintaining his control over the English 
1 CONC church. He was succeeded as duke of Normandy by his eldest son, Robert II, and as king of England by his sons, first William II, then Henry I.
