1 CONT    William Adelin 
1 CONT    Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester 
1 CONT Stephen 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT William initiated many major changes. In 1085, in order to ascertain the extent of his new dominionans and maximize taxation, William commissioned the compilation of the Domesday Book, a survey of England's productive capacity sim
1 CONC ilar to a modern census. He also ordered many castles, keeps, and mots, among them the Tower of London, to be built across England to ensure that the rebellions by the English people or his own followers would not succeed. His con
1 CONC quest also led to Norman replacing English as the language of the ruling classes, for nearly 300 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The signatures of William I and Matilda (beside the first two large Xs) on the Accord of Winchester from 1072.William is said to have deported some of the Anglo-Saxon landed classes into slavery through Bristol. Many of the latter
1 CONC  ended up in Umayyad Spain and Moorish lands. Ownerships of nearly all land, and titles to religious and public offices in England were given to Normans. Many surviving Anglo-Saxon nobles emmigrated to other European kingdoms.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Death, burial, and succession
1 CONT He died at the age of 60, at the Convent of St Gervais, near Rouen, France, on September 9, 1087 from abdominal injuries received from his saddle pommel when he fell off a horse at the Siege of Mantes. William was buried in the St
1 CONC . Peter's Church in Caen, Normandy. In a most unregal postmortem, his corpulent body would not fit in the stone sarcophagus, and burst after some unsuccessful prodding by the assembled bishops, filling the chapel with a foul smell
1 CONC  and dispersing the mourners. [1]
1 CONT
1 CONT William was succeeded in 1087 as King of England by his younger son William Rufus and as Duke of Normandy by his elder son Robert Curthose. This led to the Rebellion of 1088. His youngest son Henry also became King of England late
1 CONC r, after William II died without a child to succeed him.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Children of William and Matilda
1 CONT Some doubt exists over how many daughters there were. This list includes some entries which are obscure.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Curthose (c. 1054–1134), Duke of Normandy, married Sybil of Conversano, daughter of Geoffrey of Conversano 
1 CONT Adeliza (or Alice) (c. 1055–?), reportedly betrothed to Harold II of England (Her existence is in some doubt.) 
1 CONT Cecilia (or Cecily) (c. 1056–1126), Abbess of Holy Trinity, Caen 
1 CONT William Rufus (1056–1100), King of England 
1 CONT Richard (1057-c. 1081), killed by a stag in New Forest 
1 CONT Adela (c. 1062–1138), married Stephen, Count of Blois 
1 CONT Agatha (c. 1064–c. 1080), betrothed to (1) Harold of Wessex, (2) Alfonso VI of Castile 
1 CONT Constance (c. 1066–1090), married Alan IV Fergent, Duke of Brittany; poisoned, possibly by her own servants 
1 CONT Matilda (very obscure, her existence is in some doubt) 
1 CONT Henry Beauclerc (1068–1135), King of England, married (1) Edith of Scotland, daughter of Malcolm III, King of Scotland, (2) Adeliza of Louvain 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Honours
1 CONT William I was ranked #68 on Michael H. Hart's list of the most influential figures in history.
1 CONT
