1 CONT Westminster Abbey by Ealdred, Archbishop of York, 25 Dec. 1066.  He di edat the
1 CONT Priory of St. Gervais, near Rouen and was buried at the Abbey of St.St ephen
1 CONT Caen, which he founded.
0 @N6000000003494708890@ NOTE The following is from the Grolier Multimedia Encyclopedia Online. Grolier Interactive Inc. <http://gi.grolier.com/encyclopedial>. January 2, 1998:
1 CONT
1 CONT William I, King of England (William the Conqueror) 
1 CONT Duke of Normandy and one of the greatest English kings, William I, b. c.1028, d. Sept. 9, 1087, led the Norman conquest of England and provided stability and firm government in an age of great disorder. The illegitimate son of the
1 CONC  Norman duke Robert I, he inherited Normandy in 1035, consolidating his rule in the face of baronial opposition in about 1042. He successfully faced further rebellions and after 1050 began to take an interest in acquiring land in 
1 CONC England--perhaps given some encouragement by the English king Edward the Confessor. After Edward's death (Jan. 5, 1066), Vikings under King Harold III of Norway moved on England, and William soon followed with an invasion force of
1 CONC  his own. The new English king, Harold II, defeated the Vikings and confronted the Normans near Hastings in October 1066 (see Hastings, Battle of). William's forces achieved a decisive victory, and on Christmas Day 1066, William w
1 CONC as crowned king of England, founding the Anglo-Norman monarchy and realm. 
1 CONT A highly capable, intelligent, and determined man, William I established a strongly personal monarchy. He imposed Norman institutions and personnel both on the state and on the church, and he brought England into the mainstream of
1 CONC  continental development. Imposing royal authority directly on courts and other institutions, William ordered the inquests that resulted in the monumental Domesday Book. He revolutionized the social, political, and military struct
1 CONC ures of England, replacing the English nobility with French nobles, thus strengthening his authority, and introducing continental feudalism, a structure in which nobles held land in return for service in the royal army. 
1 CONT As the Anglo-Saxon state was Normanized, so too was the English church: English bishops and abbots were replaced by noted churchmen from the Continent, including Lanfranc of Bec, who became (1070) archbishop of Canterbury. The Con
1 CONC queror had been a great reformer of the church in Normandy, and he continued this role in England. He established the archbishop of Canterbury as primate of the English church, held reforming councils (which he attended), and exer
1 CONC cised not only his rights but also his responsibilities over the church. The bishops were among his closest advisors and officials. William I kept a close but cool relationship with the papacy, supporting ecclesiastical reform whi
1 CONC le carefully maintaining his control over the English church. He was succeeded as duke of Normandy by his eldest son, Robert II, and as king of England by his sons, first William II, then Henry I. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Author:  James W. Alexander
1 CONT [Eldad_Grannis.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [SPARKMAN DATABASE.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Brøderbund WFT Vol. 19, Ed. 1, Tree #0402, Date of Import: 20 Nov 1999]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Tab.Souv. Gen., France 22, Tab. 32, 48; Dukes of Normandy, France 5,p115-27;
1 CONT Keiser und Koenig Hist., Gen. Hist. 25,pt 1, p 100-01;
1 CONT Burke's Peerage, Eng., P, 1949, pref. p 252.
1 CONT !The Noble Lineage of the Delaware West Family;
1 CONT !TheOxford Illistrated History of the British Monarchy by Cannon andGriffith;
1 CONT
1 CONT    For hundreds of years before 1066, England had been ruled byAnglo-Saxon
1 CONT kings.  When Edward "the Confessor" died in 1066, William of Normandy sawhis chance for a suc cessful invation of England.  He built a fleet,attracted many knights, and obtained the pope' s blessing.  He gainedvictory at the Battl
1 CONC e of Hastings and was crowned King of England onChr istmas of 1066.    During the next few years, William presided over agradual redistribution o f
1 CONT land in England.  Saxon rebellions led to the confiscation of lands,which the Norman Barons r eceived.  As a result of this Norman invation,the English -people today are of both Norman an d Anglo-Saxon extraction.
0 @N6000000001783229745@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke of Normandy 1035-1087 and King of England 1066-1087.
1 CONT Died early in the morning on Thursday, September 9, 1087 at Hermentrube, Near Rouen, France.
1 CONT Interred at the Monastery of St. Stephen, Caen, Normandy which he had founded.
1 CONT http://www.robertsewell.ca/normandy.html#gen10
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the natural son of Robert the 1st (6th Duke of Normandy)  and Herleva (aka Arletta?) de Falaise; he inherited Normandy from his father and went on to conquer England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conquering England
1 CONT
1 CONT William's claim to the English throne was based on his assertion that, in 1051, Edward the Confessor had promised him the throne (he was a distant cousin) and that Harold II - having sworn in 1064 to uphold William's right to succ
1 CONC eed to that throne - was therefore a usurper. Furthermore, William had the support of Emperor Henry IV and papal approval. William took seven months to prepare his invasion force, using some 600 transport ships to carry around 7,0
1 CONC 00 men (including 2,000-3,000 cavalry) across the Channel. On 28 September 1066, with a favourable wind, William landed unopposed at Pevensey and, within a few days, raised fortifications at Hastings. Having defeated an earlier in
1 CONC vasion by the King of Norway at the Battle of Stamford Bridge near York in late September, Harold undertook a forced march south, covering 250 miles in some nine days to meet the new threat, gathering inexperienced reinforcements 
1 CONC to replenish his exhausted veterans as he marched.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the Battle of Senlac (near Hastings) on 14 October, Harold's weary and under-strength army faced William's cavalry (part of the forces brought across the Channel) supported by archers. Despite their exhaustion, Harold's troops 
1 CONC were equal in number (they included the best infantry in Europe equipped with their terrible two-handled battle axes) and they had the battlefield advantage of being based on a ridge above the Norman positions. The first uphill as
1 CONC saults by the Normans failed and a rumour spread that William had been killed; William rode among the ranks raising his helmet to show he was still alive. The battle was close-fought: a chronicler described the Norman counter-atta
1 CONC cks and the Saxon defence as 'one side attacking with all mobility, the other withstanding as though rooted to the soil'. Three of William's horses were killed under him. William skilfully co-ordinated his archers and cavalry, bot
1 CONC h of which the English forces lacked. During a Norman assault, Harold was killed - hit by an arrow and then mowed down by the sword of a mounted knight. Two of his brothers were also killed. The demoralised English forces fled. In
1 CONC  1070, as penance, William had an abbey built on the site of the battle, with the high altar occupying the spot where Harold fell. The ruins of Battle Abbey, and the town of Battle, which grew up around it, remain.
1 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10203.htm#i102022
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT William married  Matilda of Flanders in 1053 at the Cathedral of Notre Dame d'Eu, Normandy
1 CONT
1 CONT William and Mathilda had the following children:
1 CONT Robert (II) Curthose, Duke of Normandy (1054 - 1134)
1 CONT Richard, Duke of Bernay (1055 - 1075, died without issue)
1 CONT Cecilia, Abbess of Holy Trinity, Caen (1056 - 1127)
1 CONT Adeliza, who became a nun (born 1055)
1 CONT William (II) Rufus, King of England from 1087 to 1100 born 1056/60
1 CONT Constance, (1066 - 1090) who married Alan IV, Count of Brittany
1 CONT Adela, (1067 - 1137) who married Stephen, Count of Blois, their son:
1 CONT Stephen, King of England 1135 - 1154
1 CONT Agatha (born about 1064)
1 CONT Matilda
1 CONT Henry (I) Beauclerc
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.robertsewell.ca/normandy.html#gen10
1 CONT
1 CONT Appearance
1 CONT
1 CONT William matured into a tall, thick set man with dark hair, which receeded from his forehead early. His voice was rasping and guttural. William undoubtedly possessed considerable powers of leadership and courage. He was devout and 
1 CONC inspired loyalty in his followers, but could also be ruthless and cruel.
1 CONT
1 CONT William of Malmesbury provides us with a detailed description of the king in his Historia Anglorum:-
1 CONT 'He was of just stature, ordinary corplulence, fierce countenance; his forehead was bare of hair; of such great strength of arm that it was often a matter of surprise, that no one was able to draw his bow, which himself could bend
1 CONC  when his horse was in full gallop; he was majestic whether sitting or standing, although the protuberance of his belly deformed his royal person; of excellent health so that he was never confined with any dangerous disorder, exce
1 CONC pt at the last; so given to the pleasures of the chase, that as I have before said, ejecting the inhabitants, he let a space of many miles grow desolate that, when at liberty from other avocations, he might there pursue his pleasu
1 CONC res.
1 CONT
1 CONT His anxiety for money is the only thing on which he can deservedly be blamed. This he sought all opportunities of scraping together, he cared not how; he would say and do some things and indeed almost anything, unbecoming to such 
1 CONC great majesty, where the hope of money allured him. I have here no excuse whatever to offer, unless it be, as one has said, that of necessity he must fear many, whom many fear.'
1 CONT http://www.englishmonarchs.co.uk/normans.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT The last year of William's life was spent fighting in Normandy, in battle for the Vexin, a much disputed territory, which lay between Normandy and France. Amongst those opposing him was his rebellious eldest son, Robert, nicknamed
1 CONC  Curthose by his father, due to his short legs.
1 CONT On 9th September, 1087, whilst riding through the smouldering ruins of the sacked town of Mantes, in what must have appeared to him as like an act of divine retribution, William was thrown from his horse when it trod on burning as
1 CONC hes and sustained severe abdominal injuries.
1 CONT
1 CONT The King, now aged fifty nine and mortally injured, was carried to the convent of St. Gervais in Rouen, the Norman capital. There he summoned his younger sons, William and Henry, to his deathbed. Robert Curthose remained at the co
1 CONC urt of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT England was bequeathed to his second surviving and favourite son, William Rufus and despite his bitter differences with Robert Curthose, he left Normandy to him. To Henry, the youngest son, later destined to inherit all his domini
1 CONC ons, he left 5,000 silver pounds. He is reported to have ruminated on and repented of his many sins, transgressions and cruelties at the end. He tried to salve his conscience, before preparing to meet his maker and fearing for his
1 CONC  immortal soul, he ordered all the treasure he possessed in Rouen to be given to the church and the poor and forgave his enemies. William the Conqueror died on 9th September, 1087, having ruled England for 21 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was buried in the monastery of St.Stephen at Caen in Normandy, an abbey he had previously founded as an act of repentence for his consanguineous marriage to Matilda of Flanders. The body was broken as it was lowered into t
1 CONC he sepulchre, made too short by the stonemasons and the ceremony was interrupted by a dispossessed knight. A stone slab with a Latin inscription, in the abbey church of Caen today marks the burial place of the first Norman King of
1 CONC  England. His grave has since been desecrated twice, in the course of the French Wars of Religion his bones were scattered across Caen, and during the tumultuous events of the French Revolution, the Conqueror's tomb was again desp
1 CONC oiled.
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.englishmonarchs.co.uk/normans.htm
