0 @N6000000006296745742@ NOTE Matilda of Flanders (c. 1031 - 2 November 1083) was Queen consort </wiki/Queen_consort> of the Kingdom of England </wiki/Kingdom_of_England> and the wife of William I the Conqueror </wiki/William_I_of_England>.
1 CONT She was the daughter of Baldwin V, Count of Flanders </wiki/Baldwin_V,_Count_of_Flanders>, and Adèle </wiki/Adela_of_France,_Countess_of_Flanders> (1000-1078/9), daughter of Robert II of France </wiki/Robert_II_of_France>.
1 CONT At 4'2" (127 cm) tall, Matilda was England's smallest queen, according to the Guinness Book of Records </wiki/Guinness_Book_of_Records>. According to legend, Matilda (or "Maud") told the representative of William, Duke of Normandy
1 CONC  </wiki/William_I_of_England> (later king of England as William the Conqueror), who had come asking for her hand, that she was far too high-born (being descended from King Alfred the Great </wiki/Alfred_the_Great> of England) to c
1 CONC onsider marrying a bastard. When that was repeated to him, William rode from Normandy to Bruges </wiki/Bruges>, found Matilda on her way to church, dragged her off her horse by her long braids, threw her down in the street in fron
1 CONC t of her flabbergasted attendants, and then rode off. Another version of the story states that William rode to Matilda's father's house in Lille, threw her to the ground in her room (again, by the braids), and hit her (or violentl
1 CONC y shook her) before leaving. Naturally Baldwin took offense at this but, before they drew swords, Matilda settled the matter [1] by deciding to marry him, and even a papal ban (on the grounds of consanguinity </wiki/Consanguinity>
1 CONC ) did not dissuade her. They were married in 1053.
1 CONT There were rumours that Matilda had been in love with the English ambassador to Flanders </wiki/Flanders>, a Saxon named Brihtric, who declined her advances. Whatever the truth of the matter, years later when she was acting as Reg
1 CONC ent </wiki/Regent> for William in England, she used her authority to confiscate Brihtric's lands and throw him into prison, where he died.
1 CONT When William was preparing to invade England, Matilda outfitted a ship, the Mora, out of her own money and gave it to him. For many years it was thought that she had some involvement in the creation of the Bayeux Tapestry </wiki/B
1 CONC ayeux_Tapestry> (commonly called La Tapisserie de la Reine Mathilde in French), but historians no longer believe that; it seems to have been commissioned by William's half-brother Odo, Bishop of Bayeux </wiki/Odo_of_Bayeux>, and m
1 CONC ade by English artists in Kent </wiki/Kent>.
1 CONT Matilda bore William eleven children, and he was believed to have been faithful to her, at least up until the time their son Robert rebelled against his father and Matilda sided with Robert against William. After she died, in 1083
1 CONC  at the age of 51, William became tyrannical, and people blamed it on his having lost her. Contrary to the belief that she was buried at St. Stephen's, also called l'Abbaye-aux-Hommes </wiki/Abbaye-aux-Hommes> in Caen </wiki/Caen>
1 CONC , Normandy </wiki/Normandy>, where William was eventually buried, she is intombed at l'Abbaye aux Dames </wiki/Abbaye_aux_Dames>, which is the Sainte-Trinité church, also in Caen. Of particular interest is the 11th century slab, a
1 CONC  sleek black stone decorated with her epitaph, marking her grave at the rear of the church. It is of special note since the grave marker for William was replaced as recently as the beginning of the 19th century. In 1961, their gra
1 CONC ves were opened and their bones measured, proving their physical statures. [2]
1 CONT Some doubt exists over how many daughters there were. This list includes some entries which are obscure.
1 CONT Robert Curthose </wiki/Robert_Curthose> (c. 1054 - 1134), Duke of Normandy, married Sybil of Conversano </w/index.php?title=Sybil_of_Conversano&action=edit&redlink=1>, daughter of Geoffrey of Conversano
1 CONT Adeliza (or Alice) (c. 1055 - ?), reportedly betrothed to Harold II of England </wiki/Harold_II_of_England> (Her existence is in some doubt.)
1 CONT Cecilia </wiki/Cecilia_of_Normandy> (or Cecily) (c. 1056 - 1126), Abbess of Holy Trinity, Caen
1 CONT William Rufus </wiki/William_II_of_England> (1056 - 1100), King of the English
1 CONT Richard, Duke of Bernay </wiki/Richard,_Duke_of_Bernay> (1057 - c. 1081), killed by a stag in New Forest </wiki/New_Forest>
1 CONT Adela </wiki/Adela_of_Blois> (c. 1062 - 1138), married Stephen, Count of Blois </wiki/Stephen,_Count_of_Blois>
1 CONT Agatha </wiki/Agatha_of_Normandy>(c. 1064 - c. 1080), betrothed to (1) Harold of Wessex </wiki/Wessex>, (2) Alfonso VI of Castile </wiki/Alfonso_VI_of_Castile>
1 CONT Constance </wiki/Constance_of_Normandy> (c. 1066 - 1090), married Alan IV Fergent </wiki/Alan_IV,_Duke_of_Brittany>, Duke of Brittany </wiki/Duke_of_Brittany>; poisoned, possibly by her own servants
1 CONT Matilda (very obscure, her existence is in some doubt)
1 CONT Henry Beauclerc </wiki/Henry_I_of_England> (1068-1135), King of England, married (1) Edith of Scotland </wiki/Edith_of_Scotland>, daughter of Malcolm III, King of Scotland </wiki/Malcolm_III_of_Scotland>, (2) Adeliza </wiki/Adeliz
1 CONC a> of Louvain </wiki/Leuven>
1 CONT Gundred </wiki/Gundred> (c. 1063 - 1085), wife of William de Warenne </wiki/William_de_Warenne,_1st_Earl_of_Surrey> (c. 1055 - 1088), was formerly thought of as being yet another of Matilda's daughters, with speculation that she w
1 CONC as William I's full daughter, a stepdaughter, or even a foundling or adopted daughter. However, this connection to William I has now been firmly debunked--see Gundred's discussion page for further information.
1 CONT Matilda was a seventh generation direct descendent of Alfred the Great </wiki/Alfred_the_Great>. Her marriage to William strengthened his claim to the throne. All sovereigns of England, Great Britain and the United Kingdom have be
1 CONC en descended from her, as is the present Queen Elizabeth II </wiki/Elizabeth_II>.
