0 @N6000000000337179162@ NOTE (Research):Robert II Encyclopædia Britannica Article   born c. 1054 died February 1134, Cardiff, Wales   byname Robert Curthose,  French Robert Courteheuse duke of Normandy (1087-1106), a weak-willed and incompetent ruler whose po
1 CONC or record as an administrator of his domain was partly redeemed by his contribution to the First Crusade (1096-99).   The eldest son of William I the Conqueror, Robert was recognized in boyhood as his father's successor in Normand
1 CONC y. Nevertheless he twice rebelled against his father (1077/78 and c. 1082-83) and was in exile in Italy until he returned as duke on his father's death in 1087. He was totally unable to control his rebellious vassals or to establi
1 CONC sh a central authority in Normandy.   In 1091 Robert's younger brother, King William II of England, invaded Normandy and compelled Robert to yield two counties. William attacked again in 1094, and when a peace was made that gave h
1 CONC im control of Normandy in return for money, Robert joined the First Crusade. He fought at Dorylaeum (1097) and was at the capture of Jerusalem (1099). His courageous leadership contributed to the victory at Ascalon (1099).   When 
1 CONC Robert's youngest brother, Henry I, succeeded William as king of England (1100), Robert was in Italy. He hastened back to invade England, with ignominious results, and Henry in turn invaded Normandy (1105 and 1106). Captured in th
1 CONC e Battle of Tinchebrai (Sept. 28, 1106), Robert spent the rest of his life as a prisoner, dying in Cardiff castle.
0 @N6000000000437178463@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Duke Of Normandy
0 @N6000000002187596239@ NOTE Robert Curthose
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II (called Curthose for his short squat appearance) (c. 1054 – February 10, 1134) was a Duke of Normandy. He was the eldest son of William the Conqueror and Matilda of Flanders, an unsuccessful claimant to the throne of Eng
1 CONC land, and a participant in the First Crusade. His reign as Duke is noted for the discord with his brothers in England, eventually leading to the absorption of Normandy as a possession of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT His nickname, Curthose, seems to have been a reference to his height; William of Malmesbury and Orderic Vitalis report that Robert's father, King William, called him brevis-ocrea (short-boot) in derision.
1 CONT
1 CONT His birthdate is usually given as 1054, but may have been in 1051. As a child he was betrothed to Margaret, the heiress of Maine, but she died before they could be wed, and Robert didn't marry until his late forties. In his youth,
1 CONC  he was reported to be courageous and skillful in military exercises. He was, however, also prone to a laziness and weakness of character that discontented nobles and the King of France exploited to stir discord with his father Wi
1 CONC lliam. He was unsatisfied with the share of power alloted to him, and quarreled with his father and brothers fiercely.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1077, he instigated his first insurrection against his father as the result of a prank played by his younger brothers William Rufus and Henry, who had dumped fetid water on him. Robert was enraged, and urged on by his companion
1 CONC s, started a brawl with his brothers that was only interrupted by the incession of their father. Feeling that his dignity was wounded, Robert was further angered when King William failed to punish his brothers. The next day Robert
1 CONC  and his followers attempted to seize the castle of Rouen. The siege failed, but when King William ordered their arrest, Robert and his companions took refuge with Hugh of Chateauneuf-en-Thymerais. They were forced to flee again w
1 CONC hen King William attacked their base at Rémalard.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert fled to his uncle's court in Flanders before plundering the county of the Vexin and causing such mayhem that his father King William allied himself with King Philip I of France to stop his rebellious son. Relations were not
1 CONC  helped when King William discovered that Robert's mother, Queen Matilda, was secretly sending her son money. At a battle in January 1079 Robert unhorsed King William in combat and succeeded in wounding him, stopping his attack on
1 CONC ly when he recognized his father's voice. Humiliated, King William cursed his son, then raised the siege and returned to Rouen.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Easter 1080 father and son were reunited by the efforts of Queen Matilda, and a truce lasted until 1083. Robert seems to have left court soon after the death of his mother, Queen Matilda, and spent several years traveling throu
1 CONC ghout France, Germany and Flanders. He visited Italy seeking the hand of the great heiress Matilda of Tuscany (b. 1046), but was unsuccessful.
1 CONT
1 CONT During this period as a wandering knight, Robert sired several illegitimate children. His illegitimate son, Richard, seems to have spent much of his life at the royal court of his uncle, William Rufus. Like his namesake uncle, thi
1 CONC s Richard was killed in a hunting accident in the New Forest in 1099. An illegitimate daughter was later married to Helias of Saint-Saens.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1087, the Conqueror died of wounds suffered during a riding accident during a siege of Rouen. At his death, he reportedly wanted to disinherit his eldest son, but was persuaded to divide the Norman dominions between his two eld
1 CONC est sons. To Robert, he granted the Duchy of Normandy and to William Rufus he granted the Kingdom of England. The youngest son Henry was given money to buy land. Of the two elder sons, Robert was considered to be much the weaker a
1 CONC nd was generally preferred by the nobles who held lands on both sides of the English Channel, since they could more easily circumvent his authority. At the time of their father's death, the two brothers made an agreement to be eac
1 CONC h other's heir. However, this peace lasted less than a year when barons joined with Robert to displace Rufus in the Rebellion of 1088. It was not a success, in part because Robert never showed up to support the English rebels.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert took as his close advisor Ranulf Flambard, who had been previously a close advisor to his father. Flambard later became an astute but much-disliked financial advisor to William Rufus until the latter's death 1100.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1096, Robert left for the Holy Land on the First Crusade. At the time of his departure he was reportedly so poor that he often had to stay in bed for lack of clothes. In order to raise money for the crusade, he mortaged his duc
1 CONC hy to his brother William for the sum of 10,000 marks.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert had agreed with William II to name each other the Heir Presumptive of England and Normandy respectively. When William II died on August 2, 1100, Robert should have inherited the throne of England. But he was on his return j
1 CONC ourney from the Crusade, marrying a wealthy young bride to raise funds to buy back his duchy. His dilatoriness in returning from the First Crusade, coupled with luck (or murder) on Henry's part, allowed the youngest brother Henry 
1 CONC to seize the crown of England for himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his return, Robert, urged by Flambard and several Anglo-Norman barons, led an invasion of England to retake the crown from his brother Henry. In 1101, Robert landed at Portsmouth with his army, but his lack of popular support
1 CONC  among the English as well as Robert's own mishandling of the invasion tactics enabled Henry to resist the invasion. Robert was forced by diplomacy to renounce his claim to the English throne in the Treaty of Alton. It is said tha
1 CONC t Robert was a brilliant field commander, but a terrible general in the First Crusade. His government (or misgovernment) of Normandy as well as his failed invasion of England proves that his military skills were better than his po
1 CONC litical skills.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1105, however, Robert's continual stirring of discord with his brother in England, as well as civil disorder in Normandy itself, prompted Henry to invade Normandy. Orderic reports on an incident at Easter 1105, when Robert was 
1 CONC supposed to hear a sermon by the venerable Serlo, Bishop of Sées. Robert spent the night before sporting with harlots and jesters, and while he lay in bed, sleeping off his drunkenness, his unworthy friends stole his clothes. He a
1 CONC woke to find himself naked, and had to remain in bed and missed the sermon.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1106, Henry defeated Robert's army decisively at the Battle of Tinchebray and claimed Normandy as a possession of the English crown, a situation that endured for almost a century. Captured after the battle, Robert was imprisone
1 CONC d in Devizes castle for 20 years, before being moved to Cardiff.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1134, he died while imprisoned in Cardiff Castle in his early eighties. Robert Curthose, sometime Duke of Normandy, eldest son of the Conqueror, was buried in the abbey church of St. Peter in Gloucester, where an elaborate sepu
1 CONC lchre was later built. The church subsequently has become Gloucester Cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name 'Curthose' can still be seen today, in France as Courtoise and in Britain as Curthoys.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert married Sybilla, daughter of Geoffrey of Brindisi, Count of Conversano (and a grandniece of Robert Guiscard) on the way back from Crusade. Their son, William Clito, was born October 25, 1102 and became heir to the Duchy of 
1 CONC Normandy. Sybilla, who was admired and often praised by the chroniclers of the time, died shortly after the birth. William of Malmesbury claims she died as a result of binding her breasts too tightly; both Robert of Torigny and Or
1 CONC deric Vitalis suggest she was murdered by a cabal of noblewomen led by her husband's mistress, Agnes Giffard. William Clito was unlucky all his life; his attempts to invade Normandy failed twice (1119) and (1125), his first marria
1 CONC ge to a daughter of the count of Anjou was annulled by his uncle's machinations, and even his late inheritance of the county of Flanders was mishandled. William Clito died in 1128 leaving no issue, thus leaving the field clear in 
1 CONC the Norman succession (at least until Henry I died).
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert also had at least two illegitimate children - Richard who died hunting in the New Forest in 1099 (like his uncle a year later), and an unnamed daughter who married Helias of Saint-Saens (a worthy and loyal protector of his 
1 CONC young brother-in-law in 1112).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William I of England Duke of Normandy
1 CONT 1087–1105 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry I of England
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Barlow, Frank. William Rufus, 2000
1 CONT     * David, Charles Wendell. Robert Curthose, Duke of Normandy. Cambridge, Mass. : Harvard University, 1920.
1 CONT     * Green, Judith. "Robert Curthose Reassessed". Anglo-Norman Studies, 22 (1999).
1 CONT     * Mooers, Stephanie L. " 'Backers and Stabbers' : Problems of Loyalty in Robert Curthose's Entourage". Journal of British Studies, 21.1 (1981): 1-17.
0 @N6000000001744969929@ NOTE According to custom, Robert inherited Normandy upon his father's death in 1087, while his younger brother William inherited the conquered lands (England). The two of them quarreled over the spoils until 1096, when Robert pawned No
1 CONC rmandy to William and went on the crusade to the Holy Land.
1 CONT
1 CONT Then his younger brother Henry inherited the crown in 1100, but Robert challenged himfor it. They fought at the Battle of Tinchebrai in 1106, where Henry defeated Robert and held him prisoner for the rest of his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Nicholas Best, "The Kings and Queens of England" (Boston: Bullfinch, 1995).
