0 @N6000000003615400627@ NOTE Robert II, called Courteheuse \kuer-toez\ . 1054? -1134. Duke (1087-1134). Eldest son of William the Conqueror. Rebelled against his father (1077, 1080, 1082). Disputed Normandy with his brother William II of England (1089, 1091, 
1 CONC 1094); incompetent as ruler, failed to control rebellious vassals or establish a central authority. Took important part in First Crusade (1096-99), distinguishing himself at battles of Dorylaeum (1097), Jerusalem (1099), and Ascal
1 CONC on (1099); delayed in Italy on his return (1100). Invaded England (1101) in attempt to take throne from his younger brother, Henry I; retired to Normandy; at war again with Henry (1105-06); defeated at Tinchebrai (1106); imprisone
1 CONC d at Cardiff (1106-34).
1 CONT
1 CONT Funk and Wagnall's Encyclopedia
0 @N6000000005598882159@ NOTE As his father prepared for the invasion of England during the early mo nths of 1066, Robert was established as Williams heir.  By 1066, with his mother, Robert was left in control of the government of Normandie , under the guidanc
1 CONC e of his mother.  During the entire period of the c onquest, Robert remained in Normandie.  By the time that his mother tr aveled to England in 1068, Richard was left as regent in Normandie.  B y 1075, Robert began to feel exclude
1 CONC d from his fathers plans and by 10 77, Robert wished for direct rule of the duchy of Normandie.  His brot hers would taunt him at Laigle where they often occupied the same lodg ings.  In 1077, his brothers held a dice party and af
1 CONC ter Robert had go ne to bed, the two younger brothers urinated on him and his military a ides from an upper floor.  William the father was eventually forced t o break up the brothers.  Robert and his brothers rejected the stiflin 
1 CONC g piety of their parents and Robert took on the spendthrift and vagabo nd culture of camp life.  His father tried to reason with him, but Rob ert stormed away and attempted to sieze Rouen.  Failing, he and his fo llowers fled to t
1 CONC he frontier at Chateauneuf-en-Thimerais and self-impo sed exile.  Eventually he sought the aide of Robert - Count of Flander s.  He was treated warmly by Robert, but his action also opened doors for Curthose with the French Royal 
1 CONC court.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1078, Robert had obtained Phillipe I's suport in removing the Norma n influence from the Vexin.  But, on the verge of success, he was bloc ked by his father William at the Norman fortress of Gournay.  He becam e a great gift to
1 CONC  French King Philip as he became a thorn in his fathe rs side.  At this time, his younger brothers William and Henry began t o irratate him even more.  Robert and his father eventually broke in s uch a manner that open warfare eru
1 CONC pted.  Robert was supported by Willi am de Breteuil and Robert De Belleme.  William was able to dislodge hi s son Robert from the southern frontier of Normandie.  At the time, Ro bert appealed to his uncle, the Count of Flanders a
1 CONC nd King Philip of F rance established Robert as Marcher of the castle of Gerbevay.  King P hilip moved in and William (the Duc of Normandie) was forced to withdr aw.  But, by 1079, William (Duc of Normandie) had defeated his son a
1 CONC n d his allies at the battle of Gerbray.  In April of 1080, Robert and h is father were reunited in a spirit of cooperation and together crosse d to England.  Later that year, Robert, with his uncle Eudes (Odo) le d a Norman force
1 CONC  north to intimidate King Malcolm and Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT When, the Conqueror died, his bequests left Normandie to his son Rober t, England went to his son William Rufus and his son Henry received a n 'incalculable treasure' of 5000 lbs.  After the Conqueror's death, N ormandie suffered 
1 CONC a deluge of lawlessness and violence under Curthose .  At the time of the Conquerors death in 1087, Robert was encamped he ar Abbeville in Ponthieu, engaged in rebellion against his father for the second time.  At that time, Rober
1 CONC t was styled as an easy going, se lf indulgent individual.  His father's wish was that Robert would rece ive Normandie at the time of his death.  He coveted England and as ear ly as 1088 organized an expedition to conquer it.  In 
1 CONC March of 1088, O do of Bayeux and Robert of Mortain organized a rebellion in England t o conincide with an invasion by Robert.  Curthose asked his younger br other Henry for support, which initially Henry refused, but ultimatel y 
1 CONC gave Robert 3,000 lbs. in exchange for territories in western Norman die.  A fleet was assembled to support Odo, but it was decimated by a n English fleet and many of Curthose's Knights were killed during the encounter.  Odo was f
1 CONC orced to abandon his earldom in Kent and had to f lee England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rufus was still unforgiving regarding Curthose's attempt to claim the English crown.  Hostilities broke out between Henry and Curthose on 3 November 1090 near Rouen castle.  At the battle both Curthose and Rufu s were present, but
1 CONC  when the battle was in doubt, Curthose escaped to Emendreville.  Henry remained in the city with his knights and ultimat ely gained the upper hand and the eventual victory over Curthose's for ces led by Conan.  Eventually a treat
1 CONC y was arranged between Curthose a nd Rufus that completely excluded Henry.  William Rufus received the a bbey of FeCamp, the county of Eu, the city of Cherbourg and various ot her castles in Normandie.  Curthose would receive Rufu
1 CONC s' support and a ssistance in re-establishing his rule over Maine as well as various la nds in England.  But Rufus never conceded the English lands.  The trea ty also disinherited Henry as long as either Rufus or Curthose were al 
1 CONC ive.  Together, Rufus and Curthose led their armies west to take weste rn Normandie back from Henry.  Henry soon withdrew to his castle at Mo n-St-Michel, but was allowed to escape when Curthose refused to let hi m starve.  This a
1 CONC ction outraged Rufus.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Rufus came to give his consent for Henrys re-establishement i n western Normandie and by 1095 Rufus gave Henry generous financial ba cking in his campaign against Curthouse.  Curthouse continued to  harb or a grudge agains
1 CONC t Rufus for his violation of the treaty of 1091.  B y 1096, Curthose had fallen on hard times financially and his younger brother Henry began to harras his territories.  In September 1096, Cur those's brother William Rufus conveye
1 CONC d 10,000 marks to Curthose in Nor mandie and Curthose set out for Constantinople and the Hold Land for t he Crusade that had been called the previous November of 1095.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1101, Curthose had temporarily regained the confidence of the Norma n aristocracy including the families of Laigle, Montfort, Grandmesnil and Breteuil who were all hostile to their neighbors the Beaumont brot hers who supported
1 CONC  Henry I.  Curthose mobilized a fleet for the invasi on of England as early as April of 1101.  In July of 1101, Curthose la nded with a force near Portsmouth and moved on Pevensey.  He had a muc h smaller force than his father had
1 CONC  had when he invaded England in 106 6.  At Portsmouth, with his allies William de Warenne and Robert of Be lleme and 260 knights, he was very well placed to move on Winchester a nd the Royal Treasury, but instead of moving on Winc
1 CONC hester, he encampe d and waited on Henry's army to move against him.  Henry had marched w est along the South Downs down into Hampshire.  The two armies met nea r Alton in Hampshire.  Neither party was avidly disireous of a battle
1 CONC  and petitions were traded.  Robert Flambard was in charge of Curthose s negotiation party and quickly made amends to regain Henry's favor. Curthose actualy deserves credit for the the peace as he proved too am iable to pursue war
1 CONC  to the death against any opponent other than a Mus lim.  He accepted a payoff of 2,000 lbs annually to surrender his clai m to England.  Subsequently, Curthose renounced his claim to the Engli sh throne. in the Treaty of Alton, r
1 CONC atified at Wincester on 2 August 1 101.  Curthose returned to Normandie,  in September 1101, accompanied by William de Warenne.  Eventually, Curthose remitted the 2,000 lbs t o his sister-in-law Matilda as a wedding present.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of his wife Sybilla, Robert had a short affair with Ag nes de Ribemont, widow of Walter Giffard.  Robert tried to forestall, his younger brother Henry's ambitions in Normandie by granting Henry t he allegiance of W
1 CONC illiam of Evreux, but Henry returned to England in t he late autumn of 1104.  In 1106, Richard was captured by his brother Henry after the battle of Tinchebray and eventually moved to England w here his captivity was comfortable. 
1 CONC  After 1106, Richard was forced t o relinquish his rights as Duc or Normandie.  He however was never lef t unsupervised.  In about September of 1126, Curthose was moved from D evizes castle to Robert of Gloucesters castle at Brist
1 CONC ol.  Richard wa s blinded by his younger brother Henry.
0 @N6000000000337179145@ NOTE (Research):William II Encyclopædia Britannica Article    born c. 1056 died Aug. 2, 1100, near Lyndhurst, Hampshire, Eng.   byname William Rufus,  French Guillaume Le Roux son of William I the Conqueror and king of England from 108
1 CONC 7 to 1100; he was also de facto duke of Normandy (as William III) from 1096 to 1100. He prevented the dissolution of political ties between England and Normandy, but his strong-armed rule earned him a reputation as a brutal, corru
1 CONC pt tyrant. Rufus ("the Red"_so named for his ruddy complexion) was William's third (second surviving) and favourite son. In accordance with feudal custom, William I bequeathed his inheritance, the Duchy of Normandy, to his eldest 
1 CONC son, Robert II Curthose; England, William's kingdom by conquest, was given to Rufus.   Nevertheless, many Norman barons in England wanted England and Normandy to remain under one ruler, and shortly after Rufus succeeded to the thr
1 CONC one, they conspired to overthrow him in favour of Robert. Led by the Conqueror's half brother, Odo of Bayeux, Earl of Kent, they raised rebellions in eastern England in 1088. Rufus immediately won the native English to his side by
1 CONC  pledging to cut taxes and institute efficient government. The insurgency was suppressed, but the king failed to keep his promises. Consequently, a second baronial revolt, led by Robert de Mowbray, Earl of Northumberland, broke ou
1 CONC t in 1095. This time William punished the ringleaders with such brutality that no barons dared to challenge his authority thereafter. His attempts to undermine the authority of the English church provoked resistance from St. Ansel
1 CONC m, archbishop of Canterbury, who, defeated, left the country for Rome in 1097; Rufus immediately seized the lands of Canterbury.   Meanwhile, Rufus was engaged in military operations in Scotland, Wales, and particularly in Normand
1 CONC y. In 1091 he compelled King Malcolm III of Scotland to acknowledge his overlordship. Malcolm revolted in November 1093, but Rufus' forces quickly killed him near Alnwick, Northumberland. Thereafter, Rufus maintained the Scottish 
1 CONC kings as vassals, and in 1097 he subjugated Wales. William Rufus' chief interest, however, lay in the recovery of Normandy from the incompetent Robert. After waging war on Normandy for seven years (1089-96), Rufus reduced his brot
1 CONC her to the role of a subordinate ally. When Robert left for a crusade in 1096, he mortgaged his kingdom to Rufus, who quickly added Maine to his possessions. In 1100 Rufus was shot in the back with an arrow and killed while huntin
1 CONC g in the New Forest in Hampshire. The incident was probably an assassination, and Rufus' alleged slayer, Walter Tirel, lord of Poix in Ponthieu, may have been acting under orders from the king's younger brother, Henry. Henry promp
1 CONC tly seized the English throne as King Henry I.
