1 CONT
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta.1993,
1 CONT
1 CONT called Rufus (1056?-1100), king of England (1087-1100), who extended his power into Normandy and Scotland. He was the third son of William the Conqueror. He was crowned in Westminster Abbey in 1087.
1 CONT
1 CONT William invaded Normandy in 1089, 1091, and 1094, winning some concessions from his brother Robert II, duke of Normandy, each time. He forced the Scottish king Malcolm III MacDuncan (1031?-93) to pay him homage and in 1092 seized 
1 CONC the city of Carlisle and other areas claimed by Malcolm in Cumberland and Westmorland. In 1096 Robert mortgaged Normandy to William for funds to finance a Crusade. William then fought to recapture lands his brother had lost as duk
1 CONC e of Normandy and returned the county of Maine to the rule of the duchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was killed on August 2, 1100, while on a hunting trip in the New Forest in Hampshire. It is not known whether the slaying, which is traditionally ascribed to a Norman named Walter Tirel (died after 1100), was accidental or
1 CONC  intentional. William was buried at Winchester; he never married and had no children. His younger brother succeeded to the throne as King Henry I.
0 @N6000000004286199047@ NOTE
1 CONT THE DEATH OF THE RED KING
1 CONT [77] WILLIAM OF NORMANDY, by the grace of God and iron mace, had made himself king of England. An iron king he proved, savage, ruthless, the descendant of a few generations of pirate Norsemen, and himself a pirate in blood and tem
1 CONC per. England strained uneasily under the harsh rein which he placed upon it, and he harried the country mercilessly, turning a great area of fertile land into a desert. That he might have a hunting-park near the royal palace, he l
1 CONC aid waste all the land that lay between Winchester and the sea, planting there, in place of the homes destroyed and families driven out, what became known as the "New Forest." Nothing angered the English more than this ruthless ac
1 CONC t. A law had been passed that any one caught killing a deer in William's new hunting-grounds should have his eyes put out. Men prayed for retribution. It came. The New Forest proved fatal to the race of the Conqueror. In 1081 his 
1 CONC oldest son Richard mortally wounded himself within its precincts. In May of the year 1100 his grandson Richard, son of Duke Robert, was killed there by a stray arrow. And, as if to emphasize more strongly this work of retribution,
1 CONC  two months afterwards William Rufus, the Red King, the son of [78] the Conqueror, was slain in the same manner within its leafy shades.
1 CONT William Rufus-William II. of England-was, like all his Norman ancestors, fond of the chase. When there were no men to be killed, these fierce old dukes and kings solaced themselves with the slaughter of beasts. In early summer of 
1 CONC the year 1100 the Red King was at Winchester Castle, on the skirts of the New Forest. Thence he rode to Malwood-Keep, a favorite hunting-lodge in the forest. Boon companions were with him, numbers of them, one of them a French kni
1 CONC ght named Sir Walter Tyrrell, the king's favorite. Here the days were spent in the delights of the chase, the nights in feasting and carousing, and all went merrily.
1 CONT Around them spread far and wide the umbrageous lanes and alleys of the New Forest, trees of every variety, oaks in greatest number, crowding the soil. As yet there were no trees of mighty girth. The forest was young. Few of its tr
1 CONC ees had more than a quarter-century of growth, except where more ancient woodland had been included. The place was solitary, tenanted only by the deer which had replaced man upon its soil, and by smaller creatures of wing and fur.
1 CONC  Rarely a human foot trod there, save when the king's hunting retinue swept through its verdant aisles and woke its solitary depths with the cheerful notes of the hunting-horn. The savage laws of the Conqueror kept all others but 
1 CONC the most daring poachers from its aisles.
1 CONT Such was the stage set for the tragedy which we [79] have to relate. The story goes that rough jests passed at Malwood-Keep between Tyrrell and the king, ending in anger, as jests are apt to. William boasted that he would carry an
1 CONC  army through France to the Alps. Tyrrell, heated with wine, answered that he might find France a net easier to enter than to escape from. The hearers remembered these bitter words afterwards.
1 CONT On the night before the fatal day it is said that cries of terror came from the king's bedchamber. The attendants rushed thither, only to find that the monarch had been the victim of nightmare. When morning came he laughed the inc
1 CONC ident to scorn, saying that dreams were fit to scare only old women and children. His companions were not so easily satisfied. Those were days when all men's souls were open to omens good and bad. They earnestly advised him not to
1 CONC  hunt that day. William jested at their fears, vowed that no dream should scare him from the chase, yet, uneasy at heart, perhaps, let the hours pass without calling for his horse. Midday came. Dinner was served. William ate and d
1 CONC rank with unusual freedom. Wine warmed his blood and drove off his clinging doubts. He rose from the table and ordered his horse to be brought. The day was young enough still to strike a deer, he said.
1 CONT The king was in high spirits. He joked freely with his guests as he mounted his horse and prepared for the chase. As he sat in his saddle a woodman presented him six new arrows. He examined [80] them, declared that they were well-
1 CONC made and proper shafts, and put four of them in his quiver, handing the other two to Walter Tyrrell.
1 CONT "These are for you," he said. "Good marksmen should have good arms."
1 CONT Tyrrell took them, thanked William for the gift, and the hunting-party was about to start, when there appeared a monk who asked to speak with the king.
1 CONT "I come from the convent of St. Peter, at Gloucester," he said. "The abbot bids me give a message to your majesty."
1 CONT "Abbot Serlon; a good Norman he," said the king. "What would he say?"
1 CONT "Your majesty," said the monk, with great humility, "he bids me state that one of his monks has dreamed a dream of evil omen. He deems the king should know it."
1 CONT "A dream!" declared the king. "Has he sent you hither to carry shadows? Well, tell me your dream. Time presses."
1 CONT "The dream was this. The monk, in his sleep, saw Jesus Christ sitting on a throne, and at his feet kneeled a woman, who supplicated him in these words: ‘Saviour of the human race, look down with pity on thy people groaning under t
1 CONC he yoke of William.'"
1 CONT The king greeted this message with a loud laugh.
1 CONT "Do they take me for an Englishman, with their dreams?" he asked. "Do they fancy that I am fool enough to give up my plans because a monk [81] dreams or an old woman sneezes? Go, tell your abbot I have heard his story. Come, Walte
1 CONC r de Poiz, to horse!"
1 CONT The train swept away, leaving the monkish messenger alone, the king's disdainful laugh still in his ears. With William were his brother Henry, long at odds with him, now reconciled, William de Bretnil, and several other nobles. Qu
1 CONC ickly they vanished among the thickly clustering trees, and soon broke up into small groups, each of which took its own route through the forest. Walter Tyrrell alone remained with the king, their dogs hunting together.
1 CONT That was the last that was seen of William, the Red King, alive. When the hunters returned he was not with them. Tyrrell, too, was missing. What had become of them? Search was made, but neither could be found, and doubt and troubl
1 CONC e of soul pervaded Malwood-Keep.
1 CONT The shades of night were fast gathering when a poor charcoal-burner, passing with his cart through the forest, came upon a dead body stretched bleeding upon the grass. An arrow had pierced its breast. Lifting it into his cart, wra
1 CONC pped in old linen, he jogged slowly onward, the blood still dripping and staining the ground as he passed. Not till he reached the hunting-lodge did he discover that it was the corpse of a king he had found in the forest depths. T
1 CONC he dead body was that of William II. of England.
1 CONT Tyrrell had disappeared. In vain they sought [82] him. He was nowhere to be found. Suspicion rested on him. He had murdered the king, men said, and fled the land.
1 CONT Mystery has ever since shrouded the death of the Red King. Tyrrell lived to tell his tale. It was probably a true one, though many doubted it. The Frenchman had quarrelled with the king, men said, and had murdered him from revenge
1 CONC . Just why he should have murdered so powerful a friend and patron, for a taunt passed in jest, was far from evident.
1 CONT Tyrrell's story is as follows: He and the king had taken their stations, opposite one another, waiting the work of the woodsmen who were beating up the game. Each had an arrow in his cross-bow, his finger on the trigger, eagerly l
1 CONC istening for the distant sounds which would indicate the coming of game. As they stood thus intent, a large stag suddenly broke from the bushes and sprang into the space between them.
1 CONT William drew, but the bow-string broke in his hand. The stag, startled at the sound, stood confused, looking suspiciously around. The king signed to Tyrrell to shoot, but the latter, for some reason, did not obey. William grew imp
1 CONC atient, and called out,-
1 CONT "Shoot, Walter, shoot, in the devil's name!"
1 CONT Shoot he did. An instant afterwards the king fell without word or moan. Tyrrell's arrow had struck a tree, and, glancing, pierced the king's breast; or it may be that an arrow from a more [83] distant bow had struck him. When Tyrr
1 CONC ell reached his side he was dead.
1 CONT The French knight knew what would follow if he fell into the hands of the king's companions. He could not hope to make people credit his tale. Mounting his horse, he rode with all speed through the forest, not drawing rein till th
1 CONC e coast was reached. He had far outridden the news of the tragedy. Taking ship here, he crossed over in haste to Normandy, and thence made his way to France, not drawing a breath free from care till he felt the soil of his native 
1 CONC land beneath his feet. There he lived to a good age and died in peace, his life diversified by a crusading visit to the Holy Land.
1 CONT The end of the Red King resembled that of his father. The Conqueror had been deserted before he had fairly ceased breathing, his body left half clad on the bare boards of his chamber, while some of his attendants rifled the palace
1 CONC , others hastened to offer their services to his son. The same scenes followed the Red King's death. His body was left to the charcoal-burner's cart, clotted with blood, to be conveyed to Winchester, while his brother Henry rode p
1 CONC ost-haste thither to seize the royal treasure, and the train of courtiers rode as rapid a course, to look after their several interests.
1 CONT Reaching the royal palace, Henry imperiously demanded the keys of the king's treasure-chamber. Before he received them William de Breteuil entered, breathless with haste, and bade the keepers not to deliver them.
1 CONT [84] "Thou and I," he said to Henry, "ought loyally to keep the faith which we promised to thy brother, Duke Robert; he has received our oath of homage, and, absent or present, he has the right."
1 CONT But what was faith, what an oath, when a crown was the prize? A quarrel followed; Henry drew his sword; the people around supported him; soon he had the treasure and the royal regalia; Robert might have the right, he had the kingd
1 CONC om.
1 CONT There is tradition connected with the Red King's death. A stirrup hangs in Lyndhurst Hall, said to be that which he used on that fatal day. The charcoal-burner was named Purkess. There are Purkesses still in the village of Minstea
1 CONC d, near where William Rufus died. And the story runs that the earthly possessions of the Purkess family have ever since been a single horse and cart. A stone marks the spot where the king fell, on it is the inscription,-
1 CONT "Here stood the oak tree on which the arrow, shot by Walter Tyrrell at a stag, glanced and struck King William II., surnamed Rufus, on the breast; of which stroke he instantly died on the second of August, 1100.
1 CONT "That the spot where an event so memorable had happened might not hereafter be unknown, this stone was set up by John, Lord Delaware, who had seen the tree growing in this place, anno 1745"
1 CONT We may end by saying that England was revenged; the retribution for which her children had prayed had overtaken the race of the pirate king. [85] That broad domain of Saxon England, which William the Conqueror had wrested from its
1 CONC  owners to make himself a hunting-forest, was reddened with the blood of two of his sons and a grandson. The hand of Heaven had fallen on that cruel race. The New Forest was consecrated in the blood of one the Norman kings.- from 
1 CONC google
0 @N6000000005598924192@ NOTE William Rufus was the third son of William the Conqueror and Matilda .  His two elder brothers were Robert and Richard.  Robert was the eld est and the chief heir of William the Conqueror. Richard had died in a n accident while on
1 CONC  a hunting trip. William was renowned for his gener osity (ref: F. Barlow, 'William Rufus', 1986). He was anxious that hi s attire be expensive and extravagant and would become angry if purcha sed at to low of a price (ref: Chroni
1 CONC cle of the Kings of England, Will iam of Malmesbury, ed. J.A. Giles, 1904). William's reputation suffere d badly at the hands of monastic writers because of a long lasting qua rrel with Archbishop Anselm whom he regarded with susp
1 CONC ecion (ref: E.A . Freeman, 'The Reign of William Rufus', 2 Vols., Oxford, 1982).  Will iam often quarreled with the church which imputed him with irreligion , avarice and perversion.  He is styled as of great physical presence , a
1 CONC lthough a small man with red hair (whereby he received his nickname ), cynical and licentious.  Archbishop Anselm once likened him to a wi ld bull.  He wore his hair long, was easy going with his boo-companion s cracking jokes as 
1 CONC he conducted daily business and had a noticeable l ack of eloquence.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Rufus spent part of his childhood in the household of Archbish op Lanfranc.  Rufus and his brothers rejected the stifling piety of th eir parents and in his case Rufus came to be more than willing to expl oit the Church wh
1 CONC en it was to his advantage.  He was loyal to his fath er, much more than his elder brother Robert and when William the Conqu eror died, it was William Rufus who was at his father's side while Rob ert attended the court of the Conq
1 CONC ueror's enemy, Philippe the King of France.  William received his arms when he came of age in 1076.  He wa s at his fathers side at the battle of Gerberoy, where his father los t to his brother Curthose.  In about 1081, he became 
1 CONC one his fathers p rincipal military commanders.  He also independantly led a campaign in to Wales against the princes of Gwent.  He developed an open hostilit y to his older brother Curthose.  At the time of his fathers death, Wi 
1 CONC lliam was enroute to England.  In the Conqueror's will, the title of D uke of Normandie was bestoyed upon Robert and the crown of England wa s bestoyed upon William Rufus. On September 26, 1087, William Rufus wa s crowned at Westm
1 CONC inster as William II, by Lanfranc the Archbishop of Canterbury, who became quite influential upon the new King until his d eath in May 1089.  At some point, William granted the lands that his b rother Henry was to have that had be
1 CONC longed to their mother in Gloucest ershire and Buckinghamshire to Robert FitzHamon.
1 CONT
1 CONT William the Conqueror's land was divided in two. William Rufus had Eng land and his brother Robert had Normandie. This made life difficult fo r those Norman barons who had interests in both England and Normandie .  In 1087, he wel
1 CONC comed his uncle Eudes (Odo) back to England and rest ored his lands in Kent.   But by December of 1087, Eudes (Odo) and hi s brother Robert openly broke with Rufus.  In 1088 a revolt, lead by W illiam's uncles Odo of Bayeux and Ro
1 CONC bert of Mortain and supported by a t least six of Englands ten wealthiest Domesday families including Eus tace II of Boulogne, Robert of Mortain, Roger of Montgomery and Gilber t de Clare, attempted to remove William from the thro
1 CONC ne.  The main act ivity occured in Kent and Sussex.  William Rufus struck at his enemie s at Tonbridge and the castle quickly surrendered, much to the alarm o f Williams uncle Eudes (Odo).  Robert Curthose failed to come to Engla 
1 CONC nd to assist the rebels and William put the revolt down. Odo was banis hed to Normandie.  William had gained support fromthe Norman barons, a nd Robert's position became weak.  Robert took the opportunity of Pop e Urban II's call 
1 CONC for a Crusade to loan Normandie to William in retur n for money to fund the expedition.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1089, Ranulf Flambard, a leading advisor to William Rufus was apppo inted Justiciar and began to levy heavy taxes upon the Church.  In 109 0, William led an invasion of Normandie in an attempt to subdue his br other, Robert.  W
1 CONC illiam, still in England, allowed Robert - Count of E u, Walter II Giffard and Ralph of Mortimer to ravage the lands of nort hern Normandie throughout the rest of 1090.  William continued to tigh ten his grip on Normandie througho
1 CONC ut 1090.  In 1091, William put down an invasion of England led by Malcolm III of Scotland.  Also in 1091, William and his older brother Robert Curthose signed a treaty at Caen , that completely excluded their brother Henry, wherei
1 CONC n they jointly r ecognized that if either died, the other would take possession of the English crown.  In 1091, Rufus moved his court from England to the por t of Eu on the boarder of Normandie and Ponthieu.  The ducal center be c
1 CONC ame Fecamp which was surrendered to Rufus by William, son of Robert o f Mortain, Rufus' uncle.  Rufus received the Abbey of Fecamp, the coun ty of Eu, the city of Cherbourg and varioius other castles in Normandi e.  Having achieve
1 CONC d some small sense of harmony, together, Robert and Rufus led their armies west intent on taking the area of western Norma ndie from Henry.  When Rufus and his brother Curthose had Henry hostag e at Mont-St. Michel, it has been de
1 CONC scribed as a 'military fiesta, wit h daily mounted tournaments on the sands between besieged and those la ying siege.  Rufus often rode in the fray, while Curthose sent wine an d cheese across to the island when he heard the garri
1 CONC son was short on drink.
1 CONT
1 CONT In about 1093, Rufus' interest turned to Northumbria and the Welsh mar ches.  He began to foster the opinion that Malcolm of Scotland, Rhys a p Tewdwr (of Deheubarth) and Cadwgan ap Bleddyn (of Powys) had become to powerful in the
1 CONC ir areas.  By March of 1093, Rufus was at the manor of Alveston in Gloucestershire enrout to Bristol in preparaton of cros sing into Wales.  This effort was to be led by his ally Robert Fitz Ha mon who was put out of the combat by
1 CONC  a severe illness the kept him at Gloucester.  Rufus crossed into Wales in February of 1094.  Later tha t year Rufus was again in Normandie and recognized his yonger brother Henry as his Norman Lieutenant and by 29 December 1094, 
1 CONC Rufus had retu rned to England.  In 1095, William suppressed a revolt in Northumbria led by Earl Robert de Mowbray and was again faced with a threat from m any of the same barons and magnets that had stood agains him in 1088 .  In
1 CONC  about 1096, he offered Robert financial support for his crusade and received parts of Normandie in return.  He subsequently granted He nry ex integro 'the county of Contance and Bayeux', with the exceptio n of the cities of Bayeu
1 CONC x and Caan.  by 1097, Fufus found himself poss essed of his fathers reunited realm.  He stayed in Normandie until Apr il 1097 when he returned to England to lead a campaign in 1098 and he suppressed a Welsh rebellion against the N
1 CONC orman border lords.  Willia m kept his court at Westminister, which he also made great renditions to the Great Hall therein.  Rufus again returned to Normandie, but ha d less success in regaining any influence in Maine.  He was vi
1 CONC gorousl y opposed by many of the Manceaux barons and their Lord, Count Elias .  Rufus returned to England by Easter of 1099.  Count Elias' success in the field brought Rufus back to Normandie and he was able to contai n Elias, but
1 CONC  did not defeat him.  In July of 1099, he left Maine to hi s lieutenants and returned to England by September of 1099.
1 CONT
1 CONT The residence of Edward the Confessor on the banks of the Thames was r eplace by William Rufus and completed in about 1099.  What he had erec ted was referenced as the vast New Hall, now known as Westminster Hall .
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of his death, it is said that William was at the peak of h is power.  He had gone to the Royal Hunting preserve at New Forest.  T hose on the hunt included his brother Henry, the Beaumont brothers, He nry of Warwick, R
1 CONC obert of Meulan, Robert FitzHamon, William of Breteui l and a French magnet by the name of Walter Tirel.  On the 2nd of Augu st, 1100, William in preparation for a hunting trip had spent the even ign prior to the hunting trip at B
1 CONC rockenhurst.  The morning of 2 Augus t 1100 he gave two arrows to Walter Tyrell (a Knight of Poix) and the y entered the New Forest wood together with a few companions and Willi am was allegedly hit with an arrow fired by Walter T
1 CONC yrell (or Tirel), while hunting in the New Forest. (ref. 'The Ecclestical History of Ord eric Vitalis', ed. Marjorie Chiball, 5 Vols., Oxford, 1969 - 1981).  W hether this was an accident or by design is not known. William was bur
1 CONC  ied under the tower of Winchester Cathedral which collapsed the follow ing year.  The theory of his death has not been proven, wether by an a rrow from Walter Tyrell's bow, or if by order of his younger brother H enry.  William's
1 CONC  funeral was conducted by Geofffrey of Cambray.  Willi am died without issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT Additional information re. William Rufus include:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.) J.D. Mackie, A History of Scotland, 1978.
1 CONT 2.) Alan & Veronica Palmer, Pimlico Chronology of Britsh History.
