0 @N6000000000945059231@ NOTE Hertug av Aquitania og greve av Poitou fra 1058 til 1086. 
1 CONT Vilhelm VIII ?den Unge? (VI av Poitou) kalles også Guido Gotfred. Han var allerede
1 CONT hertug av Aquitania da han ved sin bror Wilhelm V's død i 1058 fikk grevskapet Poitou.
1 CONT Han erobret Saintonge i 1063 og kjempet i Spanien mot sarasenerne.
1 CONT Vilhelm var gift
1 CONT 1. gang ca. 1044 med en datter til Alderbert II av Perigord, forstøtt i 1058,
1 CONT 2. gang ca. 1058-59 med Matilde, forstøtt i 1068,
1 CONT 3. gang ca. 1068-69 med Hildegard (Alderade) av Burgund.
1 CONT Hans ekteskapelige ulykker og hans sammenstøt med kirken er berømte. Paven tvang
1 CONT ham til å skille seg fra sine to første hustruer på grunn av for nært slektskap i 1058 og 1068.
1 CONT Hans tredje hustru fant heller ikke kirkens bifall.
1 CONT Vilhelm døde i Chizé og ble begravet i Montierneuf i et vakkert gravsted som han hadde
1 CONT latt bygge for de cluny'ske munker.
0 @N6000000001783408594@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT William VIII, Duke of Aquitaine
1 CONT
1 CONT William VIII (1025 - 25 September 1086), born Guy-Geoffrey (Gui-Geoffroi), was duke of Gascony (1052-1086), and then duke of Aquitaine and count of Poitiers (as William VI) between 1058 and 1086, succeeding his brother William VII
1 CONC  (Pierre-Guillaume).
1 CONT
1 CONT Guy-Geoffroy was the youngest son of William V of Aquitaine by his third wife Agnes of Burgundy. He was the brother-in-law of Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor who had married his sister, Agnes de Poitou.
1 CONT
1 CONT He became Duke of Gascony in 1052 during his older brother William VII's rule. Gascony had come to Aquitanian rule through William V's marriage to Prisca (a.k.a Brisce) of Gascony, the sister of Duke Sans VI Guilhem of Gascony.
1 CONT
1 CONT William VIII was one of the leaders of the allied army called to help Ramiro I of Aragon in the Siege of Barbastro (1064). This expedition was the first campaign organized by the papacy, namely Pope Alexander II, against a Muslim 
1 CONC city, and the precursor of the later Crusades movement. Aragon and its allies conquered the city, killed its inhabitants and collected an important booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Aragon lost the city again in the following years. During William VIII's rule, the alliance with the southern kingdoms of modern Spain was a political priority as shown by the marriage of all his daughters to Iberian king
1 CONC s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married three times and had at least five children. After he divorced his second wife due to infertility, he remarried to a much younger woman who was also his cousin. This marriage produced a son, but William VIII had to visit
1 CONC  Rome in the early 1070s to persuade the pope to recognize his children from his third marriage as legitimate.
1 CONT
1 CONT First wife: Garsende of Périgord, daughter of Count Aldabert II of Périgord and his wife Alausie, herself the second daughter of duke Sans VI Guilhem of Gascony (divorced November 1058), no children. She became a nun at Saintes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Second wife: Matoeda (divorced May 1068)
1 CONT Agnes (1052-1078), married Alfonso VI of Castile
1 CONT
1 CONT Third wife: Hildegarde of Burgundy (daughter of duke Robert I of Burgundy)
1 CONT Agnes (d.1097), married Peter I of Aragon
1 CONT William IX of Aquitaine, his heir
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_VIII_of_Aquitaine>
0 @N6000000002187721067@ NOTE William VIII of Aquitaine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT William VIII of Aquitaine, (Guillaume VIII in French) (1025 – September 25, 1086), whose name was Guy-Geoffroy before becoming Duke of Aquitaine, was Duke of Gascony (1052-1086), and then Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Poitiers (a
1 CONC s William VI of Poitiers) between 1058 and 1086, succceeding his brother William VII (Pierre-Guillaume). Guy-Geoffroy was the youngest son of William V of Aquitaine by his third wife Agnes of Burgundy. He was the brother-in-law of
1 CONC  Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor who had married his sister, Agnes de Poitou.
1 CONT
1 CONT He became Duke of Gascony in 1052 during his older brother William VII's rule. Gascony had come to Aquitanian rule through William V's marriage to Prisca (a.k.a Brisce) of Gascony, the sister of Duke Sans VI Guilhem of Gascony.
1 CONT
1 CONT William VIII was one of the leaders of the allied army called to help Ramiro I of Aragon in the siege of Barbastro (1064). This expedition was the first campaign organized by the papacy, namely Pope Alexander II, against a Muslim 
1 CONC city, and the precursor of the later Crusades movement. Aragon and its allies conquered the city, killed its inhabitants and collected an important booty. However, Aragon lost the city again in the following years. During William 
1 CONC VIII's rule, the alliance with the southern kingdoms of modern Spain was a political priority as shown by the marriage of all his daughters to Iberian kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married three times and had at least five children. After he divorced his second wife due to infertility, he remarried to a much younger woman who was also his cousin. This marriage produced a son, but William VIII had to visit
1 CONC  Rome in the early 1070s to persuade the pope to recognize his children from his third marriage as legitimate.
1 CONT
1 CONT First wife: Garsende of Périgord, daughter of Count Aldabert II of Périgord and his wife Alausie, herself the second daughter of duke Sans VI Guilhem of Gascony (divorced November 1058), no children. She became a nun at Saintes. 
1 CONT Second wife: Matoeda (divorced May 1068) 
1 CONT Agnes (1052-1078), married Alfonso VI of Castile 
1 CONT Third wife: Hildegarde of Burgundy (daughter of duke Robert I of Burgundy) 
1 CONT Agnes (d.1097), married Peter I of Aragon 
1 CONT William IX of Aquitaine, his heir 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Owen, D.D.R. Eleanor of Aquitaine: Queen and Legend 
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Aquitaine family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William VII Duke of Aquitaine Succeeded by:
1 CONT William IX 
1 CONT Count of Poitiers 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bernat II Tumapaler Duke of Gascony 
0 @N6000000002187721203@ NOTE William VI of Aquitaine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia that anyone can edit.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT William VI of Aquitaine (1004-1038), nicknamed the Fat, was Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Poitiers as William IV of Poitou between 1030 and 1038. William was the eldest son of William V of Aquitaine by his first wife Almodis of G
1 CONC evaudun. William married Eustachie of Montreuil but had no known descendants.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Aquitaine family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William V Duke of Aquitaine Succeeded by:
1 CONT Otto 
1 CONT Count of Poitiers 
0 @N6000000002187721183@ NOTE William VII of Aquitaine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia that anyone can edit.
1 CONT
1 CONT William VII of Aquitaine, (Pierre-Guillaume in French) (1023 – 1058) was the Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Poitiers as William V of Poitiers between 1039 and 1058, following his half-brother Otto of Aquitaine. William was the thi
1 CONC rd son of William V of Aquitaine by his wife Agnes of Burgundy. He was brother-in-law of Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor who married his sister Agnes de Poitou. He married Ermesinde of Longwy (daughter of Adalbert of Longwy, Duke of
1 CONC  Lorraine) and had two daughter's, Clementia, who married Conrad I of Luxemburg and Stephanie.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Aquitaine family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Otto Duke of Aquitaine Succeeded by:
1 CONT William VIII 
1 CONT Count of Poitiers 
