0 @N6000000001353916141@ NOTE William IX (b. Oct. 22, 1071--d. Feb. 10, 1127, Poitiers, Fr.), medieval troubadour, count of Poitiers and duke of Aquitaine and of Gascony(1086-1127), son of William VIII and grandfather of the famous Eleanor of Aquitaine.
1 CONT William IX spent most of his life in warfare, including leading an unsuccessful Crusade to the Holy Land (1101-02) and battling the Moors near Cordova (1120-23). His fame rests chiefly, however, on his being the first poet in the 
1 CONC Provençal language whose works have come down to us. His chansons, or songs, are boisterous, amorous, humorous, usuallydelicate but sometimes coarsely obscene and tend, in the fashion of courtly love, to idolize one's lady love. [
1 CONC Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
1 CONT Assisted Philip I of France against William the Conqueror. He erectedthe Palace of Poictou, and, after confering large donations on the Church, died. The dates and name are unclear. Some label him as William VII. [Directory of Roy
1 CONC al Genealogical Data]
0 @N6000000001354466136@ NOTE William IX (b. Oct. 22, 1071--d. Feb. 10, 1127, Poitiers, Fr.), medieval troubadour, count of Poitiers and duke of Aquitaine and of Gascony(1086-1127), son of William VIII and grandfather of the famous Eleanor of Aquitaine.
1 CONT William IX spent most of his life in warfare, including leading an unsuccessful Crusade to the Holy Land (1101-02) and battling the Moors near Cordova (1120-23). His fame rests chiefly, however, on his being the first poet in the 
1 CONC Provençal language whose works have come down to us. His chansons, or songs, are boisterous, amorous, humorous, usuallydelicate but sometimes coarsely obscene and tend, in the fashion of courtly love, to idolize one's lady love. [
1 CONC Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
1 CONT Assisted Philip I of France against William the Conqueror. He erectedthe Palace of Poictou, and, after confering large donations on the Church, died. The dates and name are unclear. Some label him as William VII. [Directory of Roy
1 CONC al Genealogical Data]
0 @N6000000000945059216@ NOTE Vilhelm ?den Unge? var hertug av Aguitania og greve av Poitou 1086 - 1127. 
1 CONT Han var en modig kriger, elsket videnskap og poesi, skrev dikter og er en av de eldste
1 CONT kjente diktere på romansk språk. Han hadde en sterk vilje, men var usedelig.
1 CONT Vilhelm er den eldste kjente trubadur. I en provensalsk biografi heter det kort og godt:
1 CONT ?Han var en av de mest belevne menn som noensinne har levd og en stor kvinnebedårer - en
1 CONT ridder, dyktig i våpenbruk og stadig innviklet i kjærlighetseventyr. I diktning og sang var han en
1 CONT mester, og i mange år gjennomstreifet han verden for å vinne vakre damers kjærlighet.? Og at
1 CONT han selv ikke holdt sine seire i kampen om de skjønne hemmelig, gir hans dikt tydelige
1 CONT vitnesbyrd om.
1 CONT Han gjorde krav på Toulouse hos Raimuind IV, erobret og oppga Toulouse to ganger,
1 CONT og førte ellers en lang rekke kriger. Han bygde Partheney.
1 CONT Vilhelm var tilstede ved konsiliet i Clermont i 1095, men først i 1100 dro han på korstog
1 CONT med mer en 60.000 mann. De ble utsatt for et blodig angrep i Lilleasien, men nådde likevel
1 CONT Jerusalem og leverte de ?vantro? slaget ved Rama 26.05.1102.
1 CONT Han hjalp Alfons av Aragon mot Maurerne, og Ludvig den Store mot tyrkerne.
1 CONT Han var første gang gift ca. 1089 med Irmgard av Anjou, men ble skilt ca. 1092.
0 @N6000000001355732136@ NOTE William IX (b. Oct. 22, 1071--d. Feb. 10, 1127, Poitiers, Fr.), medieval troubadour, count of Poitiers and duke of Aquitaine and of Gascony(1086-1127), son of William VIII and grandfather of the famous Eleanor of Aquitaine.
1 CONT William IX spent most of his life in warfare, including leading an unsuccessful Crusade to the Holy Land (1101-02) and battling the Moors near Cordova (1120-23). His fame rests chiefly, however, on his being the first poet in the 
1 CONC Provençal language whose works have come down to us. His chansons, or songs, are boisterous, amorous, humorous, usuallydelicate but sometimes coarsely obscene and tend, in the fashion of courtly love, to idolize one's lady love. [
1 CONC Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
1 CONT Assisted Philip I of France against William the Conqueror. He erectedthe Palace of Poictou, and, after confering large donations on the Church, died. The dates and name are unclear. Some label him as William VII. [Directory of Roy
1 CONC al Genealogical Data]
0 @N6000000002187590312@ NOTE William IX of Aquitaine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT William IX of Aquitaine - Image from Bibliothèque Nationale, MS cod. fr. 12473, 13th centuryWilliam IX of Aquitaine (October 22, 1071 – February 10, 1126, also Guillaume or Guilhem d'Aquitaine), nicknamed the Troubador was Duke of
1 CONC  Aquitaine and Gascony and Count of Poitiers as William VII of Poitiers between 1086 and 1126. He was also one of the leaders of the crusade of 1101 and one of the first vernacular poets and troubadours.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life and Family
1 CONT William was the son of William VIII of Aquitaine by his third wife Hildegarde of Burgundy. His birth was an event of great celebration, but at first he was considered illegitimate by religious authorities because of his father's e
1 CONC arlier divorces and his parents consanguinity. This obliged his father to make a pilgrimage to Rome soon after his birth, where he sought and received papal approval of his marriage and children.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1094 he married Philippa of Toulouse, the daughter and heiress of William IV of Toulouse. By Philippa, William had two sons and five daughters, including:
1 CONT
1 CONT William X of Aquitaine, his heir. 
1 CONT Agnes of Aquitaine, who married (1) Aimery V of Thouars; (2) to King Ramiro II of Aragon 
1 CONT Raymond of Poitiers, ruler of the principality of Antioch, a crusader state 
1 CONT He was excommunicated twice, the first time in 1114 for some unknown offense. His response to this was to demand absolution from the Bishop of Poitiers at swordpoint. He was excommunicated the second time for carrying off Dangereu
1 CONC se, the wife of his vassal Aimery I de Rochefoucauld, Viscount of Châtellerault. He installed her in the Maubergeonne tower of his castle, and, as related by William of Malmesbury, even painted a picture of her on his shield.
1 CONT
1 CONT This greatly offended both his wife and his son, William. According to Orderic Vitalis, Philippa protested her treatment in October 1119 at the Council of Reims, claiming to have been abandoned by the duke in favor of Dangereuse. 
1 CONC She later retired to the convent of Fontevrault. Relations were only patched up with his son when the younger William married Ænor of Châtellerault, Dangereuse's daughter by her husband.
1 CONT
1 CONT His 13th century Provençal biographer remembers him:
1 CONT
1 CONT "[William IX] was one of the most courtly men in the world and one of the greatest deceivers of women. He was a fine knight at arms, liberal in his attentions to ladies, and a fine composer and singer of songs." 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Military life
1 CONT William invited Pope Urban II to spend Christmas 1095 at his court. The pope urged him to take the cross and leave for the Holy Land, but William was more interested in exploiting the absence of Raymond IV of Toulouse, his wife's 
1 CONC uncle, to press a claim to Toulouse. He and Philippa did capture Toulouse in 1098, an act for which they were threatened with excommunication. Partly out of a desire to regain favor with the religious authorities and partly out of
1 CONC  a wish to see the world, William joined the First Crusade in 1099.
1 CONT
1 CONT He arrived in the Holy land in 1101 and stayed there until the following year. His record as a general is not very impressive. William fought mostly skirmishes in Anatolia and was frequently defeated. His recklessness had his army
1 CONC  ambushed on several occasions, with great losses to his own side. In September 1101 his entire army was destroyed by the Turks at Heraclea; William himself barely escaped with a few survivors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later on in his life, William joined forces with the kingdoms of Castile (an old ally) and Léon. Between 1120 and 1123, Aquitanian troops fought side by side with queen Urraca of Castile, in an effort to conquer the Moors of Cordo
1 CONC ba and complete the Reconquista. During his sojourn in Spain, William was given a rock crystal vase by a Muslim ally that he later beqeathed to his granddaughter Eleanor. The vase probably originated in Sassanid Persia in the 7th 
1 CONC century.
1 CONT
1 CONT William IX also provided troops to Philip I of France in his war against William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Poetry
1 CONT William's greatest legacy to history was not as a warrior but as a poet. He was he was the first known troubadour, or lyric poet employing the Romance vernacular called Provencal, or Occitan. Eleven of his songs have survived into
1 CONC  the 21st century (Merwin, 2002). His artistic name was lo cons de Peitieus, and he was one of the most important troubadours of the Middle Age's Provençal literature. The topics varied, treating sex, love, women, his own sexual p
1 CONC rowess, and feudal politics. His brashness, wit, and temper caused scandal and won admiration at the same time. He stands as the first surviving troubadour, and one of the first Romance vernacular poets of theMiddle Ages, a cultur
1 CONC al phenomenon that would culminate in Dante, Boccaccio, and Villon.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was a man that loved scandal and no doubt enjoyed shocking his audiences. Upon returning from crusade, he abandoned his wife in favour of a married woman, known as Dangereuse (Dangerosa in Occitan) from his poems, and risk
1 CONC ed excommunication for the deed. He also composed a song about founding a convent in his lands, where the nuns would be picked from among the most beautiful women in the region, or from the best whores, depending on the translatio
1 CONC n. While this confirms William's lusty persona, it also makes a joke about the penitentiary convents for prostituted founded by the charismatic preacher Robert of Arbrissel. (Bond, xlix) In fact, William granted large donations to
1 CONC  the church, perhaps to regain the pope's favour. He also constructed the palace of Poitou, later added to by his granddaughter Eleanor of Aquitaine. It survives to this day in the city of Poitiers.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of William's poems is a musing on mortality; it begins: Since now I have a mind to sing/I'll make a song of that which saddens me, and goes on to say: For I have known delight and dalliance/Both far and near, yea and in my own
1 CONC  dwelling/But this day, joy and dalliance, farewell. He died on February 10th, 1126, age 55.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Bond, Gerald A., ed., transl. intro. The Poetry of William VII, Count of Poitier, IX Duke of Aquitaine, (Garland Publishing Co.:New York) 1982 
1 CONT Duisit, Brice. Las Cansos del Coms de Peitieus (CD), Alpha 505, 2003 
1 CONT Harvey, Ruth E. The wives of the 'first troubadour', Duke William IX of Aquitaine (Journal of Medieval History), 1993 
1 CONT Meade, Marion. Eleanor of Aquitaine, 1991 
1 CONT Merwin, W.S. Sir Gawain and the Green Knight, 2002. pp xv-xvi. New York: Alfred A. Knopf. ISBN 0-375-41476-2. 
1 CONT Owen, D.D.R. Eleanor of Aquitaine: Queen and Legend 
1 CONT Parsons, John Carmi. Eleanor of Aquitaine: Lord and Lady, 2002 
1 CONT Verdon, J. La chronique de Saint Maixent, 1979. 
1 CONT Waddell, Helen. The Wandering Scholars: the Life and Art of the Lyric Poets of the Latin Middle Ages, 1955 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Dukes of Aquitaine family tree 
1 CONT Provençal literature 
1 CONT Smythe, Barbara. Trobador Poets: Selections from the Poems of Eight Trobadors, including William IX, Duke of Aquitaine. 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William VIII Duke of Aquitaine Succeeded by:
1 CONT William X 
1 CONT Count of Poitiers 
